"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Technical controls': {
        'body': 'A machine can keep enforcing a rule after the last person leaves the clinic. A safeguard of that kind is a technical control. The rule sits in software or in hardware. It does not need a guard at the door, a poster on the wall, or a manager on the phone. A firewall, a full-disk cipher, a lockout counter, and an inline filter that drops a packet all belong here. A memo has no way to cut a live session at 23:41. The device can still cut that session.\n\nThe device holds a condition and compares a packet, a sign-in, or a file with that condition. On a match it allows the request, refuses it, or writes a line an analyst can read later. An inline filter that discards the packet is performing the refusal. A sensor that only copies traffic is only making a record, even when the hardware looks similar. Full-disk encryption with AES-256 is the same category, because the volume stays unreadable when the key is absent. A directory setting that locks an account after five bad passwords is the same idea applied at sign-in. The comparison is what makes the control real, rather than a wish written on a slide.\n\nLast Tuesday the clinic edge supplied the proof. The building was empty. The firewall denied TCP 22 and TCP 3389 from the internet except when the source was jump host 10.8.0.4. At 23:41 the log showed a deny for TCP 22 from 203.0.113.50 to payroll host 10.1.4.20, and the hit counter on that rule stood at fourteen. The console showed no interactive user. Analyst Priya copied the rule id, the deny line, and the clock time into ticket 4418 before she changed a single setting.\n\nA defender treats that log as evidence the control fired. The deployed mode matters, because an inline drop means the packet died and a copy-only tap means the packet passed. After every edit, someone should reread the rule, because one broad allow placed above a closing deny can hide the careful line. The same kind of filter on a laptop keeps working after the laptop leaves the clinic network. Administration paths belong off the guest wireless segment. A license screen is not proof that the rule is the one actually running.\n\nStudents mix this label with a signed decision, a practiced habit, and a tangible barrier. The privacy page that forbids copying customer lists into personal mail is a managerial control, even when that page ordered someone to build the firewall rule. The Friday restore that the desk actually performs is an operational control, even when a policy put the drill on the calendar. The closet door that wants a badge is a physical control. A packet drop is preventive by timing and technical by the place it lives. What separates the categories is the empty building. If the machine enforced the rule with nobody watching, the category is technical.\n',
        'r0': 'The clinic firewall is a technical control because the device refuses traffic with nobody at the keyboard. It denies TCP 22 and TCP 3389 from the internet toward internal hosts unless the source is jump host 10.8.0.4. One night the log recorded source 203.0.113.50, destination 10.1.4.20, protocol TCP, time 23:41, and a hit count of fourteen. AES-256 volume encryption belongs in the same category, since missing the key leaves the disk unreadable. An account lockout on the fifth bad password belongs here too. Inline prevention drops a matching packet, while a tap only observes it. The signed order that demanded the rule is a different label, and so is the Friday drill that staff initial on a sheet. Pick this label when the stem shows the box itself doing the enforcement.\n',
        'r1': 'The machine enforces a rule with nobody watching. It still works after the building is empty. A firewall can refuse a connection on its own. A signed page cannot cut a live session. A Friday drill is a habit people practice. A closet lock is a barrier you can touch. The machine compares a request with a stored rule. It can allow the request or refuse it. It can also write a line for later. Copying traffic is weaker than dropping a packet. Read that line before you change the rule. The empty building is the clue to pick.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse roll-up door whose motor bolt stays thrown after the crew goes home for supper. A driver who pounds on the metal still meets a closed door. A paper note taped beside the frame can ask drivers to wait until morning, and the note does not move the bolt. A morning practice in which the crew opens and closes the door on a timer is useful, and that practice is not the motor. The bolt stays thrown whether the driver is late or the office upstairs is dark. In the clinic, the firewall rule that refuses administration traffic after closing is that motor. It keeps refusing while the building is empty and no person is at the console.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the clinic edge log for last night and find the deny lines. Copy the source, the destination, the time, and the hit count into ticket 4418. Record the rule id beside those lines before you offer an opinion. Check whether the running rule matches the change that was approved. Note that the console showed no interactive user. Leave the rule in place until the ticket holds the evidence. Do not treat the signed privacy page as this same evidence. Do not treat the Friday drill sheet as this same evidence.\n',
        'exam': 'A device that refuses traffic while the office is empty is a technical control even when a policy demanded the rule. The signed page alone is a managerial control. The restore rehearsal is an operational control. A closet lock is a physical control. Dropping a packet is preventive in timing, and the firewall remains technical in category. A listen-only copy is not a refusal. A warning sign is not this category. Pick the technical label when the machine did the enforcing.\n',
    },
    'Managerial controls': {
        'body': 'A decision that nobody wrote down will drift the moment the meeting ends. The safeguard is the signed page, whether that page is a policy, a standard, or a risk acceptance that names an owner. A signed decision of that sort is a managerial control. It states what the organization chose. It does not drop a packet, and it does not walk a restore drill. Auditors ask for the signature and the owner because a hallway comment cannot be opened later and shown to anyone.\n\nAdoption is the mechanism. A leader approves the text, names the person who will answer for it, and stores the page where the next review can find it. A standard is tighter than a policy because the standard states the required setting rather than only the aim. A procedure lists the steps, while this control is the choice to adopt those steps and to name who owns them. A risk acceptance stays in this category when a director writes that a known gap may stand until a dated review. An unsigned slide deck is not in the category yet, however sure the presenter sounded in the room.\n\nHelen Nguyen, the clinic privacy lead, signed the rule that customer lists may not be copied to personal mail. The page names her as owner, sets a review in March, and states a consequence for a violation. Ticket 4520 opened because staff could point only at a meeting. After the signature sat in the register, the ticket could cite a decision instead of a memory. The mail gateway that can stop a spreadsheet from leaving is a different object. The annual class that people attend and initial is a different object as well.\n\nA defender opens the register before arguing about intent. The ticket should carry the owner and the review date, and the date should still be in the future. When the owner leaves, the page fails quietly unless someone inherits the duty. Every exception needs a reason and an end date, not an open-ended shrug. The intrusion console is the wrong exhibit for this label, and so is the badge drill sheet. Those exhibits prove other kinds of work. A guideline that says should is weaker than a standard that says must, and both can still be this label when an owner has signed.\n\nThe exam trap is to treat every mention of a rule as this label. A signature, an owner, or a dated acceptance points here. A gateway deny means the device did the work, which is a technical control even when this page ordered the deny to be built. People practicing a restore and writing the minutes are performing an operational control. Ink is what separates them. A machine can carry out the sentence, and a crew can rehearse it, but neither one is the signed choice.\n',
        'r0': 'A managerial control is the governance record, not the packet the firewall drops. Helen Nguyen signed the customer-list rule, named herself as owner, and set a March review. A risk acceptance that expires on 30 June is the same kind of record when a director signs the gap and the end date. A password standard that states the required length is tighter than a policy that only states the aim. The mail gateway that blocks a spreadsheet is a device action. The class roster is a practiced habit. An unsigned meeting note is not this label yet. Choose it when the stem shows a signature, an owner, or a dated exception. The page fails when the review month passes and nobody inherits the duty.\n',
        'r1': 'A boss writes the decision and signs it. The page names an owner and a review date. A policy or a standard can be that page. A dated risk acceptance can be that page too. A hallway talk is not yet a decision. An unsigned slide is not yet a decision. The mail filter that blocks a file is separate. The class people attend is a separate habit. Ask who signed and when the page expires. A standard states a required setting. A policy states the aim more loosely. Record the owner on the ticket.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen where the manager writes who may enter the food room and signs the bottom of the card. The card names a person who will answer questions next month. A lock on the food room is a different object, and a weekly walk-through is a different object too. Staff who only remember the talk in the hallway do not have a card. When the manager leaves, the old card stays wrong until a new signature replaces it. In the clinic, Helen Nguyen's signed rule about customer lists is that card. The gateway that can stop a message is not the signature on the card.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the policy register and find the customer-list rule. Confirm Helen Nguyen is the owner and the March review is still ahead. Write that owner and date on ticket 4520. Match the page wording to the copying the requester described. Leave the mail gateway rule in a separate note. Leave the class attendance sheet in a separate note. Do not change the firewall while you are only identifying the decision. Stop when the ticket cites the signature and the review date.\n',
        'exam': 'A signed policy or a dated risk acceptance is a managerial control. The gateway that carries out the text is not. The training drill is not. A meeting comment without a signature is not yet this label. A standard that says must is still this label when an owner signed it. Framework titles do not replace the adopted page. If the stem shows an owner and a signature, choose the written decision. If the stem shows a packet drop, choose the device instead.\n',
    },
    'Operational controls': {
        'body': 'A polished page still fails if nobody has practiced the motion before a bad Friday. The safeguard is the habit people actually perform. Training with a roster, a backup drill, a visitor log, or a tabletop that leaves notes all qualify. That practiced habit is what an operational control means in this lesson. The poster on the wall is not the control. The repetition, with a result written where the next shift can see it, is the control.\n\nThe work is ordinary and easy to skip. Someone puts the habit on a calendar, a person does it, and the result is written down. The note should name the person, the date, and the outcome. A drill sheet with blank lines cannot be shown to an auditor. A software license does not prove the habit happened. If the Friday slot is missed for a month and nobody writes the miss, the control has decayed even while the product stayed installed. Reading one slide with no roster is a weak substitute and usually fails this test.\n\nEvery Friday the service desk restores one payroll file into a sandbox and writes how long the restore took. Last Friday Priya Ortiz finished in eighteen minutes and initialed the sheet. Ticket 4602 asked whether the clinic really practices restores. The sheet answered, and the backup brochure did not. A visitor log at the front desk, with time in and the host name, is the same kind of habit. A quarterly test of the call tree, where the person on duty answers and the time is written, belongs with it. A phishing exercise that produces a completion roster belongs here as well.\n\nA defender pulls the sheet before trusting the story. One sandbox restore, timed again, gives a fresh minute count for the ticket. A skipped date belongs on the ticket as a failed check, together with the Friday chosen to make it up. The signed policy that ordered the slot stays in a separate note. The backup product screen stays in a separate note. The product can be a technical control while the rehearsal remains this one. The question decides which fact it wants. Evidence is initials, a date, and a result, not a purchase order for the tool.\n\nPeople confuse the habit with the order that required it and with the machine that stores the copy. The policy that named an owner for Friday is a managerial control. The backup software that runs while the desk is empty is a technical control. A locked door beside the visitor log is a physical control. An alert that fired by itself is not the drill. Initials, a date, and a result are what separate this label from its neighbors. Without a person who practiced, the control is not there yet.\n',
        'r0': 'An operational control is a human practice with a recorded outcome. Last Friday Priya Ortiz restored one payroll file into a sandbox in eighteen minutes and initialed the sheet. Ticket 4602 uses that sheet, not the backup brochure, as evidence. A visitor log with time in and a host name is the same class of habit. A phishing exercise with a completion roster qualifies, and one unread slide does not. The backup platform under the drill is a device control. The policy that ordered Friday practice is a signed decision. A skipped month is a failed check even if the license stayed current. Evidence is the person, the date, and the result.\n',
        'r1': 'People practice a habit and write the result. A restore drill with minutes and initials fits. A visitor log at the desk fits too. A poster nobody follows is not the habit. A signed order to practice is a different thing. Backup software that runs alone is different too. The sheet should name a person and a date. A skipped month means the habit decayed. A tool license does not prove practice. Repeat the drill and time it yourself. Write the minutes on the ticket. Look for initials before you trust the story.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse night crew that walks the same spill route every Friday until each turn feels dull. The value is the walking, the count of people, and the note of how many minutes the route took. A framed order on the wall is not the walk. A door that shuts by itself is not the walk. The first walk is clumsy, and later walks count only if the crew still does them when no inspector is watching. A skipped Friday leaves a blank line, and that blank is the failure. In the clinic, Priya's timed file restore is that walk. A shiny backup product that nobody has used is not the practice.\n",
        'r4': 'Pull the Friday sheet and confirm it lists minutes and initials. Repeat one sandbox restore and time the steps yourself. Write that minute count on ticket 4602. Check that a person practiced, rather than that a poster exists. Keep the policy that ordered the Friday slot in a separate note. Keep the backup product screen in a separate note. Do not change the backup settings while you are only proving the habit. Stop when the ticket shows a person, a date, and a result.\n',
        'exam': 'People doing a repeated habit are an operational control when the result is written down. Training, restore drills, and visitor logs are the usual examples. The policy that orders the drill is not this label. The backup software is not this label. A poster nobody follows is not this label. A license certificate does not prove the habit. A skipped month is a failed check. Pick this label only when you can point at practice.\n',
    },
    'Physical controls': {
        'body': 'Some safeguards can be tapped with a knuckle or met as a person in a doorway. A lock, a badge reader, a camera, a guard, a safe, or a cable lock is that kind of safeguard. The tangible barrier is a physical control. Knowing a password does not move a door that wants a badge. The wireless network and the server closet are different problems, and a contractor who knows one has not solved the other.\n\nMatter or a person does the refusing. The unwanted act has to defeat something in the room. A reader, a strike, and a door can keep a stranger out. A camera can watch the hall, and a guard can walk over when the picture shows a prop holding the frame. A safe can hold offline backup tapes. A cable lock can slow a quick grab in an exam room. Fencing, and short posts that stop a car, sit in this broad category when the question stays general. When the stem names the two-door room or the posts themselves, use the narrower name and keep this label for the general tangible class.\n\nTicket 4701 described a contractor who had heard a rumor about the closet and still could not enter. The reader wanted a live badge. The contractor already knew the wireless password, and that knowledge changed nothing. The evidence is the badge deny on the door controller, plus a photo of the door. The write-up should also say whether the closet stays locked or unlocks when power drops. A door that opens itself during a power cut is a different risk from a door that stays shut. A spare key left on the lobby counter defeats the whole arrangement.\n\nA defender looks for the propped door, the spare key, and the reader left in a mode that unlocks when power fails. A camera is still this category, and a review of the recording the next morning is often detection after the fact. Encryption on a tape does not replace the safe, and the safe does not replace encryption. A guard who never asks for a name is furniture. The visitor procedure at the desk is a habit, not the lock. Port locks on a switch are the same tangible idea applied to a cable.\n\nStudents treat every door story as a preventive control, or every camera as only a warning. Timing and effect are other axes. A badge reader can be tangible and can also stop the act before it succeeds. A visible dome can discourage a passerby and still be a real camera in the hall. What you could touch, or the person you could meet, is what separates this label. A firewall deny is not a tangible barrier. A password is not a tangible barrier. When the stem stresses the barrier in the building, the tangible category is the answer.\n',
        'r0': 'A physical control is a tangible measure such as a lock, a badge reader, a camera, a guard, a safe, or a cable lock. On ticket 4701 the closet reader refused a contractor who already knew the wireless password. The badge deny on the door controller is the evidence, together with a photo of the door. Preventive and deterrent describe timing or effect, while this label names the thing in the room. A camera is this category, often detective when reviewed later and deterrent when obvious. Doors that stay locked when power drops differ from doors that unlock. Encryption is not this label, though a safe holding tapes is. Choose it when the stem shows a barrier in the building rather than a packet filter.\n',
        'r1': 'This barrier is something you can touch. A lock, a badge reader, or a guard fits. A camera in the hall fits too. A wireless password does not open a closet. A firewall rule is not a door. A signed page is not a door either. A propped door defeats the barrier. A spare key on the counter defeats it too. Note whether the door stays locked without power. A safe around tapes is this kind of barrier. Encryption on the tapes is a different layer. Photograph the door before you call it proven.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic cupboard whose hasp is shut over the spare keys, so the keys never sit on the lobby counter. A person who has memorized every story told at the desk still meets the hasp. A camera looking at the cupboard can watch, and it does not replace the hasp. A paper list of names is not the cupboard until a barrier or a person uses that list at the door. The hasp is matter in the way. A hurried visitor still has to meet that metal. In the clinic, the server closet badge reader is that hasp. Knowing the wireless password does not open the closet.\n',
        'r4': 'Photograph the closet door and attach the photo to ticket 4701. Export the badge deny that shows the reader refused the contractor. Write whether the door stays locked or unlocks when power drops. Keep the wireless password the contractor knew in a separate note. Keep a preventive or deterrent label off the ticket unless the stem asks about timing. Check for a propped door or a key left on the counter. Do not change the reader mode yet. Stop when the ticket names the barrier that stood in the way.\n',
        'exam': 'A lock, a reader, a camera, or a guard is a physical control. A password or a firewall is not. Preventive means the control stops a try, which can overlap a lock but is a different axis. Deterrent means the control discourages, which can overlap a visible camera that is still tangible. If the stem stresses the barrier in the building, choose the tangible category. A door that unlocks when power drops is a failure mode of this barrier. A propped door defeats it until the closer and the habit are fixed.\n',
    },
    'Preventive controls': {
        'body': "Stopping an unwanted act before that act succeeds is a claim about time. The safeguard refuses the step. A refusal with that timing is a preventive control. The payroll file is still present because something said no, not because someone wrote a careful note after the loss. A log of a completed theft is a different kind of comfort, and it arrives too late to wear this name.\n\nThe refusal can sit in more than one place. A door strike that never lets go, a firewall that discards a packet, a volume that stays unreadable without its key, and an account that has already been disabled are all refusals. An allow list that blocks an unknown program before the program can run is a refusal. A payment flow that will not let one clerk both create and approve the same transfer is a refusal. A listen-only sensor on a copy of the traffic does not qualify, because the packet still arrives. Patching a flaw before anyone exploits it has this timing. The same patch after a breach is cleanup and wears a different name.\n\nTicket 4814 followed a stranger at the clinic rack door. The badge came back unknown, the strike stayed shut, and the time sits on the door controller. No hand reached a server. That reader is also a tangible barrier, so a second question may want the physical category instead of the timing. The evidence for this label is the order of events. The refusal came before entry. A video note written the next morning proves that someone looked later. It does not prove that a stop held the door.\n\nA defender attaches the badge deny time to the ticket and checks that the strike did not release. A propped door and a broad allow that replaced a refusal are the failures to hunt. A login banner is not this label, because a person who ignores the text can still open the session. Removing a contractor account on the last afternoon has this timing, aimed at a later login that should never exist. Quarantine after an infection has already landed is repair, and the clocks in the write-up should stay separate.\n\nNeighbors are easy to trade by mistake. A sign, a painted warning, or a dome with no barrier tries to change a mind, and that effort is a deterrent control. An alert about a door that already stood open speaks after the fact, and that signal is a detective control. Restoring the prior night's clean image repairs damage, and that restore is a corrective control. A jump host placed around an old application, because a second factor would not fit, is a compensating control chosen before any incident. A mandatory sentence in a procedure is a directive control. The refusal that landed before success is what separates this label from those neighbors.\n",
        'r0': 'A preventive control refuses the act before the act succeeds. On ticket 4814 an unknown badge left the rack-door strike shut, and the door controller holds that time. Firewall denies, disabled accounts, and inline packet drops are the same timing. A sign without a refusal is only a deterrent. An alert about an event that already happened is detective. A listen-only sensor does not qualify, because the packet still arrives. Patching before exploitation has this timing, and patching after compromise is repair. Encryption that withholds readable text is this timing for secrecy even if the laptop is stolen. Choose this label when harm did not finish because something refused it.\n',
        'r1': 'This control stops the act before it succeeds. A lock that stays shut is a real refusal. A machine that drops a packet is a refusal. A warning sign does not stop a determined person. An alarm after the fact speaks too late. A video review the next day is too late. Cleaning up after damage is a later job. A stand-in chosen earlier is a different idea. Point at the refusal and the time. A login banner is only a warning. Patching before any attack is this timing. Patching after a break-in is cleanup.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a bicycle locked to a clinic rack before anyone can ride it away. The lock does its job in the moment a hand tries the wheel, not after the rack is empty. A barking dog next door may scare some people, and the dog is not the lock. A photo of the empty rack the next morning is a record, and it is not the lock. If the lock was left open, the stop was never there, however bright the porch was. Buying a new bicycle the next week is past the moment the lock could have helped. In the clinic, the badge reader that never released the strike is that lock. The stop happened before a hand reached the racks.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the door-controller event for ticket 4814. Confirm the badge was unknown and the strike stayed closed. Attach the time that shows the refusal came before entry. Keep the hallway sign that only warns in a separate note. Keep the next-morning video review in a separate note. Do not call the alert a stop. Do not change the strike setting while you are only proving the order of events. Stop when the badge deny and its time are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A refusal before success is a preventive control. A sign or a visible camera without a barrier is not. An alert after the door opened is not. Inline drops qualify, and listen-only modes do not. A patch before any exploit qualifies, and a patch after a breach does not. Quarantine after an infection has landed is repair. The same lock can still be a physical control on another question. Pick this label when the stem asks about timing.\n',
    },
    'Deterrent controls': {
        'body': 'Some safeguards work by changing a mind before a hand moves. The bad act is made to look costly, so many people choose not to try. A visible warning with that purpose is a deterrent control. The act does not have to become impossible. A person who does not care about being seen can still ignore the warning. That limit is why a warning is paired with a real refusal rather than trusted alone.\n\nBeing noticed is the mechanism. A sign, a lit entrance, a camera dome people can see, a guard standing in view, or a warning banner on a sign-in screen raises the apparent cost. A hidden camera fails this job, because a person who does not know about the camera will not change the choice in the moment. A fake dome can still discourage someone who believes the dome is real, and it stores nothing, so it is a poor record. Light that removes a dark pocket makes sneaking feel exposed. A fence people can see adds delay and also this effect. When the stem is about the fence itself, the fence topic is the better answer.\n\nTicket 4906 covered a clinic side door at 01:10. A sign, a dome, and a lamp over the frame led a passerby to keep walking. The door remained an ordinary latch. The useful evidence is that the approach stopped outside, together with photos of the sign and of the lamp while the lamp was lit. If the passerby had forced the latch and the door had opened, the warning would have failed. A preventive layer would then be the missing piece. The ticket should not be marked as a stop merely because the person walked away, unless a barrier actually held.\n\nA login banner that tells people only authorized use may continue is the classic device form of this control. The banner does not stop a password that works. A visible guard is this control when presence changes behavior, and the same guard may also block a path, which is a second fact to record. A sign that threatens a report does nothing once a person decides the sign is a bluff. A defender photographs the visible pieces, confirms the lamp was on, and keeps any real refusal in a separate note. A packet drop is not a warning. A drop is a refusal.\n\nThe trap is to hear that the person did not enter and to assume a preventive control. Walking away can mean the person was discouraged, which is this label. Walking away can also mean a lock held, which is a different label. A hidden sensor is mainly a later record, closer to a detective control. Wording on a banner can support discipline afterward, and the control type for the banner itself is still the warning. Perception is what separates them. A control that had to be noticed in order to work is a deterrent. A control that would have worked on someone who never looked up is something else.\n',
        'r0': 'A deterrent control discourages an attempt by making the act look costly or visible. On ticket 4906 a sign, a dome, and a lamp over a side door led a passerby to keep walking at 01:10, while the latch itself was still ordinary. A login banner is the classic device form, and it does not block a password that works. Hidden cameras are weak here because the person may not know they exist. A badge reader that stays locked is a refusal, not this label. A fake dome can discourage and cannot record. Lighting that removes a dark pocket supports this effect at doors. Choose this label when the stem stresses warning or visibility rather than a packet drop.\n',
        'r1': 'This control makes a bad act look costly. People who care about being seen may walk away. A sign can do that job. A bright light over a door can do it. A camera people notice can do it too. A guard who is easy to see can do it. The act can still be possible. A hidden camera does not change the choice. A lock that truly holds is a stronger stop. A fake camera may scare and records nothing. A packet drop is a refusal, not a warning. Use a warning together with a real stop.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic parking lot where a bright lamp and a painted fine make a driver decide the alley is a bad idea. The lamp and the paint work only while the driver can see them and still cares. A lock on the shed behind the lot is a different object. A camera buried under the eaves that nobody notices is a different object too. If the lamp is dark, the warning in this picture goes quiet even if the paint remains. Someone who ignores both the lamp and the paint has walked past what a warning can do. In the clinic, the sign, the dome, and the lamp at the side door are that lot. The control worked because the passerby noticed it and kept walking.\n',
        'r4': 'Photograph the sign, the dome, and the lamp for ticket 4906. Confirm the lamp was lit at 01:10 so the warning was visible. Ask whether any barrier actually held, and keep that fact in a separate note. Copy the login banner text if the warning is on a sign-in screen instead. Do not label a packet drop as a warning. Do not label a next-day log review as a warning. Leave the latch as it is until the ticket records visibility. Stop when the evidence is that the person noticed the warning.\n',
        'exam': 'A visible warning is a deterrent control. A hard refusal is not. A hidden sensor is a poor answer for this label. Login banners are this label on most stems, because they do not stop a working password. A fake camera can discourage and records nothing. Do not select this label for a firewall deny. Pairing a sign with a lock does not turn the lock into a warning. Perception is the fact that separates a warning from a stop.\n',
    },
    'Detective controls': {
        'body': "Discovery in this category begins after an action has already landed. The control raises a signal a person can investigate. It does not freeze the session, and it does not rebuild the host. What you can hold onto is the notice itself: a clock time, an account, and a fact that was easy to miss from the hallway. Without that notice, quiet misuse can continue through several shifts because nobody has a reason to open the record.\n\nA watcher and a condition do the work. Logs, door sensors, or recordings arrive where a rule can compare them, and a match opens an alert with a time attached. A sensor that only copies traffic still lets the traffic pass, so the record is the gain. A device that discards the packet is a refusal, even if it also writes a line, and that refusal wears a different label. Windows Security event 4624 can show that user m.okonkwo signed in successfully. A separate rule can decide the success is strange enough to page the on-call analyst. The page is the control you name. A folder of unread logs is only raw material until someone looks.\n\nThursday night produced ticket SEC-1844 at 04:12 UTC. The alert named m.okonkwo, source address 185.203.118.14, and a country that does not appear in that clinician's travel profile. The directory had already accepted the password. Analyst Priya copied the alert name, the source address, and the timestamp into the ticket before she touched the account. That sequence matters more than the theory. The signal proves a session existed. A password reset that follows is a later action with its own clock, and the write-up should not pretend the alert refused the login.\n\nGive the queue an owner. A page that nobody accepts is a control that fired and still helped no one. Directory clocks and alert clocks should agree, or the line in SEC-1844 cannot be tied to the sign-in record. Once a quarter, a lab account should sign in from a flagged address so a silent rule is not mistaken for a calm night. After the signal is confirmed, isolation and repair keep their own names. Leave the alert described as discovery, then record who accepted the page and how many minutes passed.\n\nStudents swap this label with preventive, corrective, compensating, and directive controls. A reader that never released the door strike stopped the approach, so it is not discovery after the fact. Restoring the prior night's clean server image repairs damage, so that restore is a cleanup. A jump host placed around an old billing application was chosen because a second factor would not fit, which is a stand-in decided before any incident. A procedure that demands a change number before a firewall edit is an order to a person, not news that an edit already occurred. Time is the separating fact. This control speaks after the event, and the ticket should contain the alert that shows the order of events.\n",
        'r0': 'This control raises a signal after an event, later than a refusal and earlier than a repair. Useful forms include a SIEM page, a listen-only sensor, a log review, and a camera review of a door already propped. Impossible travel is one such signal when two sign-ins imply a speed no traveler could meet. Windows Security event 4624 can feed the rule when the sign-in succeeded, while event 4625 counts failures. An inline drop is not this control, because the packet never arrives. A restore from the prior night is not this control either. Ticket SEC-1844 shows the shape: user m.okonkwo, source 185.203.118.14, 04:12 UTC, country absent from the profile. Choose this label when the evidence is an alert, a log hit, or a recording of what already occurred. A queue with no owner wastes the page.\n',
        'r1': 'This kind of control speaks after something already happened. It raises a signal a person can check. A strange sign-in alert fits this idea. A next-morning camera review fits it too. The signal does not lock the door. The signal does not rebuild the server. A refusal that held is a different idea. A cleanup after damage is a different idea. A written order is a different idea. Someone must read the page or it sits unused. Write down the time on the alert. Point at the notice when you name this control.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a smoke alarm in a clinic break-room kitchen. The alarm shouts after a pan has already started to scorch. It does not pour water, and it does not stop the next cook from lighting a match. Its value is the shout in time for someone to walk over. A lock on the supply closet is a different object in that hallway. A mop after the scorch mark is a different object too. A card that orders staff to turn the burner off is only an instruction. In the real control, the page about m.okonkwo is the shout, not the lock and not the restore. If nobody walks over when that page arrives, the night does not change.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ticket SEC-1844 and copy the user, source address, and timestamp. Match the alert to the directory sign-in before you name a cause. Confirm the session already existed when the page fired. Leave any later password reset in a separate note. Do not describe the alert as a block. Do not describe a restore as this same control. Stop when the signal and its clock time are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'An alert after the event is this control. A lock that held is not. A restore after damage is not. A jump-host stand-in chosen beforehand is not. A mandatory procedure is not. Listen-only capture fits this label, and an inline drop does not. An ignored queue makes the signal useless in practice. Time order is the fact that separates the neighbors.\n',
    },
    'Corrective controls': {
        'body': "After an unwanted act has already succeeded, the remaining job is to repair the damage or to return a known-good state. A repair with that timing is a corrective control. A page that merely announces the harm leaves the clinic unable to pay staff if the payroll server stays encrypted. The shout and the cleanup are different moments, and the ticket should not collapse them into one word.\n\nRestoration is the work. A team brings back a clean copy made before the harm, resets accounts that were exposed, rebuilds a host from a trusted image, or moves service to a spare that still works. Quarantine of a malicious file after the file has run is usually taught here, because the damage is already underway and the quarantine limits it. Patching a hole after attackers used it belongs here too. Patching that same hole before any exploit is an earlier refusal, not this label. The repair fails when the only backup is also encrypted, or when nobody has ever practiced the path.\n\nTicket 5120 started when ransom notes appeared on the payroll server. The alert that named the note is not the repair. The repair was the restore of the prior night's clean copy and the reset of the affected accounts. The ticket should record which backup generation was still clean, who reset the accounts, and how long users were unable to work. A warm spare that takes the load after the primary fails can be this control when service actually returns. The written steps the team followed are an instruction. The act of restoring is the control a question wants when it asks what cleaned up the mess.\n\nA defender preserves evidence before wiping a host, then restores and resets. The alert id stays in one note and the image id stays in another, so the timeline stays honest. The restored copy should be opened far enough to read a payroll file, rather than trusted from a job status alone. If staff are still locked out, the cleanup is not finished. A quiet Friday rehearsal of this path, with the minutes written down, is a habit. The habit matters, and it remains a different label from the restore that follows a real incident.\n\nNeighbors sit on either side of the clock. An alert after the notes appeared is a detective control, because detective controls notice after the fact. A jump host that limited an old application before any incident is a compensating control, a stand-in used because the preferred safeguard would not fit. A procedure that orders the restore steps is a directive control. A lock that held, so the ransom never landed, is a preventive control. Cleanup after the bad act succeeded is what separates this label. Evidence of a return to a trusted state points here.\n",
        'r0': "A corrective control returns a known-good state after the unwanted event has already succeeded. On ticket 5120 the repair was the prior night's clean copy plus resets of the affected accounts, not the alert that named the ransom note. Patching after exploitation is this timing, whereas patching before exploitation is preventive. A documented jump host is a compensating stand-in, not this repair. The written restore procedure is a directive, not the restore action. Quarantine after a malicious file has run is usually taught here. Warm-spare failover that returns service after the failure belongs here too. The repair fails if that backup generation is also encrypted. Evidence is the clean image id, the account resets, and the downtime.\n",
        'r1': 'This control cleans up after the damage. It brings back a known good copy. It can reset accounts that were exposed. The bad act has already succeeded. The alarm that only shouted is earlier. A written plan is not the cleanup itself. A substitute lock chosen earlier is different. Name the restore when you mean the cleanup. A backup that is also damaged cannot finish the job. Record which copy was still clean. Record who reset the accounts. Time how long the service was down.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen after soup has already hit the floor. The mop and bucket come out to return the floor to a state staff can walk across. The mop does not stop the next bowl from tipping, and it does not shout when the bowl leaves the counter. A bell that rang as the bowl slipped is a different object. A second tray used because the first tray cracked last week is a different object too. A card that orders the cook to mop is only an instruction. If the bucket is empty, the cleanup cannot finish no matter how loud the bell was. In the clinic, restoring the prior night's clean copy after ransomware is that mop. An alert that only names the ransom note is the bell, not the cleanup.\n",
        'r4': 'Identify the last clean copy before you restore anything on ticket 5120. Record that generation id once you know it is clean. Restore it only after evidence from the host is preserved. Reset the exposed accounts and list them beside the image id. Keep the alert that announced the ransom note in a separate note. Keep the written procedure in a separate note. Do not describe the alert as the repair. Stop when the clean state and the account resets are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A restore after harm is a corrective control. The alert that revealed the harm is not. A jump host planned before the incident is not. A procedure page is not. A patch after a breach is this label, and a patch before any exploit is not. Quarantine after a malicious file has run is usually this label. A backup that is also encrypted cannot serve the repair. Return to a trusted state is the fact that separates this label.\n',
    },
    'Compensating controls': {
        'body': 'When the safeguard you wanted cannot be installed yet, another control has to carry the same goal by a different restraint. That stand-in is what this label names. It is chosen before an incident, because a gap is already known, and it should say plainly that the preferred safeguard is still missing. Pretending the original control is present is how these write-ups fail an assessor.\n\nThe work starts from the objective, not from the gadget. Write what the missing control was supposed to achieve, why it will not fit, what restraint you added, who owns it, and the date you will look again. Payment-card reviews expect that set of facts when a required control is impractical. Extra logging around a system you cannot patch counts only if a person actually reads those logs against the original goal. A note that says be careful, with no restraint behind it, does not qualify.\n\nMedBill 4.2 is the clinic billing application, and its sign-in screen cannot prompt a second factor. Engineers proved that limit in a test tenant. Change CHG-2190 then allowed the application only from admin jump host jh-adm-01 at 10.20.8.15, and the firewall drops every other source. The review date on the exception is 30 June 2026. The jump host is the stand-in. It is not multi-factor logic inside the old program, and the ticket should not describe it that way. A tester who tries the billing address from a nursing-station PC should see the connection fail.\n\nKeep the exception alive on purpose. Before the review date, either install a real second factor or renew the write-up with fresh evidence that the path is still tight. A jump host that was temporary in March should not still be the only story in December with no owner. If staff begin using a second admin workstation because the first one is slow, the restraint has quietly widened. Defenders recheck the firewall rule, the account list on the jump host, and the logs of who signed in there.\n\nStudents call an after-the-fact page this kind of control, but a page only notices misuse and does not stand in for the missing prompt. A restore after ransomware is cleanup, not a substitute chosen because a control would not fit. A poster that tells staff to avoid the old application is an instruction, not an enforced path. The separating fact is the gap you already knew about: the preferred control cannot be used yet, and a different restraint is doing its job until a dated review.\n',
        'r0': 'Use this label when a preferred safeguard cannot be implemented yet and a different restraint carries the same objective. MedBill 4.2 cannot prompt a second factor, so CHG-2190 allows it only from jump host 10.20.8.15, with a review on 30 June 2026. That host does not become multi-factor code inside the old binary. Card-data reviews expect the objective, the constraint, the substitute, and the upkeep. An alert, a post-incident restore, and a posted slogan are different labels. Extra logging qualifies only when someone reviews it against the original goal. An exception with no owner and no end date turns into a permanent hole. Ticket evidence is the missing prompt, the single source address, and the review date.\n',
        'r1': 'This control fills in when the lock you wanted will not fit. It is a stand-in, not the original lock. Name why the first choice failed. Name the restraint you added instead. Name who watches that restraint. Name the date you will look again. A guard at a door under repair can be this idea. An alarm after the fact is not this idea. A cleanup after a break-in is not this idea. A poster that says be careful is not this idea. Do not pretend the missing lock is already there.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a loading-dock door at a clinic warehouse while the badge reader is still on order. A guard and a sign-in clipboard stand in the doorway during receiving hours, and a chain holds the door after the shift. The clipboard is not the reader that was ordered. It is a brace with a date beside it. A bell that rings after someone is already inside is a different picture. A mop after mud is tracked across the floor is a different picture too. On the real application, the jump host is that brace, and the June review is the date the reader is supposed to arrive. If nobody checks the brace, the stand-in has stopped doing the job.\n',
        'r4': 'Name the preferred safeguard that will not fit before you use this label. Record jump host 10.20.8.15 as the stand-in on CHG-2190. Set the 30 June 2026 review and assign an owner. Separate this write-up from alerts that only notice misuse. Separate it from any restore that happens after damage. Attach the reason the second factor cannot be prompted. Stop when the gap, the restraint, and the review date are all on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A stand-in for a missing preferred control is this label. An alert is not. A restore is not. A poster is not. Jump-host-only access for an old application that cannot prompt a second factor is the classic stem. A write-up and an end date belong with a defensible answer. Do not describe the stand-in as if the preferred control were already installed.\n',
    },
    'Directive controls': {
        'body': 'Some controls exist as orders. They tell a person the step that is required, in a procedure, on a posted card, or in a change path, and they expect the person to obey. The control is the instruction. It is not the firewall that may also refuse a packet, and it is not the log that later shows who typed anyway.\n\nThe order has to be specific enough to follow and visible before the action. A sentence that says must is stronger than a sentence that says should. The page needs an owner and a place the night shift actually opens. If the only copy sits in a folder nobody uses, the order is theoretical. Awareness classes that people are required to attend are orders of this kind. The roster that proves they sat in the room is a separate fact about a habit being practiced.\n\nSOP-CHG-04 is the clinic change procedure. It states that a firewall edit is forbidden until a change number is written on the ticket. On CHG-2301 the night engineer quoted that sentence in the work note and waited for the number CAB-7741 before opening the ruleset. The quote is the evidence for this label. The later firewall log, which shows the edit at 22:16, is a record of what was typed. It does not replace the sentence that told the engineer to wait. A banner that only says authorized use may continue is a warning, and it becomes this kind of control only if it commands a specific act, such as locking the screen when you leave.\n\nDefenders test the order the way they test a device rule: can the person who must obey it find the current sentence, and does that sentence still match the work. When remote wipe becomes mandatory, the card that still says optional is a failed order even if a console setting changed. Quote the must sentence and the document revision in the ticket. Do not cite a hit counter as if it were the instruction.\n\nStudents see a policy page and want one label for every axis. The same page can be a governed decision because a leader signed it, and it can still be an order because it tells staff what they must do. The stem chooses the axis. A device that refuses the edit is not the order. An alert about an edit with no ticket is discovery after the fact. Rolling the ruleset back is cleanup. A jump host around an old application is a stand-in. The separating fact is the instruction itself: if the evidence you can point at is a mandatory sentence, this is the label.\n',
        'r0': 'This control is a mandatory instruction: a procedure, a posted order, or a required step such as a change number before a firewall edit. SOP-CHG-04 forbids the edit until the number is on the ticket, and CHG-2301 quotes that sentence before CAB-7741. The firewall log at 22:16 is not the instruction. Alerts, restores, and jump-host stand-ins are different labels. A signed policy can be both a governed document and an order, and the stem picks the axis. Must language points here, while should language is a weaker guide. A required class is an order, separate from the attendance roster of the habit. A login banner that only warns is usually a deterrent unless it commands a specific act. Evidence is the sentence people are supposed to obey.\n',
        'r1': 'This control tells people what they must do. It is an order, not a hint. A required step on a card fits. A posted instruction fits too. The words expect a person to obey. A machine that blocks is not this order. A late alarm is not this order. A cleanup afterward is not this order. A stand-in lock is not this order. Read for the word must. An old page sends people the wrong way. Point at the sentence when you use this label.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse pick card that tells the clerk to scan the bin before lifting the box. The card is the order, and the job counts on the clerk to follow it. The card does not grab the clerk's wrists. A buzzer after the wrong box is on the pallet is a different picture. Repacking the pallet later is a different picture too. A temporary cage used because the scanner is broken is yet another picture. On the real change path, the sentence that demands a ticket number is that pick card. If the card says should instead of must, the order has gone soft.\n",
        'r4': 'Quote the must sentence from SOP-CHG-04 on ticket CHG-2301. Record the document revision beside that quote. Check that the night engineer can open the current page before the edit. Keep the firewall log in a separate note from the instruction. Keep any alert about a missing ticket in a separate note. Keep a ruleset rollback in a separate note. Stop when the ticket cites the order itself.\n',
        'exam': 'A mandatory instruction is this label. A device refusal is not. An alert after an edit is not. A restore is not. A stand-in control is not. Must versus should is the language test. The same signed page can still be a governed document when the stem asks who owns the decision. A login banner that only warns is a deterrent unless it commands a specific act. Quote the must sentence, not a firewall hit counter.\n',
    },
    'Confidentiality': {
        'body': "Only approved readers should be able to see the data. That limit on readers is confidentiality. It is a different claim from whether the bytes were altered, and a different claim from whether the service answered when staff needed it. A salary list can be perfectly accurate and still be a serious failure once it sits in the wrong mailbox. The original file can remain unchanged. The exposure is still real.\n\nHiding the data, and limiting who may open it, is the mechanism. On a laptop, AES-256 full-disk encryption withholds the readable text when the volume key is absent. While the data moves, TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 keeps a browser session on HTTPS rather than on cleartext HTTP. A file copy belongs on SFTP or another protected channel, not on plain FTP that anyone on the path can read. A mail gateway can block a spreadsheet before the message leaves. Need to know still applies inside the building. A nurse who may open a chart should not open a neighbor's payroll row. A hash such as SHA-256 does not hide bytes, because a hash is not a secret.\n\nTicket 5510 is a disclosure. A clerk forwarded the payroll sheet to a personal mailbox outside the clinic directory. The totals were unchanged, and the payroll site was up. The harm is that an unapproved reader could see the salaries. The ticket should name the file, the sender, the personal destination, and whether the gateway blocked the forward or missed it. Someone reading a screen over a shoulder, dumpster diving for printed pages, and a trick that gets an employee to read a secret aloud chase the same goal. A shredder and a screen filter serve that goal for paper and for a glance. A shared mailbox password erases the boundary, because everyone who knows the password can read as if approved.\n\nA defender traces the forward before debating the label. The list of people allowed to read the sheet has to be compared with the destination. The gateway rule and the volume encryption on the clerk's laptop belong in the notes. The ticket should not be closed as a changed number when the bytes match. It should not be closed as an outage when the site answered. Personal data in a personal mailbox is a real exposure, not a harmless shortcut. Health details make that shortcut worse, because the reader was never approved.\n\nThe trap is to fold three goals into one word. Integrity asks whether an approved person was the one who changed the contents. Availability asks whether the service was usable at the time the work needed it. This goal asks who was able to see the data. A file can be copied with every byte intact and still be a loss of confidentiality. Unwanted reading, copying, or forwarding is what separates this goal from the other two.\n",
        'r0': 'Confidentiality restricts reading to approved parties. AES-256 at rest and TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 in transit are the usual mechanisms, so HTTPS and SFTP fit and cleartext HTTP or plain FTP do not. SHA-256 does not provide this property, because a hash does not hide the bytes. Ticket 5510 is this failure: a clerk forwarded the payroll sheet to a personal mailbox while the totals stayed intact and the site stayed up. Shoulder reading, dumpster diving, and a trick that gets a secret read aloud are the same harm. A mail gateway can block the forward. Need to know still limits readers inside an authenticated workforce. Choose this label when the harm is unwanted viewing or copying.\n',
        'r1': 'Only approved people should see the data. A secret in the wrong mailbox breaks that limit. The numbers can still be correct. The site can still be up. Seeing is the harm in the wrong mailbox. A slow page is a different harm. A closed diary is a picture of this limit. Reading over a shoulder attacks the same limit. Papers in the trash attack it too. A shared mailbox password weakens the boundary. A hash does not hide the bytes. Ask who was allowed to look.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a staff envelope in a clinic drawer, closed so only the named person may open it. The envelope and the habit of shutting it are the protection. They are not a promise that the sentences inside never change. If someone photographs the open page, the secret is already lost even though the ink is untouched. A torn page would be a different harm. An envelope locked away so completely that the nurse cannot reach it during a visit raises a different problem about getting work done. In the clinic, the payroll sheet in a personal mailbox is that opened envelope. The harm is unwanted reading, even when every total is still the same.\n',
        'r4': 'Trace the payroll sheet on ticket 5510 from the clerk to the personal mailbox. Record whether a gateway rule stopped the forward or missed it. List the people approved to read the sheet before you judge the destination. Keep any edited total in a separate note. Keep any slowness of the payroll site in a separate note. Attach the sender, the destination, and the time. Do not change mailbox rules until those facts are on the ticket. Stop when the evidence is unwanted reading.\n',
        'exam': 'Unwanted viewing is confidentiality. A changed total is not. A site that is up but too slow to use is not. A hash does not create this property. AES-256 and TLS protect it, and they are not by themselves the label for an edited total. Reading over a shoulder and dumpster diving target this property. Forwarding payroll to a personal mailbox is this loss even when the file is unaltered. Who could see the data is the separating fact.\n',
    },
    'Integrity': {
        'body': 'Contents should remain as an approved person left them, so a later reader can trust that nobody slipped in a quiet edit. That promise about unaltered content is integrity. Money can move on an invoice that still looks ordinary to a hurried clerk. The file can stay hidden from outsiders and the portal can stay online. The edited total is still a failure of this promise, because the bytes no longer match what the manager left behind.\n\nChecks make the promise testable. A cryptographic hash such as SHA-256 changes when even one bit of the file changes. SHA-1 and MD5 are the wrong tools for a dispute, because practical collisions exist. A simple error check can catch accidental noise on a line, and an attacker who can calculate the check again is not stopped. When both sides share a key, an HMAC makes a bare hash harder to replace with a forged one. A digital signature goes further by using a private key, so the check supports unaltered content and also a claim about who produced the bytes. Monitoring of important files compares live hashes with a baseline kept where that same attacker cannot quietly replace the expected value. AES-GCM can support secrecy and this check together. A hash by itself never hides the document.\n\nTicket 5622 is this failure. The total on an approved invoice moved from 18400 to 81400 after the manager had signed. The portal was online. The set of people who could open the file had not grown. The harm is the altered number. The ticket should show the original approved total, the new total, and the hash mismatch or the missing signature. An attacker who changes the total and publishes a fresh MD5 has walked past that weak check. Small repeated edits, and a quiet change to a message while it is on the path, are the same harm. The content no longer matches what an approved person sent.\n\nA defender hashes the current invoice and compares it with the protected baseline before arguing about who could see the file. The baseline has to be protected from the accounts that edit invoices. A forward of an unchanged sheet belongs in a different note, because copying is not this failure. A portal outage belongs in a different note. When the question asks who cannot deny the approval later, the signature issue has moved past a bare check of the bytes. Keep the signing key separate from the hash of the file, or the two questions will collapse.\n\nStudents stop when they hear that something is wrong with the file. Unwanted viewing, with the bytes intact, is confidentiality. A site that answers too slowly to use is availability. A manager who cannot credibly deny releasing a payment is a non-repudiation question, and that question needs proof tied to the person, not only a hash. An unapproved change is what separates this promise. A total, a seal, or a hash that no longer matches is the evidence to point at.\n',
        'r0': 'Integrity means approved content was not altered by anyone else. SHA-256 is the hash to teach, while MD5 and SHA-1 are weak because practical collisions exist. An HMAC adds a shared key so a stranger cannot swap a bare hash as easily. A digital signature uses a private key and also supports a later claim about who signed. Ticket 5622 shows an approved invoice total that moved from 18400 to 81400 while the portal stayed online. Confidentiality is about readers, and availability is about a usable service. AES-GCM can protect ciphertext as well, but a hash alone does not hide the document. Store the baseline where the same attacker cannot rewrite it. Choose this label when the stem shows modification, a hash mismatch, or a broken seal.\n',
        'r1': 'The message should stay as it was sent. An edit after approval breaks that promise. This promise is about change. It is not about who may look. It is not about a closed shop. A torn seal puts the contents in doubt. Copying a page without an edit is different. A slow counter does not change the total. Compare the numbers with the approved copy. Keep the original record somewhere safe. A weak check is easy for an attacker to redo. Point at the altered total when you name this promise.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse carton closed with numbered tape before it leaves the dock. The tape shows whether someone opened the carton after the clerk sealed it. The tape is not a rule about who may read the label on the outside. If the tape is torn and the count inside has changed, the seal failed. A stranger reading the label through a window is a different harm. A carton that never arrives at the clinic is a different harm too. A seal the same suspect can reprint at the dock is a weak seal. In the clinic, the invoice total that moved after the manager signed is that torn tape. The failure is an unapproved change, not a question of who was allowed to look.\n',
        'r4': 'Compare the approved invoice total with the current total on ticket 5622. Hash the file and match it to the stored baseline. Record the original number, the new number, and the mismatch. Keep a forward of an unchanged sheet in a separate note. Keep any portal outage in a separate note. Protect the baseline from the accounts that edit invoices. Do not rewrite the baseline to match the altered total. Stop when the ticket shows an unapproved change.\n',
        'exam': 'An edited total after approval is an integrity failure. An unchanged file in the wrong mailbox is not. A site that will not load in time is not. MD5 and SHA-1 are weak choices where collisions matter. SHA-256, an HMAC, and a digital signature are the tools to remember. A simple error check does not stop an attacker who can recompute it. A question about who cannot deny an act later is non-repudiation, not only this promise. Point at the unapproved change.\n',
    },
    'Availability': {
        'body': "A service that answers and still cannot be used has missed the goal that matters on a Friday deadline. That requirement for usable service is availability. Up is not the same claim as usable. A form that takes most of a minute to open can miss a deadline as surely as a form that never opens. Staff do not experience a green status icon. They experience whether the work finished before the cutoff they actually have.\n\nCapacity, a working path, and a spare when the primary fails are the mechanism. The service has to accept the real request and finish it in time for the task. Monitoring should watch the finish time, not only whether a port answers. A rate limit, and failover to a healthy spare, protect this goal when load or a dead host is the cause. A backup that nobody has restored is not yet proof that the service can return. The clock that matters sits beside the user's deadline, not on a status page that only says the process is running. A light that says the process is alive does not submit the form.\n\nOn Friday at 16:10 the payroll site answered on TCP 443 and still failed the clinic. The weekly hours form took forty-eight seconds to open, and staff could not submit before the 17:00 deadline. Ticket 5704 recorded the clock time, the form name, and the number of people stuck on the page. Salaries were not exposed. The stored totals were not edited. The harm was that approved work could not be finished when it was due. A status page that said the process was running did not help the clerk who still could not press submit. The site existed, and the Friday work still did not get done.\n\nA defender measures the path a person actually uses, not a green icon alone. The ticket should carry the observed seconds, the deadline, and the number of failed submissions. The hunt is for a resource that is exhausted, a dependency that is down, or a spare that did not take over. A disclosure belongs in a separate note. A changed total belongs in a separate note. After evidence is safe, fixing the cause is part of making the service usable again. If an incident is underway, disabling a stolen account and isolating a host are containment. Those steps are not, by themselves, proof that the service is usable again.\n\nThe trap is to hear that the site is up and to mark this goal as met. Confidentiality can be intact while nobody can submit the form. Integrity can be intact while the form never finishes. A backup that exists on a shelf does not prove Friday will go well. Usable at the time of the work is what separates this goal. If staff could not finish the real task, availability failed, even though a status page said the process was running.\n",
        'r0': 'Availability means the approved service is usable when the work needs it. On Friday at 16:10 the payroll site answered on TCP 443, yet the weekly hours form took forty-eight seconds and staff missed the 17:00 deadline. Ticket 5704 records the time, the form, and how many people were stuck. Salaries were not disclosed and totals were not edited, so the other two goals stayed intact. Watch finish time, not only an open port. A rate limit and failover to a healthy spare address load and a dead host. A backup nobody has restored is not proof the service can return. Disabling a stolen account and isolating a host are containment during an incident. They do not by themselves show that the form can be submitted again.\n',
        'r1': 'The service must be usable when work needs it. Up is not the same as usable. A very slow page can miss a deadline. Friday pay hours are a fair test. Staff who cannot submit have lost this goal. Secrets can still be hidden. Totals can still be unchanged. Watch the finish time, not a green icon. A spare that never takes over does not help. A backup nobody has restored is not proof. Write the seconds and the deadline. Measure the path a real person uses.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic elevator that opens and then climbs so slowly the patient misses the appointment. The car is present, and the trip still fails the purpose. A stairwell locked all morning can protect a chart and also block care, which is a different mix of problems. A sign beside the doors does not make the car arrive on time. A spare car helps only if someone actually moves the patient across. The clock on the wall matters more than a lamp that says the motor is running. In the clinic, the payroll site that answers and still will not finish the weekly hours form is that elevator. Staff could not complete the Friday work while the form crawled.\n',
        'r4': 'Time the weekly hours form the way a clerk does, and write the seconds on ticket 5704. Record the Friday deadline beside that number. Count how many people could not submit. Note that salaries were not exposed and totals were not edited. Look for an exhausted resource, a dead dependency, or a spare that stayed idle. Keep any disclosure in a separate note. Do not call a green status icon proof that the work finished. Stop when the ticket shows the form was not usable in time.\n',
        'exam': 'A site that is up but too slow for the Friday deadline is an availability failure. Hidden salaries are a different goal. An edited total is a different goal. A green status icon does not prove the form could be submitted. A backup on a shelf is not proof until someone has restored it. Disabling a stolen account and isolating a host are containment, not proof the service is usable again. Usable at the time of the work is the fact that separates this goal.\n',
    },
    'Non-repudiation': {
        'body': 'The person who acted should not be able to credibly deny it later. That tie between actor and act is non-repudiation. The proof has to be tied to the actor. A log that any shared password could have written is not enough, because the actor can still shrug. Closing that shrug is the point of the control. A record that several people could have produced does not pin the act on one of them.\n\nA digital signature is the usual mechanism. The signer uses a private key, and other people check the signature with the matching public key. The record should carry the time and the exact action, so the proof is about one payment rather than a vague claim that someone was nearby. Unaltered bytes are necessary and not sufficient. A hash can show that the content was not quietly edited. A hash does not, by itself, name the human who is stuck with the act. A sign-in check shows who is asking at the door right now. It does not, by itself, leave a proof for a dispute next month. A shared mailbox login breaks the tie, because several people can produce the same trail.\n\nElena Diaz released payment 18420, and the approval record carries her signature. Ticket 5808 asks whether she can later say she never released the payment. The clinic can show the signed record, the amount, and the time, checked against her key. She cannot shrug off the release without claiming the key was stolen, and a stolen key is a different investigation. The payment record has to survive her later denial. A chat line from a shared account would not have done that job, because any member of the team could have typed it.\n\nA defender protects the private key and the time source. If the whole team can copy the key, the signature no longer picks out one person. If the clock can be moved, the time on the record becomes soft. The signed record should be stored where the signer cannot delete the only copy. The key id, the amount, and the timestamp belong on the ticket before anyone debates intent. A message that merely says the payment was approved, sent from a shared account, is not this kind of proof. Keep the public check working, or the signature becomes an ornament nobody can test.\n\nThe trap is to stop at integrity or at authentication. An invoice total that changed after approval is an integrity failure. A password plus a phone prompt answers who is at the door at that moment, which is authentication. A log line that names a shared account can support accounting, and the actor may still deny the click. Proof tied to the person is what separates this property. If the manager cannot credibly deny the release, the control has done this job.\n',
        'r0': 'Non-repudiation ties an act to a person so a later denial is not credible. Elena Diaz released payment 18420, and ticket 5808 rests on her signature over that amount and time, checked with the public key that matches her private key. A hash can show the bytes were not edited and still fail to name her. A password prompt shows who is at the door now and does not preserve the dispute record. A shared mailbox login lets several people produce the same trail. Protect the private key from the whole team, protect the clock, and keep a copy she cannot delete. A stolen-key claim is a separate investigation. Choose this label when the stem asks who cannot deny the act.\n',
        'r1': 'The actor should not be able to deny the act. The proof has to be tied to that person. A signature on a payment can provide that tie. A shared password makes denial too easy. A hash alone does not name the person. A sign-in proves who is here right now. A log from a shared account is weak proof. Protect the private key from the whole team. Protect the clock that stamps the record. Store a copy the signer cannot erase. Write the amount, the time, and the key id. A later shrug is what this control should stop.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse receiver who signs a clipboard when a pallet comes in. The mark ties that person to that pallet, so a later claim that the pallet never arrived is hard to sustain. A line that says only the day shift lets everyone deny the receipt. A torn carton is a different problem about what was inside. A badge check at the door only shows who stood there at that moment. The page has to be kept where the receiver cannot throw away the only copy. In the clinic, the signed approval Elena Diaz gave for the payment is that clipboard. She cannot credibly deny the release while the signature still ties the act to her.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 5808 and copy the signer, the amount 18420, and the time. Record the key id used to check the signature. Confirm the private key is not shared by the whole team. Confirm the signed record is stored where the signer cannot erase the only copy. Keep any hash-only comparison in a separate note. Keep a shared-account chat line in a separate note. Do not delete the approval record while you are identifying the tie. Stop when the ticket shows proof tied to one person.\n',
        'exam': 'Proof that stops a credible denial is non-repudiation. An edited invoice total is an integrity failure, not this property by itself. A password plus a phone prompt is authentication at the moment of sign-in. A log from a shared account can still be denied. A hash without a person attached does not finish this job. A signature checked with the matching public key is the usual proof. If the manager cannot credibly deny the release, this is the label.\n',
    },
    'Authentication': {
        'body': 'The first question at a door is who is asking. The check that answers it uses a secret, a device, or a body trait. That check of identity is authentication. It does not decide which chart the person may open. It does not write the history of what the person did afterward. Those later questions have their own names, and mixing them produces the wrong lesson on a ticket.\n\nThe check has to be something the real person can produce and an impostor should lack. A password is a secret. A prompt on a phone the person carries is a device check. A body trait is the third family. Two of them together, such as a password plus a one-time code from an authenticator, are stronger than a password alone, because a stolen password is not enough. The directory compares the offered proof with the stored proof. A match means the system will treat the session as that person. A mismatch should fail closed rather than fail open. The check can be repeated when risk changes. A fixed second prompt on every sign-in is still this same question, asked the same way each time.\n\nIdris signed in to the clinic directory from a nursing station PC. The password was accepted, and the sign-in then waited for a code from the phone Idris carries. Without that phone, the session never opened. Ticket 5902 records the account, the station name, and the failed second check at 07:42. A coworker who knew the password could not finish. The control worked. In that moment the directory did not decide whether Idris may approve a drug order. It decided only that the asker matched the account.\n\nA defender reads the failed and successful checks before resetting anything. The second factor should not be a code written on a note stuck to the station. When the proof is in the wrong hands, disabling the stolen account is containment. That disable does not mean the original check was the wrong kind of control, and it is not eradication. Eradication would remove malicious code and close the hole after evidence is kept. The decision about what the account may do belongs in a separate note. A body-trait reader at a closet still answers who is there, and it is also a tangible barrier. The question states which axis it wants.\n\nStudents hear the word access and reach for one label for the whole path. Authorization answers what that identified person is allowed to do. Accounting records who did what, and when. A harder prompt because the sign-in came from a new country is adaptive identity, which changes the difficulty and still rests on this check. The who is what separates this label. Evidence of a secret, a device, or a body trait offered as proof of identity points here.\n',
        'r0': 'Authentication answers who is asking. Idris reached the clinic directory from a nursing station, passed the password, and then failed the phone code at 07:42, so ticket 5902 shows a session that never opened. A password is a secret, the phone is a device, and a body trait is the third family. A one-time code from an authenticator, often a TOTP value, means a stolen password is not enough. Fail closed on a mismatch. A fixed second prompt on every sign-in is still this question. Disabling a stolen account is containment, not eradication. Eradication removes the malicious code and closes the hole after evidence is preserved. Authorization and the action log are different labels.\n',
        'r1': 'The question is who is asking. A secret can answer that question. A device the person carries can answer it. A body trait can answer it too. A password plus a phone prompt is stronger. A passed check is not permission to act. A record of the act comes later. A failed second check keeps the door shut. Read the log before you reset a password. A note with the code defeats the phone. Disabling a stolen account limits further use. The who is the fact that separates neighbors.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic desk that compares a face with a photo badge before handing over a chart. The comparison answers who is standing there. It does not decide which pages that person may read, and it does not file a history of the visit. A password whispered by a coworker is like a borrowed badge. A second look at a phone in the person's pocket is like asking for a card from that pocket. A desk that waves everyone through has stopped doing the check. In the clinic, a password plus a prompt on the phone Idris carries is that desk. Without the phone, the session stays shut even when the password is already known.\n",
        'r4': 'Open ticket 5902 and copy the account, the station, and the 07:42 result. Confirm the password succeeded and the phone code failed. Check that the second code is not written on a note at the station. Leave any drug-order decision in a separate note. Leave the action history in a separate note. If the account is stolen, disable it and label that step containment. Do not call that disable eradication. Stop when the ticket shows which proof of identity failed.\n',
        'exam': 'A secret, a device, or a body trait used to prove who is asking is authentication. What that person may do is authorization. The record of the act is accounting. A harder prompt from a new country changes difficulty and still rests on this check. Disabling a stolen account is containment, not eradication. Eradication removes the malicious code and closes the hole after evidence is kept. A failed phone code with a known password means the who was not completed. Do not treat a passed sign-in as permission.\n',
    },
    'Authorization': {
        'body': 'Being known is not the same thing as being allowed to act. The step after identity is the decision about what that person may do. That decision about permitted actions is authorization. A nurse and a doctor can both be genuine and still not share the same permissions. The sign-in can succeed for both. The drug order can still be reserved for only one of them, and the system should say so in a way an analyst can read.\n\nRules run after identity is accepted. A role, a group, or an explicit permission on an object is compared with the action the person requested. If the role includes the action, the system permits it. If the role does not include the action, the system refuses it even though the sign-in succeeded. The refusal should name the action and the role, so an analyst sees a decision rather than a mystery error. A privilege that lets one clerk both create and approve a payment weakens the split. Keeping those two actions on different roles is a common way to keep the rule honest. The comparison happens on each request, not once at the start of the year.\n\nThe clinic chart system shows the split in ordinary work. Nurse Brooks can read the assigned chart. The same account cannot approve a drug order that clinic policy reserves for a doctor. Ticket 6004 records the account, the approve action, the nurse role, and a deny at 09:15. The password and the phone prompt had already succeeded. The deny is not a failed sign-in. It is a successful identification followed by a refused action. A staff card that opens the lobby and does not open the pharmacy safe is the same split in the building. The building and the chart system are different rooms, and the logic is the same.\n\nA defender reads the deny reason before resetting a password. A password reset would repeat a check that already passed. The role and the written policy both belong on the ticket. Accounts that have gathered extra permissions over time deserve a look, and the first look should not delete those permissions if the task is only to explain the deny. A later change to the role is a separate ticket. If a policy engine made the decision, name the engine when the question asks who decided. In this chart story, the chart system itself refused the order.\n\nThe trap is to treat a successful sign-in as permission to act. Authentication only established who asked. A log of the deny can feed accounting, and an alert on that log can be a detective control. Neither one is the rule that refused the click. An extra prompt from a new country is adaptive identity, and that prompt is still about who is asking. The allowed action is what separates this label. A genuine person met with a refused action points here.\n',
        'r0': 'Authorization decides what an identified person may do. Nurse Brooks could read the assigned chart, and ticket 6004 shows a deny at 09:15 on the approve action because the role was nurse, not doctor. The password and phone prompt had already succeeded, so the failure was not authentication. A role or an explicit permission is compared with the requested action on each try. Letting one clerk both create and approve a payment weakens that split. A password reset would repeat a check that already passed. An alert on the deny is detective, and the deny line can feed accounting. The refused action, not the successful sign-in, is the fact to cite.\n',
        'r1': 'A known person can still be refused. This step asks what that person may do. The nurse can read the assigned chart. The nurse cannot approve the drug order. The doctor role is what policy reserves. A successful sign-in can still end in a deny. Read the deny reason before a password reset. A password reset repeats a check that passed. Extra rights gathered over time need a look. Do not change the role on the first look. Write the account, the action, and the role. The refused action is the fact to name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic hallway where a staff card opens the lobby and still does nothing at the pharmacy safe. The card showed that a person could enter the building. The safe asks a further question about what that person may take. A nurse and a pharmacist can both be genuine and still not share the key. A note of the refused attempt is a record, not the lock on the safe. Changing the card because the safe said no repeats the wrong check. In the clinic, Nurse Brooks reading a chart and being refused a drug order is that safe. The person was known, and the action was still not allowed.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 6004 and copy the account, the approve action, the nurse role, and the 09:15 deny. Confirm the password and the phone prompt had already succeeded. Match the role to the written rule that reserves the drug order for a doctor. Leave any password reset in a separate note. Leave the alert on the deny in a separate note. Do not remove permissions while the task is only to explain the deny. Stop when the ticket shows a known person and a refused action.\n',
        'exam': 'A genuine person refused an action is authorization. The sign-in that established who asked is authentication. A log of the deny is not the rule that refused the click. An alert on that log is a detective control. An extra prompt from a new country is about identity, not about the allowed action. Resetting a password repeats a check that already passed. One person creating and approving the same payment is a weak split. The allowed action is the fact that separates this label.\n',
    },
    'Accounting': {
        'body': 'After a person is known, and after an action is allowed or refused, the system still needs a record of what happened. In security work, that record of actions is accounting. It states who did what, and when, so a later review is possible. It is not the finance ledger that totals salaries. A budget meeting can use the same word and mean a different job. The security meaning is the trail of actions on a system, kept so someone can reconstruct the afternoon.\n\nA durable write at the time of the action is the mechanism. A useful line names the account, the action, the object, and the clock time. The line should be stored where the actor cannot erase the only copy. A copy sent to a central log each minute survives even if someone later wipes the payment server. The line does not, by itself, prove that a private key was used. It does not, by itself, decide whether the action was allowed. It keeps the facts for a person who will review them. A pile of lines that nobody can search is a weak form of the control. Review has to be possible, or the write is only storage.\n\nThe clinic payment system writes a line for every transfer. On Tuesday it recorded account Priya, amount 4200, and 14:06 UTC for transfer 8831. Ticket 6106 asks who moved the money. The line answers without asking anyone to remember the afternoon. A paper sign-out for a cabinet key, with a name and a time, is the same idea in a hallway. If the payment line had named only a shared account called billing, Priya could still deny the click. The trail would not pick out a person. The useful line is specific enough that a reviewer can say which account acted.\n\nA defender copies the line onto the ticket before changing the account. The log clock and the payment clock should agree. The central copy should exist, and the notes should say who can delete it. The line is not a block. The transfer had already happened when the line was written. The line is not a signature unless the record is tied to a private key. Without that tie, a later denial may still be credible. Reading these lines on a schedule is a habit. The habit and the write itself wear different labels, and the stem chooses which one it wants.\n\nStudents hear the everyday word and picture a budget. The finance total is a different job. Authentication answered who was at the door. Authorization answered whether that person could start the transfer. A detective control may raise a page when a line looks strange, and the page is not the line. A record that shows user, action, and time, and that someone can review, is what separates this label. When the stem shows those three facts, this is the answer.\n',
        'r0': 'In the security sense, accounting records who did what and when. The clinic payment system wrote account Priya, amount 4200, and 14:06 UTC for transfer 8831, which is the evidence on ticket 6106. A useful line also names the object, and a copy should leave the server each minute so a later wipe does not erase the review. The line does not prove a private key and does not itself allow or refuse the transfer. A shared account called billing would leave the actor able to deny the click. Authentication answered who was at the door, and authorization answered whether the transfer was allowed. A strange-line alert is detective. Clocks on the log and the payment should match.\n',
        'r1': 'This record says who did what, and when. It is not the ledger that totals pay. A payment line should name the user. It should name the amount and the time. A shared account leaves the who too vague. Keep a copy the actor cannot erase. The line is not itself a block. A signature is a stronger tie than a line. Check that the two clocks agree. Copy the line before you change the account. A write nobody can search is a weak write. User, action, and time are the facts to show.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse board where every hook has a line for a name, a key number, and a clock time. The board does not decide who may take a key, and it does not prove a private mark only one person can make. It does make a later review possible. A line that says only the team name lets each person deny the withdrawal. If the only board is one the holder can wipe, the record is fragile. A bell after the key is already gone is a different object. In the clinic, the payment line that names the user, the amount, and the time is that board. The transfer had already happened, and the line is what makes a review possible.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the payment line for transfer 8831 onto ticket 6106 before you change the account. Record the user Priya, the amount 4200, and the time 14:06 UTC. Check that the log clock and the payment clock agree. Confirm a central copy exists and note who can delete it. Keep any password reset in a separate note. Keep any signature claim in a separate note. Do not call the line a block. Stop when the ticket shows who did what, and when.\n',
        'exam': 'A line that shows user, action, and time is accounting in the security sense. The salary ledger is a different job. Who is at the door is authentication. Whether the transfer was allowed is authorization. An alert on a strange line is a detective control, not the line itself. A shared account leaves room for a denial. A signature tied to a private key is a stronger proof than the line alone. A trail someone can review is the fact that separates this label.\n',
    },
    'Gap analysis': {
        'body': 'A gap analysis sets the control a rule requires next to the control you can show today, and it writes down the difference. It does not install the missing piece. It does not score how likely a loss is. The product is a named delta: this requirement, this evidence, these assets still short.\n\nYou build it line by line. Take a standard clause, collect a query or a screenshot that shows the live state, and list every asset that fails the clause. Opinion without the query is not a gap record. Write the asset tags in the report so a later check can find the same machines. A vulnerability scan can feed the evidence, but the scan is not the comparison by itself. A risk register may later rate the delta. That rating is a second document. Shrinking the standard until the blank disappears, without an owner and a reason, hides the gap instead of naming it.\n\nSTD-ENC-01 requires disk encryption on every clinic laptop. The 12 March inventory query returned loaner tags LN-440 through LN-451, twelve machines, with encryption status none. Report GAP-77 lists those tags, the standard clause, and the query time. It does not claim that images were encrypted that afternoon. A manager who says the loaners rarely leave the building has described a wish, not a closed gap. The twelve blanks remain until a later inventory shows encryption or a signed exception names each tag.\n\nDefenders attach three things to the report: the requirement identifier, the evidence query, and the owner who will accept the delta. Hand GAP-77 to the change that deploys the laptop image, then run the same query after deployment. If two loaners are in a trunk and miss the image, the gap record stays open for those two. Do not mark the standard met because the project plan says the work is scheduled. A scheduled intention is not a present control.\n\nStudents confuse the comparison with the repair project, with a risk score, and with a full audit opinion. The project that turns encryption on is the change that follows. A risk score asks likelihood and impact. An audit may use this comparison and still include interviews and samples the comparison does not replace. The separating fact is the side-by-side result. If you can point at twelve specific loaners that lack a required control, you are looking at the gap, not at the score and not at the finished fix.\n',
        'r0': 'A gap analysis compares a required control with the control you can evidence, then names the shortfall. STD-ENC-01 demands disk encryption on every laptop. The 12 March query found twelve loaners, LN-440 through LN-451, with status none. GAP-77 records the clause, the tags, and the query. It does not turn encryption on, and it does not compute likelihood times impact. A vulnerability scan can supply evidence and is not the comparison by itself. A signed exception can cover a tag only when it names that tag. Re-run the query after the image change. Two machines still blank means the gap stays open for those two. A project plan date is not a completed control.\n',
        'r1': 'This comparison sets a required control beside what you have. It writes down the difference by name. It does not install the missing piece. It does not score how likely a loss is. Twelve loaner laptops with no disk encryption are a difference. A query should list the actual asset tags. A plan to fix them later is not a closed gap. A risk score is a second document. An audit can use the comparison and still be wider. Point at the missing control when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a building inspector walking a clinic supply room with a checklist. One line calls for a lock on the medication cupboard. The cupboard has a latch and no lock, so the inspector writes the blank instead of installing hardware on the spot. The blank is not a guess about how often someone might open the door. It is not the work order that will arrive next week. It is the named miss. A later score of the miss can live on another form. In the laptop story, GAP-77 is that blank line for twelve loaners. The encryption project starts after the blank is written, and it does not erase the blank until a new check passes.\n',
        'r4': 'Open GAP-77 and copy the standard clause and the query date. List tags LN-440 through LN-451 rather than writing loaners in general. Do not mark encryption present because a project is scheduled. Keep any likelihood score in a separate risk note. Assign an owner to the delta before you close the report. Re-run the same query after the image is deployed. Stop when each short laptop is either encrypted or named on a signed exception.\n',
        'exam': 'The side-by-side shortfall is this analysis. The project that installs the missing control is not the analysis. A likelihood-and-impact score is not the analysis. A scan without the comparison is only evidence. Twelve unencrypted loaners against a rule that requires encryption is the stem to recognize. A plan date does not close the gap. Recheck after the change or the record goes stale.\n',
    },
    'Adaptive identity': {
        'body': 'The same account should not face the same difficulty in every situation. The hardness of the sign-in changes with risk signals such as place, device, and behavior. That shift in difficulty is adaptive identity. A familiar pattern stays quiet. A strange pattern has to pass an extra check. The account is still the same person under test. The test is what changes, and the permissions waiting behind the door are a separate question.\n\nSignals feed a decision at sign-in time. The service knows the usual laptop, the usual office network, and the usual hours. When those match, it may accept the password and continue. When the place is a country that account has never used, or the device is new, or the behavior breaks the pattern, the service demands more. More can be a phone prompt, a second code, or a hold until an analyst clears the session. The extra demand is not a new permission model. It is a harder proof of who is asking. A prompt required on every sign-in, with no regard for risk, is ordinary authentication. That fixed prompt does not adapt to the signals in front of it.\n\nThe clinic account Okonkwo uses shows the split. From the usual office laptop on Tuesday morning, the directory accepted the password and opened the session with no extra prompt. Later that week the same account appeared from a new country. The directory required a phone prompt before it would continue, and it recorded the reason as a new location. Ticket 6208 holds both events. One is the quiet success at 08:12 from the office address. The other is the added check at 19:40 from the unfamiliar address. Nothing in those two lines changed what Okonkwo may open. They changed only the difficulty of the proof.\n\nA defender tunes the signals instead of celebrating a quiet morning. A rule that never adds a check is not this control. A rule that adds a check on every sign-in, including the desk laptop, has stopped adapting and has become a fixed prompt. False prompts should be reviewed with the people who travel, so a real trip is not a weekly outage. The ticket should name the signal that fired, the extra check, and the result. The file rule that also looks at device health and the hour belongs in a separate note. That rule decides entry to a resource. This control decides how hard identity is to prove.\n\nThe trap is to call every extra prompt by this name, or to treat the prompt as the whole of a zero trust design. Policy-driven access grants entry from rules and rejects the idea that the inside of the network is already safe. A phone prompt demanded every time is authentication with a second factor. A log of the sign-in is accounting. The change in difficulty is what separates this control. A usual laptop that stays quiet, and a new country that does not, is the pattern to recognize.\n',
        'r0': "Adaptive identity changes sign-in difficulty from signals such as place, device, and behavior. Okonkwo's usual office laptop was accepted at 08:12 with no extra prompt. The same account from a new country at 19:40 had to pass a phone prompt, and ticket 6208 holds both events. The lines do not change what the account may open. A prompt required on every sign-in, including the desk laptop, is ordinary authentication and does not adapt. A rule that never adds a check is not this control either. The folder rule that also demands a healthy device and a work hour is policy-driven access. Cite the signal, the extra check, and the result.\n",
        'r1': 'The sign-in gets harder when risk looks higher. Place, device, and behavior are usual signals. A usual office laptop can stay quiet. A new country should demand an extra check. The account is the same person either way. What changes is how hard the proof is. A prompt on every sign-in does not adapt. A rule that never asks more has stopped adapting. Write which signal forced the extra check. A file rule about health and hour is separate. A second factor every time is ordinary proof. The change in difficulty is the fact to name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic reception desk that waves a familiar face through on a weekday morning and stops someone whose bus just arrived from far away. The person may be the same patient either way. The questions get harder when the arrival looks unusual. Asking every regular for a full stack of papers each morning is no longer a response to risk. Never asking a second question is not a response to risk either. A rule about which room they may enter is a later decision. In the clinic, the quiet office-laptop sign-in for Okonkwo and the extra phone prompt from a new country are that desk. The difficulty changed, and the permissions did not.\n',
        'r4': "Open ticket 6208 and copy both sign-in events. Record the quiet success at 08:12 from the office laptop. Record the extra phone prompt at 19:40 and the new-country reason. Confirm the account's permissions did not change between the two events. Keep the file-share rule about device health and hour in a separate note. Do not treat a prompt that fires on every sign-in as this control. Stop when the ticket shows the signal and the change in difficulty.\n",
        'exam': 'A quiet sign-in on the usual laptop and an extra check from a new country are adaptive identity. The permissions did not have to change. A second factor required every time is ordinary authentication. A log of the sign-in is accounting. A folder that also demands a healthy device and a work hour is a different rule about entry. Never raising the difficulty is not this control. The change in difficulty is the fact that separates it. Place, device, and behavior are the signals to expect.\n',
    },
    'Threat scope reduction': {
        'body': 'This design goal limits how far one compromise can travel. It does not claim you have fewer adversaries, and it does not claim the first device is safe. A guest laptop can still be lost. The win is that the lost laptop cannot open the payroll servers, because the path between them is gone.\n\nYou shrink reach with routes, accounts, and placement. Separate networks, deny the paths you do not need, and give each subject only the systems its job requires. A flat interior, where any address on the office wifi can talk to every server, is the opposite result. Adding a monitoring page does not shrink a path. The packets still arrive. A spare site in another city does not shrink a path either. Record the blocked destinations beside the change so a review has something concrete to inspect. It gives you somewhere to recover after the path has already been used.\n\nGuest VLAN 40 uses 10.40.0.0/16. Payroll VLAN 12 uses 10.12.0.0/16. The core switch ACL denies the guest range toward the payroll range, including host 10.12.4.20. When a guest laptop was infected, endpoint tooling on that laptop showed connection attempts toward the payroll host, and the switch log showed DENY for those attempts. Ticket IR-88 records both lines. Payroll jobs kept running. The infection was real, and the scope stopped at the ACL. A request the next week to allow any guest address to any server port would have undone the ticket.\n\nDefenders draw the paths they mean to keep, then deny the rest and test from the low-trust side. From a guest port, a permitted name lookup may work while payroll ports stay closed. Review rules that punch a hole for a vendor laptop, and put an end date on them. When an administrator account can reach every VLAN from a daily workstation, scope has grown again even if the guest ACL looks strict. Least privilege on accounts is part of the same goal as the network cut.\n\nStudents hear the phrase and think it means a smaller threat list, or they credit an alert with reducing scope because the team learned about the malware. Learning is discovery. Scope changes only when a later attempt can touch fewer systems. A backup does not do that. The separating fact is reach. After the change, name a subject and the systems it can no longer open, the way IR-88 names the guest laptop and the payroll host it failed to reach.\n',
        'r0': 'Threat scope reduction limits how far one foothold can reach. It does not reduce the count of adversaries, and it does not clean the first device. Guest 10.40.0.0/16 is denied toward payroll 10.12.0.0/16, so attempts from a compromised guest laptop to 10.12.4.20 show DENY on the core switch in IR-88. An alert about those attempts is discovery, not a smaller path. A hot spare in another city is recovery, not a smaller path. Test from the guest side and confirm payroll ports stay closed. Vendor any-any rules reopen scope unless they carry an end date. Admin accounts that can touch every VLAN widen scope again. The proof is a subject and the systems it can no longer open.\n',
        'r1': 'This work makes one broken machine less able to reach the rest. It does not erase the first break. It does not shrink the list of enemies. Separate networks are a common way to do it. A guest laptop should not open payroll. An alert about the laptop does not close that path. A spare building does not close that path either. Test from the weak side and see what still answers. A wide allow rule grows the reach again. Name what the broken machine can no longer touch.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse fire wall between a paint cage and the office file room. A flare in the cage can ruin the paint and still stop at the wall, so the files do not burn in the same minute. The wall does not prevent the flare, and it does not shorten the list of things that can spark. A smoke alarm tells you the cage is hot and leaves the doorway unchanged. A second warehouse across town saves the business later and does not stop this doorway. On the clinic network, the deny between the guest range and payroll is that wall. IR-88 is the scorch mark that failed to cross it.\n',
        'r4': 'Draw the path from guest VLAN 40 to payroll host 10.12.4.20. Confirm the core ACL denies 10.40.0.0/16 toward 10.12.0.0/16. From a guest port, check that payroll ports do not answer. Attach both the endpoint attempt and the DENY line to IR-88. Do not treat the alert alone as a smaller scope. Put an end date on any vendor rule that reopens the path. Stop when you can name the subject and the systems it cannot reach.\n',
        'exam': 'Smaller reach after one compromise is this goal. A shorter list of adversaries is not the same claim. An alert does not shrink a path. A backup or a spare site does not shrink a path. A flat office network is the failure mode. The proof is a subject that can no longer open a named system. An any-any exception undoes the work.\n',
    },
    'Policy-driven access': {
        'body': 'Entry should be granted by a written rule each time a request arrives. It should not rest on a standing belief that the inside of the network is safe. That rule-based grant is policy-driven access. Presence on the office wireless network is not a reason to open every folder. The user, the health of the device, and the hour can all be required. A miss on any one of them is a deny, even when the laptop is plugged in at a clinic desk.\n\nThe mechanism breaks the old habit of trusted zones. A rule is written in advance. When a request arrives, the relevant signals are compared with that rule. An allow means the signals matched. A deny means at least one required fact was missing or wrong. The part that weighs the signals is a policy engine. The part that actually opens or shuts the path is a policy enforcement point. The part that pushes the allow or the deny to that gate is a policy administrator. This label names the approach, not one of those parts. Access comes from the rule. An implicit trust zone, where inside means safe, is the design this approach refuses.\n\nThe clinic payroll folder makes the rule visible. The folder opens only when the user is on the payroll list, the device reports as healthy, and the hour falls between 08:00 and 18:00. Helen Nguyen on a healthy laptop at 10:00 is allowed. The same account on a healthy laptop at 22:14 is denied, even though that laptop sits on the office network. Ticket 6302 records the user, the health result, the hour, and the deny. A nursing station PC that fails the health check is denied at noon under the same rule. The inside of the network did not vouch for either machine. Each request was judged on its own facts.\n\nA defender tests the rule by changing one signal at a time. An allowed user on a healthy device during work hours should succeed. The same user after hours should fail. An unhealthy device at noon should fail. All three results belong on the ticket before anyone edits the rule. Fixing a deny by moving the PC onto a network that skips the checks rebuilds the assumption the rule was written to avoid. If the question asks which part decided, name the engine. If it asks which part blocked, name the gate. Mixing those names turns a clear design into a muddle.\n\nThe trap is to treat every access story as one slogan about trust. Adaptive identity changes how hard the sign-in is, and it does not by itself require a healthy device and a work hour for a folder. A firewall that drops traffic is a technical control. It may carry out a rule, and a stem about user, device health, and hour is still this approach. A signed page that adopts the rule is a managerial control. The grant condition is what separates them. When the inside network is not enough, and the rule is what opens the share, this is the label.\n',
        'r0': 'Policy-driven access grants a request from rules rather than from a belief that the inside network is safe. The payroll folder allows Helen Nguyen on a healthy device at 10:00 and denies the same healthy laptop at 22:14, which ticket 6302 records along with the health result. The window in the rule is 08:00 to 18:00, and a nursing PC that fails its health check is denied at noon. The policy engine weighs the signals. The policy enforcement point opens or shuts the path. The policy administrator pushes the decision to that gate. An implicit trust zone would have treated office wireless as enough. Adaptive identity changes sign-in difficulty and does not state this folder rule.\n',
        'r1': 'A rule should decide entry every time. Being on the network is not a free pass. User, device health, and hour can all matter. One miss among them should deny the folder. The decider and the door are different parts. Test one signal at a time. Try a healthy device during work hours. Try the same person late at night. Try an unhealthy device at noon. Write all three results before you edit the rule. Do not fix a deny by trusting the network. The grant condition is the fact to name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse parts window that stays shut until the worker, a current safety check on the cart, and the shift hour all match. Standing inside the building does not open the window by itself. The person who reads the list and decides is not the same person who slides the glass. A test that changes only the hour, and then only the cart, shows which fact failed. Waving the cart down a trusted aisle and skipping the checks brings back the old habit. In the clinic, the payroll folder that opens for a healthy device only during the allowed hours is that window. At night the same laptop is denied, because the inside network is not enough.\n',
        'r4': 'Test the payroll folder as Helen Nguyen on a healthy laptop at 10:00 and record the allow. Repeat the test at 22:14 and record the deny on ticket 6302. Repeat it at noon from a device that fails the health check. Write the user, the health result, and the hour beside each result. Do not edit the rule until those three results are stored. Do not clear the deny by trusting the network. If the stem asks who decided, name the engine, not the gate. Stop when the ticket shows the grant condition.\n',
        'exam': 'Entry granted only when the user, the device health, and the hour match is policy-driven access. Being on the office network is not enough. A harder sign-in from a new country is adaptive identity, not this folder rule. The engine decides, and the enforcement point blocks. Naming the wrong part is a common miss. A signed page that adopts the rule is a managerial control. A firewall drop can carry out a rule and is still a technical control. The grant condition is the fact that separates this approach.\n',
    },
    'Policy administrator': {
        'body': "In a zero trust design, the policy administrator is the component that carries a decision to the gate. The decision is allow, deny, or revoke. This component does not weigh the signals. It takes the result from the engine and tells the enforcement point to open a path, keep it closed, or tear down a path that should no longer exist.\n\nThe handoff is easy to skip in a diagram because the boxes sit close together. The engine thinks. The administrator acts on that thought by configuring the gate, often with a session-specific credential the subject must present. If the result is deny, the administrator instructs the gate not to complete the session. If a user who was allowed at noon should be cut off at 12:40, revocation has to travel the same path. A decision that stays inside the engine never becomes a closed session. A human job title with the same words, the person who edits the written policy, is a different role. Do not mix the staff job with this component when you read a stem.\n\nTicket ZT-441 follows subject j.patel asking for the mail application. The engine returned deny. The administrator log then shows decision_id 9f3c, action DENY, resource mail-app, sent onward to gateway gw-app-02. The gateway drop comes after that message. Priya's note records three timestamps in order: engine result, administrator message, gateway drop. That order is the mechanism. When a test last month showed an engine deny with no administrator line, the gateway still allowed the session, and the defect was in the handoff rather than in the rule.\n\nDefenders trace denied and revoked sessions across those three records. Clocks must match or the order becomes a guess. The console that can push decisions should be limited to the platform team, not granted to every application owner who wants a quick allow. Test revocation on a live lab session and confirm the gate tears it down, not merely blocks the next login. If the administrator is down, decide in advance whether paths fail closed. An open failure turns a dead messenger into a quiet allow.\n\nStudents point at the engine or at the gateway and use this name for both. The engine produces the result. The gateway moves. This component is the messenger and the path controller between them. The separating fact is the handoff you can log. A weighed decision means you are looking at the engine. A dropped packet means you are looking at the gate. The instruction that told the gate what to do is the piece that belongs here.\n",
        'r0': 'The policy administrator carries allow, deny, or revoke from the engine to the enforcement point and can establish or tear down the path. It does not weigh posture, time, or identity itself. In ZT-441, decision_id 9f3c action DENY for j.patel and mail-app was pushed to gateway gw-app-02, and the drop follows that push. An engine deny with no administrator line left the gate open in a prior test, which isolates the fault to the handoff. Revocation has to ride the same path or a noon allow survives all afternoon. This component is not the staff job that edits written policy. Limit the console that can push decisions. Prefer a fail-closed result if the messenger is down. Evidence is the message that told the gate what to do.\n',
        'r1': 'This part carries the yes or the no to the door. It does not make the decision itself. It does not stand in the doorway. Allow, deny, and revoke are the messages it can send. A cut-off must travel the same path as a new request. A decision that never leaves the thinker changes nothing. The person who edits a written rule is a different role. Trace the message before you blame the door. If the messenger is down, decide whether paths stay shut. Point at the handoff when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a hotel desk at night. The manager in the back office decides whether a guest may go up. The clerk at the desk receives that decision and tells the door staff to let the guest pass or to keep the door shut. The clerk is not the one who weighed the reservation, and the clerk is not the door itself. A decision that stays in the back office never moves the door. A guest who was allowed at dinner can be stopped only if the clerk passes a later stop order. In the mail-app path, the administrator log for decision 9f3c is that clerk. The gateway is the door staff who act on what the clerk passed along.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the engine result, the administrator message, and the gateway result for ZT-441. Confirm decision_id 9f3c says DENY before gw-app-02 drops the session. Order the three timestamps before you name the failing part. If the engine denied and the gate allowed, look for a missing handoff. Do not treat the written-policy editor as this component. Test a revocation and confirm a live session actually ends. Stop when the ticket shows the message that instructed the gate.\n',
        'exam': 'This component delivers the decision to the gate. The engine weighs the signals, so a reasoned allow is not this component. The gateway drops or opens the session, so the drop alone is not this component. A human editor of written policy is not this component. Revoke is part of the job, not only the first allow. A missing handoff explains a deny that never took effect. Fail closed if the messenger is down, or the outage becomes an allow.\n',
    },
    'Policy engine': {
        'body': "The policy engine is the part that decides. It takes signals about the asker and the request, compares them with the rules you loaded, and returns allow or deny. It does not stand in the doorway, and it does not push the result to the gate. Another component carries the result. If you only have a dropped session, you have not yet shown the decision.\n\nSignals are the raw material. Identity, group membership, device posture, time of day, and the sensitivity of the system can all matter, but only the signals your rules actually read will change the result. A healthy laptop at a normal hour can be allowed for mail and denied for payroll if the rules say so. The same person on a laptop that failed its health check can be denied for mail a minute later. The engine should record the inputs it used. A bare allow with no inputs cannot be explained when the user appeals.\n\nAt 14:10 on ticket ZT-442 the engine evaluated j.patel for the mail application. Device posture was compliant, the account was in mail-users, and the local hour sat inside the clinic window. The log line reads decision ALLOW, reason posture_ok and time_window, decision_id 4ab1. The gateway configuration was not edited by that evaluation. When the lab repeated the request with posture failed, the same account received DENY, reason posture_fail, decision_id 4ab2. Two results from one engine, differing by one signal, are the clearest way to teach the mechanism.\n\nDefenders version the rule set and test it with known subjects before it faces the clinic. Store those rules apart from the gateway configuration so a late tweak on the gate does not silently rewrite the decision. Sample real decisions each week and check that the logged inputs still match the rule you think is running. If the engine is unreachable, the design should fail closed for new sessions rather than let every request through. An allow that cannot be tied to a rule version is a finding, not a success.\n\nStudents see a gateway drop and name the engine, because the drop is the visible event. The drop is enforcement. Students also name the engine when they see the message that told the gate what to do, and that message is the administrator's handoff. A hand-written access list living only on a firewall can be a gate's local rule, and it is not this component unless something is weighing live signals into a decision. The separating fact is the weighing. Point at the inputs and the allow or deny they produced, as 4ab1 and 4ab2 do, and you are looking at the engine.\n",
        'r0': 'The policy engine weighs signals against rules and returns allow or deny. It does not configure the gateway and does not drop the session itself. On ZT-442 at 14:10, j.patel with compliant posture and membership in mail-users received ALLOW decision_id 4ab1, reason posture_ok and time_window. The same account with failed posture received DENY decision_id 4ab2. Log the inputs or an appeal cannot be checked. Keep the rule store separate from gateway configuration. Version the rules and test known subjects before clinic traffic hits them. If the engine cannot be reached, fail closed on new sessions. A firewall list with no live signals is not this component. Evidence is the weighed result, not the later drop.\n',
        'r1': 'This part thinks about the request. It returns yes or no. It does not stand at the door. It does not carry the answer to the door. A healthy device and a normal hour can lead to yes. A failed health check can lead to no for the same person. Write down the facts that produced the answer. A drop you can see is a different part. A message sent to the door is a different part. Keep the rules somewhere you can version. If this part is down, new requests should wait. Point at the weighing when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic engineer in a small room of gauges. The engineer reads pressure, time of day, and which wing is asking for heat, then writes yes or no on a slip. Someone else walks the slip to the valve, and the valve is what actually opens. The engineer does not turn the valve by hand. Two slips for the same wing, one yes and one no, can differ because a single gauge changed. A valve that slams shut is not proof you understand the gauges. In the mail request, decision 4ab1 is the slip that said yes while the laptop was healthy. Decision 4ab2 is the slip that said no after that health reading changed.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ZT-442 and copy decision_id 4ab1 with its inputs. Copy decision_id 4ab2 and confirm only posture changed. Do not edit the gateway while you are only checking the decision. Keep the rule version beside the two results. Separate the engine line from the later gateway drop. Separate the engine line from the handoff message. Stop when the ticket shows inputs, a result, and the rule version that produced them.\n',
        'exam': 'The weighed allow or deny is this component. A gateway drop is the enforcement point, not the engine. The message that tells the gate what to do is the administrator, not the engine. A static firewall list with no live signals is not the engine. Log inputs or the result cannot be explained. Fail closed when the engine cannot be reached. Two decisions that differ by one signal are the stem that proves the weighing.\n',
    },
    'Implicit trust zones': {
        'body': 'An implicit trust zone is an area of the network that still treats inside as safe enough. Once a laptop is on the office wireless network, applications accept it because of where it sits, with no fresh look at the person, the device health, or the request. Zero trust is the refusal of that habit. The design goal is to make these zones smaller, not to rename the old interior and keep the habit.\n\nThe mechanism is an assumption hiding in placement. Addresses on the office subnet are allowed to file servers, printers, and admin pages because a firewall at the internet edge already did its job. That edge does not see a stolen laptop that is already associated. Malware on a clinic PC inherits every permission the subnet was given. A password stolen from one desk becomes a key to the whole interior. The zone can be tiny, such as a single outdated application that trusts every caller behind one connector, or it can be the entire building LAN. Size is a finding. The label applies whenever location is doing the work of a decision.\n\nCORP-WIFI drops clients onto 10.1.0.0/16. File server fs-01 allows SMB from that whole subnet and does not ask for a device posture check. Ticket ARCH-12 calls the result a flat trust behind the firewall. In a test, a laptop on that wireless network opened a finance share with only a stolen password. No second question appeared. The same laptop on guest wireless could not open the share, which shows the trust was tied to the interior address, not to a rule about the user. That contrast belongs in the ticket next to the firewall diagram that still draws one safe inside.\n\nDefenders inventory every app that allows a source subnet and nothing else. Put an enforcement point in front of the sensitive ones so network membership is not enough. Split guest, clinical, and server networks so a hallway laptop is not already inside the payroll zone. Do not accept a diagram that shows a firewall on the edge and a cloud with the word trusted across the building. When a team claims zero trust because staff use a second factor at the VPN, ask what happens after the VPN address is assigned. If the answer is full interior access, the zone is still there.\n\nStudents call the office LAN a zero trust network because an internet firewall exists. The firewall is the old perimeter. The implicit zone is what remains behind it. A screened subnet for public servers is a placement pattern, and it is not this label unless the stem is about undeserved trust. A two-door lobby is a physical room, not a network assumption. The separating fact is the assumption itself. If being inside is treated as permission, you are looking at this zone, and zero trust has not arrived yet.\n',
        'r0': 'An implicit trust zone treats interior placement as permission, which zero trust tries to shrink rather than celebrate. CORP-WIFI puts clients on 10.1.0.0/16, and fs-01 allows SMB from that subnet with no posture check. ARCH-12 records a test laptop that opened a finance share with only a stolen password. The same laptop failed from guest wireless, so the trust was the address. An edge firewall does not remove the zone behind it. A VPN second factor does not remove it if the VPN address then reaches everything. Inventory apps that allow a subnet and nothing else. A screened subnet is a different pattern. Evidence is an allow based on location alone.\n',
        'r1': 'This zone treats the inside of the network as safe. Being on office wireless becomes a free pass. Apps stop asking who is calling. A stolen laptop inside inherits that pass. A firewall at the internet edge does not fix the inside. A remote login prompt may still leave the inside wide. Smaller zones are the goal. A guest network should not count as the inside. Look for a rule that trusts an address and nothing more. Zero trust keeps asking after you are inside.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a fairground where a wristband opens every tent, including the cash office, once you are past the front gate. The gate was real. The mistake is the band that means the same thing in every tent. A guest who never received a band cannot enter the cash office, which shows the band, not a fresh question, is what the tents trust. A guard who checks a list at the cash door is a different design. A turnstile far away does not change the band. On the clinic LAN, an address on the office wireless network is that wristband. ARCH-12 is the moment a test laptop cashed in the band with a stolen password and no second question.\n',
        'r4': 'Map which applications accept 10.1.0.0/16 with no posture check. Include fs-01 SMB in that list for ARCH-12. Repeat a test from CORP-WIFI and from guest wireless and save both results. Do not call the edge firewall a fix for the interior allow. Ask what a VPN address can reach after login. Separate this finding from a two-door lobby control. Stop when the ticket shows an allow that rests on location alone.\n',
        'exam': 'Inside-means-safe is this zone. An edge firewall does not erase it. A VPN prompt does not erase it if the session then reaches the whole interior. Zero trust is the effort to shrink the zone, not a new name for the same LAN. A screened subnet is not automatically this label. A physical two-door room is not this label. The stem to recognize is an app that accepts the office subnet and asks nothing further.\n',
    },
    'Subjects and systems': {
        'body': 'Access rules need two names. The subject is the person or the service that is asking. The system is the thing being asked. A sentence that names only one of them cannot be enforced, because the gate does not know which pair is allowed. Human users are subjects, and so are backup jobs, scanners, and deployment pipelines. A database does not become a subject merely because it is important. It is a system when something is asking it for data.\n\nThe mechanism is identity on both ends of the request. The subject presents an account, a certificate, or a workload identity. The system is a named application, host, or data store, not a whole subnet when the data is sensitive. The engine can weigh a request only after both identities are known. A rule that says the network may back up names neither side. A rule that says authenticated users may use internal systems names both sides so broadly that it is barely a rule. Non-human subjects are where teams get careless. The job runs every night, the password is shared, and nobody can say which host is allowed to be that subject.\n\nRule ZT-014 names subject svc-backup-night and system payroll-db-01. The subject may connect at 01:15 from host bk-01 only, on TCP 5432. Ticket ZT-014-R records a night when the job ran from new host bk-09. The engine denied it because the subject was bound to bk-01, not because the password was wrong. That deny is the control working. Widening the rule to any backup host would erase the binding that made the deny possible. The payroll database stays the system in both the success and the deny. The backup job stays the subject even though no person sat at a keyboard.\n\nDefenders inventory service accounts and tie each one to an owner and a host or certificate. Retire subjects whose owner has left. When a scanner must reach many systems, list those systems instead of writing all internal. Review rules quarterly for the phrase authenticated users on one side and a subnet on the other. Alert when a known subject appears from a host it was not bound to, as bk-09 did. Do not solve a deny by handing the service account to every server team.\n\nStudents treat only people as subjects, so a service-account misuse looks like a mysterious process rather than an access decision. Students also swap the words and call the database a subject because the incident is about payroll. The asker is the subject. The thing asked is the system. An interior zone that trusts every address skips this naming and lets location stand in for both. The separating fact is the pair. A complete rule, like ZT-014, names who is asking and what they are allowed to ask.\n',
        'r0': 'A subject is the person or service asking, and a system is the resource being asked. Both identities belong in the rule. ZT-014 allows subject svc-backup-night to reach system payroll-db-01 at 01:15 from host bk-01 only, on TCP 5432. When the job ran from bk-09, the engine denied it in ZT-014-R even though the secret was correct. Any-backup would have removed that deny. Authenticated-users to all-internal is barely a rule. Service accounts, scanners, and pipelines are subjects. The database is the system, not the subject. Bind each non-human subject to an owner and a host or certificate. Evidence is the named pair, plus the host binding that made the unexpected source fail.\n',
        'r1': 'Name the one who asks and the thing they ask. A person can be the one who asks. A backup job can be the one who asks. The payroll data store is the thing being asked. A rule that names only the network is unfinished. A service account is not a person. It is still an asker. Bind that asker to the right machine. A deny from the wrong machine can be the rule working. Do not call the data store the asker just because it matters. Write both names in the rule.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic appointment slip with two lines. One line names the nurse who is asking for a room. The other line names the room. A slip that says someone needs a room cannot be honored, because the desk does not know which pair is intended. A slip that says any nurse may use any room is barely a rule at all. The medication cabinet is not the nurse, even when the visit is about medication. A backup of that cabinet's inventory is still an asker if it requests the record. On the real rule, the night backup job is the nurse line and the payroll store is the room line. The deny from the new host is a slip that named the right nurse and the wrong doorway.\n",
        'r4': 'Read ZT-014 and write down the subject and the system as two separate names. Confirm svc-backup-night is bound to bk-01 and TCP 5432 at 01:15. On ZT-014-R, record that bk-09 was denied. Do not widen the rule to any host to make the job pass. List an owner for the service account. Separate this pair from a rule that only names a subnet. Stop when the ticket shows who asked, what they asked, and which host was allowed to ask.\n',
        'exam': 'The asker is the subject, and the resource is the system. Service accounts are subjects. A database is a system when it is being asked. A rule that names only one side is incomplete. A deny for the right secret from the wrong host can be success. Trusting a whole subnet skips this naming. Do not swap the words because the data feels more important than the job.\n',
    },
    'Policy enforcement point': {
        'body': 'The policy enforcement point is the component that actually opens or closes the session. It is the gate that moves. A decision sitting in a log does not stop a user. A message that nobody enforces does not stop a user. This component enables the connection, watches it, and can terminate it when the decision changes.\n\nIt sits in front of the system being asked. Common forms are an application gateway, a proxy, or an agent on the host that will not let the application listen except through the controlled path. The enforcement point does not invent allow or deny. It receives that result from the policy administrator, which received it from the engine. A direct route that reaches the server and skips the gateway is a bypass, and the control is then only a diagram. The gate should be able to end a session that is already open, not merely refuse the next login, because revoke is part of the design.\n\nOn ticket ZT-443, subject j.patel requested mail. Gateway gw-app-02 logged session 88af, action DROP, reason PA_DENY, on port 443. The user saw the application fail. The engine had returned DENY a moment earlier, and the administrator had forwarded that result. Priya saved the gateway line next to those two earlier lines. The gateway line is the enforcement evidence. In a second test, an engineer reached the mail host on a management address that skipped gw-app-02, and the session stayed up despite the same deny. That bypass is a broken gate, not a broken decision.\n\nDefenders place an enforcement point on every sensitive application and test the drop from a normal client. Alert on traffic that reaches the server subnet while avoiding the gateway. Align the gateway clock with the engine so the three lines can be ordered. Do not store the only copy of the business rules on the gateway and then call the gateway the engine. The gateway may cache a result briefly. It should not become a second, unreviewed policy source. When the gate is down, fail closed so an outage is not a wide open door.\n\nStudents name the engine when they see a drop, because they remember that something decided. The drop is this component. Students name this component when they only have a reasoned ALLOW in the engine log, and that log is the decision, not the moving gate. A human at a two-door lobby is a physical control, and the stem wants this name when the story is a session being cut. The separating fact is the component that opened or closed the path. Session 88af on gw-app-02 is that component in action.\n',
        'r0': 'The policy enforcement point opens, watches, or terminates the session. It does not weigh the signals. On ZT-443, gw-app-02 dropped session 88af for j.patel on port 443 with reason PA_DENY, after the engine deny was forwarded. A management address that skipped the gateway left the session up, which is a bypass. Revoke must end a live session, not only the next login. Agents and proxies count when they are the component that moves. Failing closed matters when the gate itself is down. Do not relocate the rule logic onto the gateway and rename it the engine. Evidence is the drop or the open, tied to the decision id that caused it.\n',
        'r1': 'This is the door that actually moves. A written yes does nothing until the door moves. A written no does nothing until the door moves. The door does not decide the question. It follows the answer it was given. It should be able to shut a session already open. A side path around the door makes the door a picture. If the door is broken, stay shut rather than swing wide. A person at a lobby is a different kind of door. Point at the drop or the open when you use this name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture the gate at a clinic loading dock. A clerk inside has already decided whether the truck may enter, and a runner has carried that answer to the gatehouse. The arm of the gate is what rises or stays down. The arm does not read the purchase order. If drivers learn a side lane with no arm, the gatehouse becomes scenery. An arm that cannot lower again cannot undo a truck that was waved in at dawn. In the mail path, gw-app-02 is that arm. Session 88af staying down is the arm obeying a no, and the management address that skipped it is the side lane.\n',
        'r4': 'Collect the gateway line for session 88af on ZT-443. Confirm it says DROP with reason PA_DENY on port 443. Place it after the engine result and the handoff in time order. Test whether a management address can skip gw-app-02. Do not rewrite gateway config while you are only gathering the drop. Note whether a live session can be ended, not only a new login. Stop when the ticket shows the component that opened or closed the path.\n',
        'exam': 'The moving gate is this component. The weighed decision is the engine, not the gate. The handoff message is the administrator, not the gate. A bypass path means the gate is not actually in front of the system. Ending a live session is part of enforcement. A lobby guard is a physical control, not this session component. Fail closed when the gate is down. The stem to recognize is a dropped or opened session tied to a decision.\n',
    },
    'Bollards': {
        'body': 'Short steel posts along a curb or in front of a storefront have one job: stop a vehicle before it reaches a door. The name for those posts is bollards. A person on foot can usually step between them, and that gap is part of the layout, not a broken lock. A fence around a yard delays a climb and shows a boundary. A vestibule is a double door, a small room where the first door closes before the second opens. Neither of those controls takes the hit from a van. Bollards stop the vehicle. When the threat is a car, a truck, or any rolling mass, these posts are the control that belongs in the answer.\n\nThe mechanism is placement and strength. Each post sits in a solid footing so the mass of a vehicle meets steel instead of glass. The line removes the straight path a car would need while leaving room for people. If a delivery crew lifts a post out to save a few steps, the vehicle path is open until that post is set again. The post does not ask who is driving, and it does not record a face. It only interrupts the vehicle. Cameras and badges can sit nearby, but they are other controls with other jobs.\n\nDana Cole in facilities looks after the posts in the clinic drop-off lane, in front of the pharmacy door. Last month a stolen van left the lane at 01:40. The north post took the bumper. The pharmacy glass stayed whole. Work order 118 lists the dent, the concrete footing, and the camera clip that shows the van stopped short of the door. Those three facts, the order number, the dent, and the time, are what you would hold up if someone asked whether the posts did their job.\n\nA defender keeps the line continuous and treats a gap as an open vehicle path. After a strike, photograph the post and the glass before anyone swaps the steel. Write the work order number and the clock time on the ticket. Check the next morning that a post moved for a delivery is back in its footing. Use the camera when you need the driver, because the steel will not name a person. Do not take the posts out because the sidewalk feels tight. Do not describe a bent post as a failure if it stopped the van and the door is intact.\n\nStudents mix this control up with fencing, with a guard, and with a vestibule. Fencing delays someone on foot and works best when a light and a watch are there too. A guard can challenge a person and walk them to a room. A vestibule lets one person through two doors. The separating fact is the thing being stopped. If a vehicle could have reached a door or a storefront, and posts are what stopped it, this is the control. A person walking between the posts does not turn them into a failed lock for people.\n',
        'r0': 'Steel posts set in a vehicle path stop cars, vans, and trucks before they reach a door or a storefront. At the clinic pharmacy entrance the north post stopped a van at 01:40. Work order 118 records the dent, the concrete footing, and a post height of about one meter. A walking gap between posts is expected, because people are not the mass these posts are built to catch. Fencing would only slow a climber at a yard. A vestibule would cycle two doors for one person at a time. Keep every post seated after a delivery. A camera can show the driver, and the post itself will not. Choose this control when the stem shows a vehicle that could not reach the glass.\n',
        'r1': 'Short posts stand in the drive. They stop a car before the door. A person can still walk between them. That gap is normal. A fence only slows someone on foot. A vestibule is two doors for people. Posts do not do either of those jobs. Look for steel at the curb. A dent means a vehicle hit. Leave the posts in place. Do not pull them out for a cart. Put a moved post back the same day. The job is stopping the vehicle.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a hospital lane where short concrete posts stand between the cars and the glass doors. A van that leaves the lane meets the posts and stops. People with bags still walk through the gaps. A low rope at the garden edge is a different object, because it only marks a line and sways if a bumper noses it. A pair of lobby doors that let one person through at a time is different again. Those posts in the lane are the same idea as bollards at a clinic door. They are there to stop a vehicle, and the walking gaps do not change that job.\n',
        'r4': 'Photograph the dent on the north post and the intact pharmacy glass. Record work order 118 and the camera time 01:40. Walk the drop-off lane and note any post that is missing or leaning. Write that the barrier is there to stop a vehicle. Do not pull a post out for a delivery while the notes are unfinished. Do not call the posts a fence or a vestibule in the write-up. Stop once the path, the dent, and the time are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Students call a line of posts a fence, but a fence only delays a person on foot and shows a boundary. They also pick a vestibule, which is a double door where one door closes before the other opens. A camera that filmed the van did not stop it. If the stem is a vehicle aimed at a door or a storefront, the posts are the control. Walking between the posts is normal and is not the failure. The miss is treating a car barrier as a lock for people.\n',
    },
    'Access-control vestibule': {
        'body': "An access-control vestibule is a small room with two doors that cannot be open at the same time. The outer door must close before the inner door will unlock. Older exam wording calls the same room a mantrap. The names are not opposites. A tailgater who follows a badged person through the first door is stuck in the room, and the office stays closed until the desk acts or the second person presents a badge of their own.\n\nThe mechanism is an interlock wired between the two strikes. The controller watches door position, not just badge events. If the outer door is still ajar, the inner strike stays locked even for a valid badge. That is stricter than a single reader, where a second person can walk through on the first person's access if they stay close. The room needs to be small enough that a crowd cannot pack in, and visible enough that reception can see who is waiting. Video of the room supports a later argument about who was inside. The interlock is the preventive part. The camera is a record after the fact.\n\nClinic lobby doors D1 and D2 form that room. At 08:14 the badge of A. Singh opened D1. A second person had no badge. The controller log recorded interlock hold on D2, and the inner strike did not release. Reception left the inner door locked until the visitor signed the book and a host came down. Ticket PHY-61 holds the badge event, the interlock line, and a still from the camera showing both people in the room with D2 shut. A single door at the same lobby, tested the year before, had let a follower through because nothing forced the first door to close first.\n\nDefenders test the interlock on a schedule, including a power cut, and write down whether each door fails locked or unlocked. A fail-unlocked inner door during a power cut undoes the room for the length of the outage. Do not prop either door with a wedge, and treat a wedge as a broken control rather than as a convenience. Pair the hardware with a person who can see the room, because the strikes cannot tell a planned escort from a stranger without a procedure. Keep the release of the inner door as its own logged action.\n\nStudents call any badge door by this name. One reader on one leaf is not a vestibule. A turnstile is a single lane, not two interlocking doors. A lobby whose two doors can stand open together is only a lobby. Students who think mantrap and vestibule are different designs are splitting one control into two. The separating fact is the interlock. If the first door must shut before the second opens, and PHY-61 can show a follower held between them, you are looking at this room.\n",
        'r0': "An access-control vestibule is a two-door interlock: the outer door closes before the inner door unlocks. Mantrap is the older name for the same room. At 08:14, A. Singh's badge opened D1, a second person had no badge, and the controller logged interlock hold on D2. PHY-61 keeps that line, the badge event, and a camera still of both people inside. A single reader does not force the first leaf shut, so a close follower can pass. Test power loss and record fail-locked or fail-unlocked for each door. A wedge in either leaf defeats the interlock. Reception release of the inner door should be its own log event. Evidence is the hold that stopped the second person.\n",
        'r1': 'This room has two doors, and only one opens at a time. The first door must shut before the second unlocks. A person who follows a badge can get stuck inside. The office stays closed until someone acts. One badge door is not this room. A spinning gate is not this room. Two doors that can open together are just a lobby. The older name mantrap means this same room. Test what the doors do when the power fails. Do not prop either door open.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clean-room entry where you step into a tiny chamber and the door behind you has to latch before the door ahead will release. You cannot wave a friend through on your heels, because the second leaf never opens while the first is ajar. A single locked door down the hall does not create that pause. A wide lobby with both leaves often open does not create it either. The chamber is the control, not the sign that asks people to badge in. At the clinic, D1 and D2 are that chamber. The hold on D2 at 08:14 is the second leaf refusing to open while a follower stood inside.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the 08:14 badge event for A. Singh on D1. Pull the interlock hold for D2 and attach both to PHY-61. Save the camera still that shows the second person inside with D2 shut. Do not call a one-leaf reader this room. Record fail-locked or fail-unlocked for each door after a power test. Note any wedge as a broken interlock. Stop when the ticket shows the first door closing before the second can open.\n',
        'exam': 'Two interlocking doors are this control. Mantrap is the older name, not a rival design. A single badge door is not it. A turnstile is not it. Two doors that can be open together are a lobby. The follower stuck between the doors is the stem to recognize. Fail-unlocked on power loss is a failure mode. A camera records the room and does not replace the interlock.\n',
    },
    'Fencing': {
        'body': 'A tall barrier of mesh or rails around a yard, a roof, or a lot is there to mark a boundary and to slow someone who tries to cross it. That barrier is fencing. It is not a vault, and it is not a row of posts built to catch a car. A casual climb should be obvious to anyone watching, especially when a lamp washes the line and a person is assigned to look. Bollards stop a vehicle at a door. A vestibule is a double door for people. Fencing delays entry. The delay is the point, and the boundary is how you show where the protected ground starts.\n\nThe mechanism is time and visibility. A climber has to reach, cut, or go over, and each of those moves takes longer than a step across an open lot. While that happens, a camera and a watch have a chance to notice. Lighting belongs with the fence because a dark line hides the climb the fence was meant to expose. A determined person can still get over a fence. The control has not failed its definition when it fails to be a wall. It has failed when the cut sits unseen until morning, or when the fence is so low that a step-over looks like nothing.\n\nPriya Okonkwo walks the generator yard at 06:10. The fence there is tall enough that a casual climb is obvious on the yard camera. She finds a cut panel on the east side. The clip at 01:22 shows a person at that panel, the yard lamp is on, and the generator door is still shut when the guard arrives. Ticket 227 holds the clip time, the cut panel, and the note that the enclosure was not opened. The fence did not make entry impossible. It made the attempt visible and slow enough that the generator stayed closed.\n\nA defender walks the line, repairs cuts, and keeps the lamp and the watch in the same design. Write down a leaning section or a gap at a gate before you treat the yard as closed. Do not park a bin against the mesh so a person can step up out of the camera view. Do not swap this control for bollards in the notes when the threat is a person on foot. If the stem is a car aimed at a storefront, the fence is the wrong tool. Pair the fence with light and with someone who will look, or the delay happens in the dark and helps no one.\n\nThe trap is to treat the fence as a vehicle stop, as a locked room, or as a camera. Posts in a drive stop a van. A vestibule cycles two doors and does not circle a yard. A camera remembers a climb and does not by itself slow the climber. The separating fact is delay plus a visible boundary. If the stem shows a yard, a roof, or a lot where a person would be slowed and seen, fencing is the control, and it still wants light and a watch.\n',
        'r0': 'Fencing marks a yard, a roof, or a lot and delays a person who tries to cross. The generator yard fence is tall enough that a casual climb is obvious on camera. At 01:22 the yard camera shows a person at a cut on the east panel, the lamp is on, and the generator door is still shut at 06:10 when Priya Okonkwo finishes the walk. Ticket 227 should hold those times. Bollards would be the tool if a van were aimed at a door. A fence does not replace that vehicle stop. Keep lighting and a watch with the fence, or the delay is invisible. Choose fencing when the stem is about slowing and showing entry, not about catching a car.\n',
        'r1': 'A fence shows where the yard starts. It slows a person who tries to cross. It does not stop a car. Posts in the drive do that job. A dark fence hides the climb. A lamp and a watcher belong with it. A cut should be easy to see. Fix the cut and write the time. Do not call the fence a locked room. A determined person can still get over. The win is time and a clear view. Walk the line in the morning.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse lot with a tall mesh wall around the generator shed. A stranger who wants the shed has to climb or cut, and that motion shows against the sky if the lot lamp is lit. A night watch can see the shape before the shed door opens. Short posts by the public road are a different object, because they are there for cars, not for climbers. A watcher who never looks is not part of the picture yet. In the real yard, that mesh wall is fencing. It delays the entry and shows the boundary, and it still needs light and someone watching.\n',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 227 and attach the 01:22 clip of the east panel. Note that the yard lamp was on and the generator door was still shut at 06:10. Write the cut location before anyone repairs the mesh. Do not park equipment against the fence while you are still documenting the gap. Do not describe the fence as a vehicle stop. Stop when the clip time, the cut, and the closed generator door are all on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A fence that delays a climber is not a row of posts that stop a van. A vestibule is a double door for people, not a boundary around a yard. A camera can show the climb and still is not the fence. Lighting helps the fence and is not the fence. If the person got over after a visible delay, do not say the control had no job. The miss is calling fencing a sealed room, or calling it a car barrier. Delay and a seen boundary are the facts that separate it.\n',
    },
    'Video surveillance': {
        'body': 'A camera that records or watches a space lets you see what happened there, and sometimes who did it. That practice is video surveillance. The camera remembers the scene. It does not lock the door, it does not ask a visitor who they came to see, and it does not feel weight or heat. If nobody looks at the picture, and nobody stores it, the lens is only glass on a wall. The value shows up later, or on a live view, as a record you can compare with other facts such as a badge log.\n\nThe mechanism is light, a lens, a recorder, and a clock. The camera needs enough light to form a picture a person can use. The recorder keeps the pictures, and the clock lets you line them up with a door event. A wide view of a dock can show a prop in a door even when the badge reader never saw a card. A hidden camera may still record, but it does not warn a person the way a visible one might. Either way, the recording is a memory. Stopping the act would take a lock, a guard, or some other control that acts on the person.\n\nElena reviews the loading dock recorder after a morning report of a door left ajar. Camera two shows a person propping that door at 02:10. The badge log for the same door has no grant and no deny in that minute. Elena writes ticket 314 with the camera name, the time 02:10, and the note that the badge log missed the prop. The system in front of her is the recorder, not the reader. The specific thing she can point to is that clip, with a wedge in the door and an empty badge minute beside it.\n\nA defender keeps clocks aligned, keeps the recording long enough to answer a question, and actually looks when a door, an alarm, or a guard report says something happened. Compare the picture with the badge log instead of trusting either one alone. Replace a dead lamp if the frame is black, because a camera in the dark is not doing this job. Do not tell a manager the camera locked the door. Do not skip the 02:10 clip because the badge log looks quiet. Write the time and the camera name on the ticket before anyone rewinds over the file.\n\nStudents treat the camera as a guard or as a lock. A guard can challenge, escort, and judge intent. A lock can hold the door shut. A badge identifies a token at the reader and can miss a person who never presents one. The separating fact is memory of the scene. If the stem shows a picture of what already happened, including a prop the badge log missed, this is the control. If the stem shows someone stopping the person in the moment, pick the lock or the guard instead.\n',
        'r0': 'Video surveillance stores or shows a scene so a person can see what happened and sometimes who was there. On the loading dock, camera two shows a door propped at 02:10. The badge log for that door has no grant and no deny in the same minute. Ticket 314 should cite the camera, the time, and the empty badge minute. The recorder is the system, and the clip is the evidence. A guard could have challenged the person, and a lock could have held the door. The camera did neither. Keep the recorder clock aligned with the door clock. A black frame means the lamp or the lens failed before any review can help.\n',
        'r1': 'A camera remembers what happened in a space. It can show who was there. It does not lock the door. It does not ask why someone came. A badge log can miss a propped door. The picture may still show the wedge. Someone has to watch or store the video. A dark frame helps no one. Match the picture to the clock. Write the time before you move on. Do not say the camera stopped the person. A guard would have to be standing there.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic loading room with a camera over the side door. Later, a clerk plays the night back and sees a wedge in the frame. The door itself did nothing new while the clerk watched. A person at the desk who could have walked over is a different kind of help. A bolt that stays thrown is different again. The clerk can pause the picture, point at the wedge, and still cannot claim the door was held shut by the lens. On the real dock, the camera is that memory of the wedge. It shows what happened, and it does not hold the door shut.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the dock recorder and find the frame at 02:10. Write camera two, the propped door, and the empty badge minute on ticket 314. Confirm the recorder clock matches the badge clock before you compare them. Save the clip somewhere the ticket can point to. Do not delete the night to free space while the review is open. Do not write that the camera locked the door. Stop when the time, the picture, and the badge gap are all noted.\n',
        'exam': 'A clip of a propped door is not a lock, and it is not a guard who can challenge someone. A quiet badge minute does not erase the picture. A lamp that makes the dock visible is lighting, not the recording itself. If the stem shows what already happened on camera, choose the recording. If the stem shows a person stopped at the door, choose the control that stopped them. The miss is saying the camera kept the door shut because a picture of the prop exists.\n',
    },
    'Security guards': {
        'body': 'A person posted to watch, question, and act can do something a sensor and a camera cannot: judge intent and respond. That person is a security guard. The guard can challenge a visitor, walk with them, and stay until the right host appears. A camera can show the lobby later. A badge can open a door for a token. Neither one asks who the visitor is here to see, and neither one escorts. When the stem turns on a human decision in the moment, the guard is the control.\n\nThe mechanism is presence plus authority to intervene. The guard sees a person, asks a question, and chooses to allow, to escort, or to refuse. Post orders tell the guard which doors, which hours, and which questions matter. A radio or a desk log lets the next shift see what was decided. A guard who never looks up is furniture. A guard who leaves the desk to run an errand has opened a gap no camera fills while the chair is empty. The control lives in the person who can still act after a sensor has done all it can.\n\nElena is the morning guard at the clinic lobby. Luis Patel arrives as a visitor. Elena walks with him to the conference room and waits. Host Dana Cole comes down at 09:17. The desk log reads visitor Luis Patel, host Dana Cole, escort started 09:12, host arrived 09:17. The system is that log and the post Elena holds. The specific line is the pair of times. A door sensor could say the lobby door opened. It could not decide that Luis should wait for Dana, and it could not walk him there.\n\nA defender writes post orders the guard can actually follow, keeps the log filled, and treats an empty post as a down control. Review a sample of escort lines to see that hosts arrive and visitors are not left loose. Do not replace the guard with a camera in the write-up when the fact you need is a challenge or an escort. Do not ignore a log that shows the guard left the desk during the visit window. If a sensor alarms, the guard is who can go look and decide what the alarm means.\n\nStudents call a camera a guard, or they call a badge a guard. A camera remembers and does not judge. A badge identifies a token to a reader and does not walk anyone to a room. A sensor can notice heat, weight, radio motion, or sound, and it still cannot ask a question. The separating fact is a person who can challenge, escort, and respond. If the stem shows someone asking who a visitor is here for, and staying until the host arrives, the guard is the answer.\n',
        'r0': 'A security guard is a person who can challenge, escort, and respond when a camera or a sensor cannot judge intent. Elena escorts visitor Luis Patel starting at 09:12 and waits until host Dana Cole arrives at 09:17. The desk log is the record: visitor, host, and those two times. A badge might have opened a door without that escort. A camera might show the lobby later and still never ask the question. Post orders define which questions Elena must ask. An empty chair during that window means the control was absent. Choose the guard when the stem turns on a human decision and a response.\n',
        'r1': 'A guard is a person who can ask questions. A guard can walk with a visitor. A guard can wait for the host. A camera only remembers the room. A badge only opens for a card. A sensor cannot judge intent. Write the visitor and the host. Write when the escort started. Write when the host arrived. An empty desk is a gap. Do not call a camera a guard. The person who responds is the control. Stay at the post until the host arrives.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse receiving desk where a clerk looks up, asks which office a driver wants, and walks the driver there. The clerk waits until someone from that office comes to the door. A lens over the desk can show the walk later. It cannot decide the driver should wait. A card that opens the gate cannot make the introduction. The clerk uses judgment the machines in the lobby do not have, and that judgment is the whole point of posting a person there. On the real lobby, that clerk is the guard. The escort and the wait are the job, not the picture and not the card.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the lobby log for Luis Patel and Dana Cole. Write 09:12 for the escort start and 09:17 for the host arrival on the ticket. Confirm Elena stayed until the host arrived. Check the post orders for the question the guard is supposed to ask. Do not close the note by calling the lobby camera the same control. Do not change the post orders while you are only copying the log. Stop when the two times and the escort are written down.\n',
        'exam': 'A camera that shows the lobby is not a guard. A badge that opens a door is not a guard. A sensor that alarms cannot ask who someone is here to see. If the stem has a person challenging, escorting, and waiting for a host, choose the guard. An empty post is not a working guard. The miss is giving the guard credit to a device that cannot judge intent or walk a visitor to a room.\n',
    },
    'Access badges': {
        'body': "An access badge is a token a door reader checks so the building can identify which person is asking to enter. The badge stands for that person until someone disables it. People can hand a card to someone else, which is why a lost badge or a badge from a person who has left is dangerous the same day. A password is typed. A guard is a person who can ask a question. A vestibule is a double door. The badge is the thing the reader accepts or rejects, and it identifies the holder the token was issued to.\n\nThe mechanism is simple. The reader sees the token, the door system looks up who it belongs to, and the strike releases or stays shut. The log should show the door, the badge, the result, and the time. If the token is still enabled, the door treats the holder as that person. Disabling the badge cuts off that identity at the door. Waiting a week, or waiting until the next audit, leaves a key in the world. The same-day disable is part of the control, not a courtesy. A badge left on a lanyard at the desk is still a live token if nobody has turned it off.\n\nLuis Patel was a contractor through Friday. Nobody revoked badge 44192 on his last day. On Monday at 07:04 the side door reader returns grant for badge 44192. Dana Cole finds that line while checking weekend entries. The system is the side door reader and the badge list behind it. The specific fact is the Monday grant for a person who should have been disabled on Friday. The door identified the token as Luis and let the holder in. It had no way to know the contract was over, because the token was still valid.\n\nA defender disables a badge the day it is lost and the day the person leaves. Search the reader log for grants tied to people who are already gone. Do not accept a returned laptop as proof the badge is dead. Do not leave the side door grant unwritten because the visit looked brief. Write badge 44192, the door, the time 07:04, and the Friday end date on the ticket, then disable the token. A guard can still challenge a face the badge gets wrong. The badge itself only identifies the token the reader saw.\n\nStudents confuse the badge with a password, with a guard, or with the door alone. A password does not open the side door by itself in this scene. A guard can escort and can notice the wrong face. The reader without a disable list will keep honoring an old token. The separating fact is identification by a token the door checks, plus same-day disable when the person leaves or the card is lost. If the stem shows a former person's badge still opening a door, the miss was revocation, and the control in the story is the badge.\n",
        'r0': 'An access badge is a token the door checks so it can identify the person the token was issued to. Badge 44192 for contractor Luis Patel still returns grant at the side door on Monday at 07:04. His last day was Friday, and nobody disabled the badge that day. The reader log is the evidence: door, badge number, grant, and time. A lost card must be disabled the same day, because anyone holding it is identified as the owner. A password is not this token. A guard can question a face the card cannot. Choose the badge when the stem is about a card the reader still trusts.\n',
        'r1': 'A badge is a token the door checks. It tells the door who is asking. A lost badge must die the same day. A badge for someone who left must die that day too. Anyone holding a live card looks like the owner. A password is not a badge. A guard is a person, not a card. Check the reader log for old grants. Turn the old token off. Write the badge number and the time. Do not wait for next month.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a workshop where each person has a small tag that the tool-room latch reads. The latch opens for a tag that is still on the list, and it names the person that tag was given to. If someone keeps a tag after their last shift, the latch still says they belong. A clerk who could ask questions is a different control. A code someone types by hand is different again, because it is not a token you can hand over. On the real side door, the badge is that tag. It identifies the holder, and it should stop working the day the person leaves.\n',
        'r4': 'Find the Monday 07:04 grant for badge 44192 on the side door reader. Write the badge number, the door, and the time on the ticket. Record that Luis Patel ended on Friday and the token was still live. Do not disable a different badge while you are still copying this line. Do not call the grant a guard action. After the facts are written, disable badge 44192. Stop when the log line and the Friday end date are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A badge identifies a person to a door by a token, and a live token still works after the person leaves. Same-day disable is part of the control. A password typed at a keyboard is not this token. A guard who escorts is not this token. A vestibule is a double door, not a card. If the stem shows a former contractor badge still opening a door, the miss is revocation. Do not treat the door grant as proof the person was still allowed.\n',
    },
    'Protective lighting': {
        'body': 'Protective lighting is light placed so dark hiding spots go away around doors, fences, and parking. People can see, and cameras can see. The lamp is not a lock, and it is not a recording. A bright door can still be opened with a key, and a bright fence can still be climbed. What the light changes is whether the approach happens in a hiding spot. Without it, a camera aimed at that door stores a black frame, and a guard at a distance is looking at a shadow.\n\nThe mechanism is removing cover. You put lamps where a person would stand to work unseen: above a rear door, along a fence line, across a parking row. The beam has to land on the place you care about, not only in the eyes of a driver. Glare that blinds a camera is a failed aim even if the lamp is on. A dead lamp returns the hiding spot the same night. Lighting works with other controls. It makes a fence climb visible, and it gives a camera a picture. It does not replace either one.\n\nSam Ortiz checks the rear door after a report that the camera saw nothing all night. At 23:40 camera four shows a black frame. The lamp above that door is out. He replaces the lamp, and the same camera then shows the walkway and the door. Ticket 356 holds the black frame time, the lamp location, and the later frame that shows the walkway. The system is the lamp plus the camera that needed it. The specific thing you can see is the change from a black frame to a visible door. The camera did not start working because someone started it again. The scene became visible.\n\nA defender walks the building at night and writes down every dark door, fence corner, and parking pocket. Replace dead lamps and aim them so a face and a door handle can be seen, not so the lens fills with glare. Do not call the new lamp a camera. Do not call it a fence. Check the camera again after the lamp is in, and keep the before-and-after times. If a fence is the control you meant, the lamp is only the partner that lets a watch see the climb.\n\nStudents pick the camera, the fence, or the lock when the stem is really about darkness. The camera records what the lamp reveals, and a black frame means the light failed first. The fence delays a climber whether or not it is lit, but a fence with no lamp hides that delay from the watch. A lock holds a door in the dark and still is not lighting. The separating fact is the hiding spot. If people or cameras cannot see until a lamp is restored, protective lighting is the control that was missing.\n',
        'r0': 'Protective lighting removes dark hiding spots at doors, fences, and parking so people and cameras can see. At 23:40 the rear door camera shows a black frame because the lamp above the door is out. After Sam Ortiz replaces that lamp, the same camera shows the walkway. Ticket 356 should keep both times. The lamp is not the recording, and it is not the lock. A fence still needs this light if a watch is supposed to see a climb. Glare that washes the lens out is a bad aim, not a success. Choose lighting when the stem is a scene that stays hidden until a lamp is fixed.\n',
        'r1': 'Light takes away a dark hiding spot. People can see the door. Cameras can see the door too. A dead lamp brings the dark back. A black picture means the light failed. The lamp is not a lock. The lamp is not the camera. Aim the light at the door, not into the lens. Check the picture after you change a lamp. Write the time of the dark frame. Do not skip the night walk. Parking rows need light too.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic back step where the lamp over the door has gone dark. Anyone at the step can work without a shape showing on the wall. A camera pointed at that step stores nothing you can use. A bolt on the door still holds or fails on its own. When the lamp is replaced, the step is no longer a hiding spot, and the camera can see a person standing on the step with the door in view. On the real rear door, that lamp is protective lighting. It lets people and cameras see, and it does not lock the door.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull the rear door frame from 23:40 and confirm it is black. Write the dead lamp location and that time on ticket 356. After the lamp is replaced, capture a frame that shows the walkway. Do not aim the new lamp so the lens is only glare. Do not describe the lamp as the camera or as the lock. Do not erase the black frame. Stop when both times and the lamp location are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A black camera frame fixed by replacing a lamp is a lighting problem. The camera is what records, and the lamp is what makes the scene visible. A lock can hold a door in the dark and is not lighting. A fence can delay a climber in the dark and is not lighting. Glare that hides a face is not success. The miss is choosing the camera or the lock when the stem is a hiding spot that light removes.\n',
    },
    'Infrared sensors': {
        'body': 'Infrared sensors notice heat, or they notice a broken invisible beam. They fit doorways and short halls, where a person passes close or steps through a line. They are not a mat that feels weight, they are not a microwave field filling a wide bay, and they are not silent sound. Heat and a broken beam are the two ways this sensor works. A long warehouse aisle is a poor match for a single beam, because the line never reaches the far racks. Use this sensor where the path is short and the thing you need is heat or a crossed line.\n\nA passive unit watches for a change in heat, such as a warm body entering a small room. An active unit sends a beam and alarms when something blocks it. The closet case is the beam. You aim it across the door so a step through breaks the line, and the panel shows that zone. The beam does not know a name, and it does not know a badge. It knows the line was whole and then it was not, or it knows heat appeared where the view is set. Sun, a heater, or a blocked lens can confuse a heat unit, so placement is part of making the sensor honest.\n\nPriya Okonkwo is on call when alarm zone 14 trips at 22:16. That zone is the beam across the closet door. Someone stepped through after hours, the line broke, and the panel recorded the zone and the time. The closet is a short path, which is the right shape for this sensor. The system is the beam and the alarm panel. The specific line is zone 14 at 22:16. A mat was not in this closet, so weight was not the fact. A wide bay sensor was not the fact either.\n\nA defender tests the beam by walking through after the door is set, and writes the zone that lights. Keep boxes from sitting in the line, or the zone stays in alarm for a boring reason. Do not put a single beam across a bay that is too wide and call it coverage. Do not clear zone 14 only because no badge was used, since the beam does not wait for a card. If the room is a drape-filled hall where you needed sound-based motion, this beam is a different tool and may still be the right one for the doorway itself.\n\nThe neighboring sensors measure different things. Pressure sensors notice weight where a foot must land. Microwave sensors notice motion inside a radio field spread through a wide room. Ultrasonic sensors use sound people cannot hear, and heavy curtains swallow that sound. The separating fact is heat or a broken invisible beam on a short path. If the stem is a closet door beam after hours, or a heat trip in a short hall, infrared sensors are the control. Do not rename them as weight or as a warehouse field.\n',
        'r0': 'Infrared sensors notice heat or a broken invisible beam, which suits a doorway or a short hall. Alarm zone 14 is the beam across the closet door. It trips at 22:16 when someone steps through after hours. The panel line is the zone and the time. A single beam does not cover a bay that is too wide for that line. Weight on a mat is a different sensor. A radio field in an open room is a different sensor. Sound that curtains can swallow is a different sensor. Test the beam with a walk-through and keep stored items out of the line. Choose infrared when the stem is heat or a broken beam.\n',
        'r1': 'These sensors notice heat or a broken beam. They fit a doorway or a short hall. They do not notice weight on a mat. They do not fill a wide room with radio. They do not use sound. A step through the beam is the trip. A heater can fool a heat unit. Keep boxes out of the line. Write the zone and the time. A wide bay needs a different sensor. Test by walking through the door. A short hall is the right place.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a short hall outside a clinic supply closet with a thread of light no person can see stretched across the frame. A step through the frame breaks the thread, and a bell in the office sounds. The thread does not feel a foot on a rug, and it does not wash the whole warehouse in a hum. Heavy curtains in a meeting room are a different problem, because this thread is at the door, not in the cloth. On the real closet, that thread is the infrared beam. It notices the break, or a sister unit notices heat, and neither one is weighing the floor.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the alarm panel and copy zone 14 at 22:16. Walk the closet beam once and confirm the same zone trips. Note that the path is a doorway, not a wide bay. Write whether the trip was a broken beam or a heat unit. Do not move stock into the beam while you are still testing. Do not label the zone as a floor mat or as a bay sensor. Stop when the zone, the time, and the beam path are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Heat or a broken beam is not weight, not a microwave field, and not sound. A mat can alarm while a door beam stays whole, and that mat is a different sensor. A warehouse bay too wide for one line is a poor infrared layout. Curtains that swallow sound do not explain a beam across a closet door. If the stem is zone 14 at the closet after hours, choose the beam. The miss is swapping this sensor for whichever motion device was in the last question.\n',
    },
    'Pressure sensors': {
        'body': 'Pressure sensors notice weight on a mat, a pad, or a floor. They belong where a foot must land, not where you only need a broken beam or a wash of radio energy. The sensor does not care about heat, and it does not listen for sound. It cares that a load showed up on a spot you chose. A person who steps around the mat does not trip it. A person who stands on it does, even if a door contact stays quiet because the leaf never moved.\n\nThe mechanism is a switch or a pad under the surface that changes when load crosses a threshold. You place it in the records-room doorway, under a desk approach, or on a landing nobody can skip. The panel gets a zone for that mat. A door contact on the same opening tells a different story: the leaf opened or it did not. Those two facts can disagree, and the disagreement is useful. Weight without an open event means someone is on the mat while the contact thinks the door is still, or the contact missed a move the feet did not miss. The mat does not name the person. A badge would do that. The mat only says the weight is there.\n\nDana Cole checks the records room after closing. At 18:42 the mat zone alarms. The door contact on that same room stays quiet for the whole hour, with no open and no close. Dana writes ticket 402 with the mat time, the quiet contact, and the room name. The system is the mat and the door contact beside it. The specific pair of facts is the alarm at 18:42 and the contact that did not speak. Someone stood where feet must land. The contact alone would have let a reviewer call the room empty.\n\nA defender tests the mat by standing on it and by stepping beside it, and writes which zone lights. Compare that zone with the door contact before you clear the alarm. Do not dismiss weight because the contact is quiet. Do not cover the mat with a rug so thick that a person no longer crosses the threshold. If the stem is a beam across a closet, do not call it this sensor. If the stem is motion in a wide bay, the mat is the wrong shape unless the feet have to hit one pad.\n\nStudents swap this sensor for a door contact or for infrared. A contact reports a leaf. Infrared reports heat or a broken beam. Microwave reports a disturbed radio field, and ultrasonic reports motion by sound. The separating fact is weight where a foot must land. If the mat alarms and the door contact stays quiet, you are looking at pressure sensors. Clearing the ticket from the contact alone throws away the only fact that said a person was there.\n',
        'r0': 'Pressure sensors notice weight on a mat, a pad, or a floor at a spot where feet must land. In the records room the mat zone alarms at 18:42. The door contact on that room stays quiet, with no open and no close in the hour. Ticket 402 should show both facts. The contact reports the leaf, not the weight. A closet beam would report a broken line, not a load on the floor. Test by standing on the mat and by stepping off it. A thick cover that keeps the load under the threshold blinds the sensor. Choose pressure when the stem is weight, especially weight the door contact missed.\n',
        'r1': 'This sensor notices weight. It sits on a mat, a pad, or a floor. It fits a spot where feet must land. It does not notice heat. It does not notice a broken beam. A quiet door contact is not the same fact. Standing on the mat should alarm. Stepping beside the mat should not. Do not bury the mat under a thick rug. Write the time the weight showed up. Compare it with the door contact. The mat does not know a name.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a pantry in a clinic kitchen with a mat in front of the only shelf that holds the drug log. A cook who wants that shelf has to stand on the mat. The floor creaks in the picture only when the foot lands there. A bell on the pantry door can stay silent if the leaf was already open. The mat still knows about the weight. A beam across a different closet would tell a different story about a broken line. In the real records room, the floor mat is that same idea. It notices weight where a person must step, even when the door contact stays quiet.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the 18:42 mat alarm and the quiet door contact onto ticket 402. Stand on the records-room mat and confirm the zone trips. Step off the mat and confirm it clears. Do not clear the alarm from the contact alone. Do not stack a rug on the mat before the test is written down. Note that the sensor is weight, not a beam. Stop when the mat time and the quiet contact are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Weight on a mat is not a door contact, even on the same opening. A quiet contact plus a mat alarm is the classic split. Infrared is heat or a broken beam, not a load on the floor. A microwave field covers a volume and does not need a foot on one pad. If the stem is someone standing where feet must land, choose pressure. The miss is closing the ticket because the door leaf never reported an open.\n',
    },
    'Microwave sensors': {
        'body': 'A wide open room can be watched by filling the space with radio energy and noticing when movement disturbs that field. Those devices are microwave sensors. They are not a beam across a doorway, they are not a mat that feels weight, and they are not sound people cannot hear. One line fails when the aisle is too wide for a single beam to matter. The sensor instead treats the whole volume as the space being watched. Motion near the door and motion at the far rack can both change the field. Microwave sensors belong in that bay.\n\nThe mechanism is a field, not a string across a frame. The unit fills the bay with radio energy. A person, a cart, or a swinging leaf changes the way that energy comes back, and the change is the alarm. A spinning fan or a loose banner can disturb the same field, so a person still has to look. The field has to reach the far aisle, or the bay is only partly watched. You test by walking the far end, not only by waving a hand at the housing. If that far walk does nothing, the volume is not covered, whatever the label on the box claims.\n\nSam Ortiz runs the warehouse bay. The aisle is about 40 feet, too wide for one beam from the near door to catch a person at the last rack. At 03:05 he walks that far end during a test. The sensor alarms, and the panel shows the bay zone at that time. Ticket 511 records the aisle length, the time, and the note that a doorway beam was never going to see him. The system is the bay sensor. The specific thing you can point to is the 03:05 alarm from a walk a single beam could not cover. A mat at the entrance would have stayed quiet while he stood at the far rack.\n\nA defender uses this sensor for a volume and tests the edges before trusting it. Write the far-walk result on the ticket before anyone moves the unit. Check for fans, banners, and doors that swing in the field and create alarms with no intruder. Do not aim the unit at a short closet and pretend it is a broken beam. Do not ignore a miss at the far rack and still call the bay covered. Soft cloth is a bigger problem for sound-based motion than for this radio field, so do not swap the two when the room is full of drapes. Pick the measure the room actually needs.\n\nStudents trade this sensor for infrared or for ultrasonic. Infrared notices heat or a broken beam and fits a doorway or a short hall. Ultrasonic devices notice motion with sound, and heavy curtains swallow that sound. Pressure devices notice weight where a foot must land. The separating fact is radio energy across a volume. If the stem says the space is too wide for a single beam, and motion anywhere in that space should count, this is the sensor.\n',
        'r0': 'A microwave sensor covers an open bay with radio energy, and movement that changes the field raises the alarm. The warehouse bay aisle is about 40 feet, too wide for a single beam. At 03:05 Sam Ortiz walks the far end and the bay zone alarms. Ticket 511 should show the length, the time, and the far-rack result. A closet beam would never have reached that walk. A mat at the entrance would have stayed quiet. Fans and swinging doors can disturb the same field, so a test still needs a person to look. Choose this sensor when the stem is motion across a space one line cannot cover.\n',
        'r1': 'This sensor fills a big room with radio. Motion that disturbs the field sets it off. A single beam cannot cover a wide bay. Heat is a different measure. Weight on a mat is a different measure. Silent sound is a different measure. Test by walking at the far rack. A fan can disturb the field too. Aim the sensor and test again. Write the time of the alarm. Do not call it a doorway beam. The bay is the right kind of space.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a large clinic storeroom with open aisles and no inner walls. A string across the near door would miss a person at the back wall. The room is filled instead with a quiet hum, and a step at the far rack changes that hum. A mat on one tile would miss the same walker. Cloth that eats a shout is a different problem and belongs to a different device. A spinning fan can mimic a person in the hum, so someone still checks. In the real warehouse, that hum is the microwave sensor. The bay is too wide for one beam, and motion in the volume is what counts.\n',
        'r4': 'Walk the far end of the 40-foot aisle and record whether the bay zone trips. Write 03:05 and the aisle length on ticket 511. Note any fan or swinging door inside the field. Do not move the sensor while you are still writing the test. Do not label the zone as a closet beam. Do not clear the alarm until the far walk is recorded. Stop when the volume test and the time are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A bay too wide for one beam is not an infrared doorway. Heat or a broken line is a different sensor. Weight on a mat does not cover the far rack. Sound that curtains swallow is not this radio field. A fan can cause an alarm and still does not change what the sensor measures. If the stem is motion anywhere in a large open room, choose the radio volume. The miss is calling every motion device a beam.\n',
    },
    'Ultrasonic sensors': {
        'body': 'Some motion sensors do their work with sound people cannot hear. Those are ultrasonic sensors. They can watch a room the way a radio field can, but the measure is sound, not microwave energy, not heat, and not weight. The sound has to travel out and come back. Heavy curtains, drapes, and soft furnishings swallow it. A plush conference room is a poor place for this sensor, and a team that tests honestly will pick another type there. The room ate the echo.\n\nThe mechanism is a pulse of sound above hearing and a listen for the return. Movement changes that return, and the change is the alarm. In a hard room the return is clean enough to trust. In a room of cloth the return may never come, so a person can walk and the zone stays quiet. That quiet is a miss, not a calm night. You notice the drapes, you write the failed walk, and you choose a sensor that measures something the cloth does not destroy. A beam at the door or a mat at the threshold may fit the path better than a sound unit buried in fabric.\n\nElena surveys conference room C, which is plush and full of drapes. At 14:10 she walks from the door to the far chair. The ultrasonic sensor stays quiet. She writes on the survey sheet that this type does not belong in room C and that another type must be chosen. Ticket 528 holds the room name, the time 14:10, and the quiet result. The system is that sensor plus the room that defeated it. The specific fact is a walked test with no alarm, in a space staff already knew was soft. A microwave test is not what she recorded. She recorded sound that did not come back.\n\nA defender refuses to leave this sensor as the only detector in a draped room. Run the walk test before you call the room covered. Photograph the drapes with the survey note so the next person sees why the type was rejected. Do not hide the quiet result because the hardware is new. Do not describe the miss as a curtain problem for a radio sensor, because the cloth is hurting the sound. If the doorway itself still needs a trip, a beam or a mat can cover the spot where feet or a body must pass. The sound unit does not get a pass in a soft room just because it worked in a bare hall.\n\nStudents call this the same device as a microwave sensor because both can notice motion in a room. The measures differ. Microwave energy can cross a volume that swallows sound. Infrared is heat or a broken beam on a short path. Pressure is weight on a mat. The separating fact is inaudible sound that heavy curtains and soft rooms defeat. If the stem is a plush room full of drapes and a quiet test walk, ultrasonic sensors are the wrong type, and the answer is to pick another. Do not say they succeeded there.\n',
        'r0': 'Ultrasonic sensors notice motion with sound people cannot hear, and soft rooms defeat them. Conference room C is plush and full of drapes. At 14:10 Elena walks the room and the sensor stays quiet. Ticket 528 should record the room, the time, and the failed walk. The team then picks another type. A radio field is not undone by the same cloth. A doorway beam or a mat measures heat, a broken line, or weight, not this echo. Do not treat a quiet draped room as proof the space is empty. Choose this label when the stem is sound-based motion, especially where curtains make it fail.\n',
        'r1': 'This sensor uses sound people cannot hear. It notices motion when that sound returns. Heavy curtains swallow the sound. A soft plush room is a poor place. A radio field does not share this flaw. Heat and weight are different measures. Walk the room and watch for silence. If it stays quiet, pick another type. Do not leave it as the only sensor. Write the failed test on the survey. Drapes are the reason it missed. Sound and radio are not the same thing.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a meeting room hung with heavy drapes and a thick carpet. A person claps, and the cloth eats the sound before it can bounce back. A bare hall would have returned the clap. A quiet hum that is not sound would still cross the drapes, because cloth is not the same problem for it. A mat at the door would only know about a foot. The team in this picture stops trusting the clap. In the real conference room, that failed clap is the ultrasonic sensor. Soft drapes are why it stays quiet, and another type has to take the job.\n',
        'r4': 'Walk conference room C at the time you are testing and note whether the sensor stays quiet. Write 14:10, the drapes, and the quiet result on ticket 528. Photograph the soft furnishings with that note. Do not tear the drapes down while the survey is still open. Do not mark the room covered. Do not call the miss a radio-field problem. Stop when the failed walk and the choice to switch types are both written down.\n',
        'exam': 'Sound people cannot hear is not a microwave field, even when both notice motion. Drapes and a plush room explain a quiet ultrasonic test. They do not explain a broken beam at a closet door. They do not explain weight on a mat. If the stem says the team rejected this sensor in a draped room, do not answer that it worked. The miss is treating every motion sensor as the same measure. The cloth is the clue that the measure was sound.\n',
    },
    'Honeypots': {
        'body': 'A single decoy system that should never receive real work is a honeypot. Any touch is suspicious, because no honest job has a reason to be there. It is one box, not a network of decoys, and it is not a production server people are supposed to use. It is also not a fake document and not a fake password. The point is to notice interest in a system that should have stayed dark. The touch does not by itself repair the real network, and it does not mean the real servers are fine. It is a signal that someone knocked on a door that should have had no visitors.\n\nThe mechanism is isolation plus logging. You build or reserve a system that mimics something tempting, such as a file share, and you keep real data off it. Production accounts are not given a reason to connect. Every login attempt is recorded, and a page goes to the person on call. If a real application starts using the decoy, you have ruined the signal, because later knocks can be excused as business. The decoy should be unable to reach systems that matter, so a curious visitor is watched rather than handed a path into payroll. The alarm is the touch itself.\n\nPriya Okonkwo is on call when the unused file server pages her at 03:14. The log reads failed logon from 203.0.113.44 to the decoy share. No clinic job is supposed to use that server. She opens ticket 640 and copies the address, the time, the account name that was tried, and the fact that the target is the decoy. The system is that one server. The specific line is the failed logon at 03:14. She does not look for a second decoy hop yet, because this control is the single box. A quiet production log would not erase this page.\n\nA defender keeps real files off the decoy, alerts on any connection, and refuses to let a scheduled job use it for convenience. Check that production accounts cannot reach it as part of normal work. When a page arrives, write the log line before you wipe anything. Do not turn the box into a real share because disk space is tight. Do not describe the page as proof the intruder is already done, or as proof the payroll server is healthy. Containment of a real account, if you later find one, is a separate step from this signal.\n\nStudents call one decoy a honeynet, or they call a production server a honeypot because it has logs. A honeynet is several decoys, used when you want to see how someone moves. A server that holds real work will see honest logons, so a touch there is not automatically suspicious. A fake file or a fake key is a smaller bait. The separating fact is a single system that should never receive real work. If any touch is the alarm, and there is only one decoy box, honeypots are the control.\n',
        'r0': "One decoy computer kept away from real work is a honeypot, and any connection to it is suspicious. The unused server mimics a file share and listens on TCP 445. At 03:14 it logs a failed logon from 203.0.113.44 and pages Priya Okonkwo. Ticket 640 should carry that address, the time, and the decoy name. No production job is allowed to use the share. A network of decoys would be a larger control. A real server's ordinary logon is not this signal. Keep real data off the box. The page is detection, not a repair of the live payroll host.\n",
        'r1': 'A honeypot is one fake system. Real work should never touch it. Any touch is suspicious. It is not a whole fake network. It is not a server people use daily. A login there should page someone. Keep real files off that box. The decoy can mimic a file share. Write the address and the time. Do not let a normal job use it. The page is a signal, not a repair. One box is the whole idea. Watch it because nobody should knock.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic desk with a cash box that staff are told never to open. A bell under the lid rings if the lid moves, and the supervisor treats every ring as odd. Honest shifts have no reason to touch that box. Staff walk past it all day and leave it shut. A whole row of empty shops would be a larger trick. A marked page in a drawer is only paper. The bell does not lock the real drug cabinet. On the real network, that unused box is the honeypot, one decoy system. Any touch is suspicious, because real work was never supposed to arrive.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the 03:14 failed logon and the address 203.0.113.44 onto ticket 640. Confirm the target is the unused decoy share and not a live file server. Write that no production job is supposed to connect. Do not delete the log while the ticket is open. Do not copy real payroll files onto the decoy. Do not call this one box a network of decoys. Stop when the log line and the decoy name are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'One decoy system is not a network of decoys. A production server that people must use is not a decoy, so its ordinary logons are not this alarm. A fake document and a fake key are smaller bait. The page does not mean the live systems were repaired. If any touch on a single unused system is the signal, choose the single decoy. The miss is naming the whole fake street, or naming a real server, when only one dark box was touched.\n',
    },
    'Honeynets': {
        'body': 'A small network of decoy systems, kept so you can watch how an intruder moves, is a honeynet. One dark box can tell you that someone knocked. Several dark boxes can tell you they tried a second and a third. Production accounts are not allowed to use the segment, because honest work would muddy the trail. Real data does not belong on those systems. The control is the fake street, not the live clinic network, and not a single file left as bait. You are studying movement.\n\nThe mechanism is a separated segment plus rules that keep real users out. Each decoy logs touches. The useful event is the sequence: a connection to the first decoy, then a connection to another, with no business reason for either. A firewall deny for a production account is a healthy sign that the boundary is holding. If that account can roam the segment, you no longer know which hits are intruders and which are staff. The decoys should not be a bridge into payroll or mail. Watching movement is the goal. Handing over the real network is not.\n\nElena runs the lab segment that holds the decoys. Production accounts are blocked. The firewall shows a deny for Dana Cole from her clinic workstation toward 10.9.9.12, which is one of the decoy hosts. Later the same night a source that is not a clinic account touches the file decoy and then the second decoy. Elena writes ticket 655 with the deny for Dana, the two decoy names, and the order of the touches. The system is the fake segment. The specific facts are the production deny and the hop from the first decoy to the second. That hop is the movement a single box cannot show.\n\nA defender keeps the segment closed to production accounts and reviews paths across the decoys rather than a single login. When you write the ticket, record the order of systems touched. Do not open the segment so a technician can grab a file. Do not place real mail or real payroll data on a decoy to make it look richer. Do not call the live server network a honeynet because it has more than one host. If you only have one decoy, use the single-box name and do not invent a trail you did not see.\n\nStudents stop at the first decoy and call the whole design a single honeypot. They also call the production network a honeynet because it is a network. The production network is where real work happens, so touches there are not automatically suspicious. A fake document on a real share is not this segment. The separating fact is a set of decoys used to watch movement, with production accounts kept off. If the stem shows a second system after the first, and real users are denied, honeynets are the control.\n',
        'r0': 'Several decoy hosts in a lab segment form a honeynet when the goal is to see movement rather than one knock. The lab segment denies production accounts. Dana Cole is denied from her workstation to decoy host 10.9.9.12. A later source touches the file decoy and then a second decoy. Ticket 655 should list the deny and the order of those two touches. Real data stays off the segment. One decoy alone cannot show that hop. The live clinic network is not this control, because honest work belongs there. Choose the network of decoys when the stem is movement across more than one fake system.\n',
        'r1': 'A honeynet is several fake systems together. You watch how someone moves between them. One fake box is a smaller idea. Real accounts should not be allowed on. The path matters more than one login. Keep real data off those systems. A deny for a real user is a good sign. Write which decoy was touched second. Do not treat the lab as the live network. Movement is the fact you want. Block honest jobs from that segment. Several boxes make the trail.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a side lane of empty shops behind a clinic, all of them dark on purpose. Shoppers are not allowed down that lane. If someone tries the first door and then the second, you learn the route, not only that one lock rattled. A single dummy box on a counter is a smaller picture. The real sales floor is not this lane. Staff who belong on the sales floor are turned away at the rope. A walker in that lane is not a shopper. On the real lab network, that lane is the honeynet. It exists to show movement across decoys, and production accounts stay off it.\n',
        'r4': 'Record the deny of Dana Cole toward 10.9.9.12 on ticket 655. Write the order of decoys touched after that, if a second hit exists. Confirm production accounts are still blocked. Do not add Dana to the segment while you are documenting the path. Do not copy live mail data onto a decoy. Do not describe a single box as this whole network. Stop when the deny and the movement order are both written down.\n',
        'exam': 'Several decoys watched for movement are not one decoy box. The live network is not a fake street, even if it has many servers. A production user who can roam the decoys has broken the design. A single login on one dark host does not show a path. If the stem has a second decoy after the first, and real accounts are denied, choose the network of decoys. The miss is stopping at the first box or pointing at production.\n',
    },
    'Honeyfiles': {
        'body': 'A decoy document that legitimate jobs are never supposed to open is a honeyfile. The open is the alarm. Nobody needs to steal a password for this signal to fire, and nobody needs to log on to a fake server. The file sits where a curious person or a script might click it, and payroll staff are told never to touch that one name. It does not hold the real salary book. If it did, an honest quarterly task would open it and the alarm would become noise. The control is the forbidden open, not the real spreadsheet the business actually uses.\n\nThe mechanism is placement, a warning to the people who work nearby, and an alert tied to the open. You give the file a tempting name and you put it beside real work without making it part of any job. When the file is opened, the file server or the alert tool records the account, the name, and the time. Closing the file does not undo the signal. A person who was told never to click has either made a mistake you still want to see or has no business in that folder. Either way the open is news. A fake key used from the internet is a different news item, because that signal is use of a secret, not a document view.\n\nHelen Nguyen tells payroll never to open the spreadsheet named salaries-full.xlsx. It sits in a folder they use for other work. At 11:22 the account kim opens that file, and an alert is created with the account, the file name, and the time. Dana Cole copies the alert into ticket 670. The system is the file server that saw the open. The specific fact is kim at 11:22 on salaries-full.xlsx, a file the job roster says never to click. The real salary workbook has a different name and was not the object in the alert. Helen can show the instruction that forbade the click.\n\nA defender keeps real payroll data out of the decoy, keeps the do-not-open rule visible to the staff who sit near it, and treats every open as a ticket. Do not rename the real workbook to match the decoy. Do not open the decoy yourself to check the contents and then ignore your own alert. Write the account and the time before you move the file. If the same user later uses a fake password that was never issued, record that as a separate signal. This control ends at the document open.\n\nStudents call the decoy file a honeytoken, or they call a normal payroll file a honeyfile because the name sounds sensitive. A honeytoken is a fake credential, record, or key with no real use, and using it means theft. A honeypot is a whole system. The real salary file is supposed to be opened by the people whose job it is. The separating fact is a document that legitimate jobs never open, so the open itself is the alarm. If the stem is a forbidden spreadsheet and an open event, honeyfiles are the answer.\n',
        'r0': 'A honeyfile is a decoy document that legitimate jobs never open, so the open is the alarm. Payroll is told never to click salaries-full.xlsx. At 11:22 the account kim opens it, and the alert names the account, the file, and the time. Ticket 670 should quote that alert. The file must not hold the real salary book, or honest work will trip it. A fake key used from the internet is a different control. A decoy server is a different control. The real workbook, the one payroll must open, is not this file. Choose the decoy document when the evidence is an open that should never have happened.\n',
        'r1': 'A honeyfile is a fake document. Honest jobs should never open it. The open itself is the alarm. It is not a fake password. It is not a whole fake computer. Tell staff never to click that name. Do not store real payroll inside it. Write who opened it and when. A normal workbook open is different. The name can look tempting. An alert should follow the open. Leave the decoy in place while you write. Payroll must leave that one name alone.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a payroll drawer with one extra page the clerks are ordered never to lift. The page looks like a salary list and holds nothing the business needs. If the page is lifted, a mark shows that it was opened. A key that fits no lock is a different object on another shelf. A fake computer in a closet is a different object too. The real salary binder is the one clerks must open every Friday. In the real file share, that marked page is the honeyfile. Opening it is the alarm, and honest jobs are the ones that never click.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the 11:22 alert for account kim and salaries-full.xlsx onto ticket 670. Confirm payroll was told never to open that name. Check that the decoy does not contain the real salary book. Do not open the file again while you are still copying the alert. Do not rename the live workbook to match the decoy. Do not call the alert a stolen password. Stop when the account, the file name, and the time are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'An open of a forbidden document is not the use of a fake key. A decoy server is not a document. The real salary file, the one the job must open, is not bait. If staff were told never to click a named spreadsheet and an open alert fired, choose the decoy document. Filling that file with live payroll data destroys the signal. The miss is calling the open a stolen credential, or calling a normal workbook this control.\n',
    },
    'Honeytokens': {
        'body': 'Plant a credential or key that no application will ever send, and you have a honeytoken. A later use means theft. No job is supposed to present it, and a successful use cannot be explained as business. A decoy document alarms because someone looked. This alarm means someone took a secret that was never valid and tried it where a real secret would have worked. The token can be a dummy key, a fake account record, or a planted value in a database.\n\nThe mechanism is to create the secret, keep it out of every real configuration, and alert the moment it appears in a request. You may leave it where a person who is already snooping would copy it, such as a private repository that staff use for code and that an outsider should never read. The alert should include where it was used from and when. Do not register the dummy value in the live application, or the next release will use it and you will have taught yourself to ignore the alarm. A honeyfile can sit next to this trick in the same program of bait, but the file open and the key use are different events and different tickets.\n\nSam Ortiz watches alerts for secrets that should never move. A dummy key was stored in a private repository and was not loaded by any clinic application. At 16:02 that key is used from 198.51.100.20, an address on the internet, and the alert fires. Sam writes ticket 688 with the time, the source address, and the note that the key has no real use. The system is the alert on that dummy key. The specific fact is the use at 16:02 from 198.51.100.20. Nobody can claim a partner integration, because no partner was ever given that value. Using it means the value was taken and tried.\n\nA defender plants only secrets that are absent from production, tests that a use creates an alert, and treats the alert as theft rather than as a mistyped password on a real account. Write the source and the time before you rotate unrelated real keys in a panic. Do not paste the dummy key into a config file to see what happens. Do not close the ticket as a honeyfile open if no document was viewed and a secret was presented instead. If a real key is in the repository by mistake, that is a different problem: a live secret exposed, not a decoy doing its job.\n\nStudents hear bait and pick a honeyfile, or they hear key and assume every key in a repository is fake. A honeyfile alarms on an open of a document legitimate jobs never touch. A real key that an application sends every day is not a honeytoken, and its use is not automatic proof of theft. A honeypot is a system, not a secret string. The separating fact is a credential or key with no real use, so using it means theft. If the stem is a dummy value used from the internet, honeytokens are the control.\n',
        'r0': 'A planted key that no live application should send is a honeytoken, and using it means theft. A dummy key sits in a private repository and is absent from every live application. At 16:02 it is presented from 198.51.100.20 and the alert fires. Ticket 688 should show the time, the source address, and the fact that no partner was issued that value. A document open is a different signal. A decoy server is a different signal. Do not load the dummy value into production or the alarm becomes ordinary traffic. Choose this control when a secret that should never work is used anyway.\n',
        'r1': 'A honeytoken is a fake secret. It has no honest use. Using it means theft. A fake document is a different trick. Opening a file is not this signal. A real key an app sends is not fake. Hide the fake where a snoop would look. An alert should fire when it is used. Write the source and the time. Do not put the fake into production. No honest system should send it. Treat the use as stolen material. A record can be fake in the same way.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a spare key on a high shelf that fits no lock in the building. Staff have no errand that needs it. The shelf is not where real keys are kept. Nobody on the night shift should try it. If that key turns up in a lock across town, someone took it and tried it. A marked page in a drawer only shows that a page was opened. A fake cash box shows that a container was touched. The spare key opens nothing on purpose. In the real repository, that useless key is the honeytoken. Using it means theft, because the secret had no honest job.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the alert from 16:02 and copy the source address 198.51.100.20 onto ticket 688. Write that the dummy key has no real use and is not in any live application. Do not paste the dummy key into a config file. Do not rotate unrelated live keys before this note is written. Do not file the alert as a document open. Stop when the time, the source, and the lack of a real use are on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Use of a secret that has no real purpose means theft. An open of a decoy document is a different alarm. A live key that an application sends all day is not a decoy, and its use is not this proof. A decoy computer is not a credential. If the stem is a dummy value used from the internet, choose the fake secret. Loading that value into production destroys the test. The miss is calling the use a file open, or calling a real key fake.\n',
    },
    'Change approval': {
        'body': "The recorded yes, given before anyone makes the edit, by the person allowed to accept the risk, is change approval. It is a decision, not the typing, and not the name of whoever will carry the work afterward. A firewall rule that appears at midnight with no ticket and no yes was not approved. It was only typed. People who will feel an outage are stakeholders, and they are not the yes unless one of them is the person allowed to accept the risk and they actually recorded it. Ownership is who is accountable after the decision. Approval is the decision itself, and it has to come first.\n\nThe mechanism is a ticket or other record that shows who accepted the risk, what they accepted, and that the acceptance landed before the change. The person who may say yes is defined by the change practice, not by whoever is awake. If that person has not recorded the yes, the operator does not get to invent it by making the edit. A conversation in a hallway can explain intent, but the control is the record. After the edit, a note that says we meant to ask does not move the decision backward in time. The clock order is the whole test: decision, then change.\n\nAt 00:14 the firewall log shows a new rule. There is no ticket number, and Helen Nguyen, who is allowed to accept this risk, has no recorded yes for that rule. The night operator typed the change and went on. Dana Cole finds the log in the morning and opens ticket 701 to describe what is missing, not to pretend the yes exists. The system is the firewall and the empty approval record. The specific facts are the 00:14 log line and the absence of Helen's yes before that minute. The rule may be a good idea. It is still an unapproved edit.\n\nA defender refuses to call the typed rule the decision. Find whether a yes exists, who gave it, and whether its time is earlier than the edit. If the yes is missing, write that gap and stop further edits until the person allowed to accept the risk records a decision. Do not write a yes today and describe it as last night's approval. Do not put a department name where a person's decision should be. Stakeholders can be told what happened, and telling them is not the same as approval. The owner who will roll the rule back if mail breaks is still a separate line on the ticket.\n\nStudents pick the person who typed, the team name, or the owner, and they call that approval. Typing is the edit. A team name is not a decision by the person allowed to accept the risk. The owner is who carries the change and answers later. The separating fact is a recorded yes before the edit. If the stem is a midnight firewall change with no ticket and no recorded yes, the missing control is change approval. Do not grade the rule as approved because it is already in the firewall.\n",
        'r0': "The decision called change approval is a yes written down before the firewall edit, by the person who may accept the risk. At 00:14 a firewall rule appears with no ticket number. Helen Nguyen is the person who may accept that risk, and she has no yes on record before 00:14. Ticket 701 should state the log time and the missing decision. A note written in the morning does not become last night's yes. The person who will later roll the rule back is the owner, which is a different fact. Stakeholders who feel an outage are not the decision. Choose approval when the stem asks whether a recorded yes came first.\n",
        'r1': 'Approval is a yes before the edit. The yes has to be written down. The person who says yes accepts the risk. Typing the change is not the yes. A team name is not the yes. Midnight work with no ticket is not approval. Get the decision before anyone edits. Write who said yes and when. Do not call the firewall line the decision. The owner is a different fact. People who only feel the outage are not the yes. A morning note does not rewrite last night.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic kitchen where the menu card stays as written until the owner marks yes on the card. The cook does not cross out a dish and start a new soup in the same breath. A hallway nod that nobody writes down is not the card. The cook who will stir the pot is a different name from the yes. Diners who will taste the soup are not the yes either. A scribble after the soup is served does not pretend to be the earlier mark. On a real edit, that written yes is change approval. It is the decision before the change, from the person allowed to accept the risk.\n',
        'r4': 'Find the firewall rule time 00:14 and search for a ticket and a yes from Helen Nguyen. Write on ticket 701 that no decision was recorded before the edit. Name Helen as the person who is allowed to accept the risk. Do not add another firewall rule while this gap is open. Do not date a new yes as if it happened at 00:14. Do not call the night operator the person who accepted the risk. Stop when the log time and the missing yes are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A recorded yes before the edit is the decision. The typed rule is not the decision. The owner who carries the work is not the decision. A department name is not the decision. Stakeholders who feel the outage are not the decision unless they are the person allowed to accept the risk and they recorded the yes first. A morning note does not move the clock backward. If the stem is a midnight change with no ticket, the missing piece is approval. The miss is calling the edit itself a yes.\n',
    },
    'Change ownership': {
        'body': 'One person, named on the work, who will carry the change and answer questions after it lands, is what change ownership means. The duty is accountability. It is not the yes that had to come before the edit, and it is not the list of people who will feel the outage. A department written in the owner line is a crowd, not a person. When mail breaks, someone has to know who rolls the rule back and who explains the choice. If the ticket says network team, nobody holds that duty. The change can be approved and still be unowned.\n\nThe mechanism is a single name in the owner field before the window starts, plus a real expectation that this person can act. The name is not a group mailbox. The person should know how to carry the edit and how to undo it. Approval can already be on the ticket from the person who accepted the risk. That yes does not fill the owner line. Stakeholders such as payroll, who cannot send mail while the rule is wrong, need to be told, and telling them does not make them the owner. After the change, questions go to the named person. A ticket that cannot answer who owns the rollback is not finished, even if the rule is already typed.\n\nHelen Nguyen recorded the yes on CHG-4401 yesterday, so the decision exists. The owner line says network team. Overnight the firewall change stands, and the mail queue starts to fail. Dana Cole asks who will roll the rule back. The ticket has no person to call. Sam Ortiz is the engineer who can actually reverse the rule, and his name is not on the ticket. Dana writes that gap on the same change: owner line is network team, mail is failing, no named rollback. The system is the ticket. The specific fact is an owner field that is not a person, while CHG-4401 is already in progress.\n\nA defender puts one name on the ticket before the window and checks that the person accepts the duty. Write who rolls back if mail breaks, and make that the same named owner or a step that owner controls. Do not leave network team in the field because it looks polite. Do not assume the person who approved the risk will be awake to answer. Tell stakeholders what will stop, and do not list them as a substitute for the owner. If the outage is already underway, still record the name before more hands touch the rule.\n\nStudents see a yes from Helen and think ownership is done. They see a team name and think a group can be accountable in the same way a person can. They see payroll suffering and think the sufferers own the change. The separating fact is one accountable person who carries the work and answers after it lands. Approval was the earlier decision. Stakeholders feel the impact. If the stem says the ticket names a team and nobody knows who will roll back a broken mail flow, change ownership is the gap.\n',
        'r0': 'One person named on the ticket has to carry the work and answer once it is live, and that duty is change ownership. On CHG-4401 Helen Nguyen already recorded the yes, so approval is not the hole. The owner line says network team. The mail queue then fails, and nobody can say who rolls the rule back. Sam Ortiz can reverse the rule, but the ticket never names him. Dana Cole should write that the owner field is not a person. Stakeholders who cannot send mail are not the owner. A department cannot be accountable the way one name can. Choose ownership when the stem asks who answers, not who approved.\n',
        'r1': 'Ownership is one accountable person. That person carries the change. That person answers after it lands. A team name is not an owner. The yes before the edit is approval. People who feel the outage are not the owner. Write one name on the ticket. Name who will undo the work if it fails. A broken mail flow needs that name. Do not leave the owner line as a department. Do not assume the person who said yes will answer. Ask who gets the call.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen after the owner has already marked yes on the menu card. One cook's name is written on the pot, and that cook answers if the soup burns. Saying the kitchen owns the soup gives you nobody to call. Diners who miss lunch feel the change and still are not that cook. The mark on the card already happened and is not the name on the pot. A second cook who could have helped is not the owner unless the pot shows that name. On the real ticket, the name on the pot is change ownership. One person is accountable for the work and for the answers after it lands.\n",
        'r4': 'Open CHG-4401 and read the owner line. Write that it says network team and that mail is failing. Record that Helen Nguyen already gave the yes, so the gap is not approval. Name Sam Ortiz only if he accepts the duty to roll the rule back. Do not type a new firewall rule while the owner line is still a team. Do not list payroll as the owner because they feel the outage. Stop when one accountable person is written on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'One named person who carries the change and answers afterward is ownership. The recorded yes before the edit is approval, even when Helen already gave it. A team name such as network team is not a person and is not an owner. Stakeholders who cannot send mail feel the change and do not own it. If nobody knows who will roll the rule back, the owner is missing. The miss is treating the yes, the department, or the sufferers as the accountable name.\n',
    },
    'Stakeholders': {
        'body': 'Stakeholders are the people who will feel a change, and those people have to be named before the work begins. The requester who asked for the edit is a stakeholder. Every operator who keeps the affected system running is a stakeholder. Anyone whose work will stop or change shape is a stakeholder. A department label such as network team does not name a stakeholder by itself. The stakeholder list is an audience owed notice, not a single job title typed in the corner of a form.\n\nNames on the stakeholder list come from the request, from every system in the path, and from the business calendar. Each person on the list gets a reason for being there, a channel for the notice, and a time to object before the window opens. The person who approves the risk may also be a stakeholder. The person who will carry the change may also be a stakeholder. Approval and ownership are narrower roles, and neither role replaces the rest of the audience. A late message that draws no reply is not agreement from the people who were skipped. When the scope of a change grows, the newly affected people are added and given the same chance to object.\n\nTicket 9004 set a file server to reboot during the Friday payroll close. The change board had already accepted that window. The payroll lead was missing from the stakeholder list and learned of the reboot only when the share went dark. The payroll close stopped. A workstation log at 16:12, later attached by the service desk, showed a failed open of the payroll share and the text that the host was down. The reboot had a change record. The people who lost the close had not been told.\n\nA defender writes every stakeholder name on the ticket before approval and records the clock time each person was told. The business calendar is part of that check, because a payroll close, a clinic day, or another peak changes who must hear. A missing name is added and the window waits, rather than hoping the outage will be short. Proof of notice stays on the ticket beside the names. An objection that arrives before the window gets a real answer while the plan can still change.\n\nStudents mix this audience up with the single owner who carries the work, and with the person who signs the risk acceptance. Ownership answers who will do the edit and explain it afterward. Approval answers who accepted the risk. The separating fact is whose work stops. Payroll is a stakeholder when the Friday close cannot finish, even if another person owns the reboot and another person signed the approval. Customers and on-site contractors are stakeholders when their work will stop. Notice that starts after the share is already down has missed the duty.',
        'r0': 'Name the requester, the operators, and anyone whose work will stop, then tell them before the window opens. Ticket 9004 ran a reboot of a file server during the Friday payroll close and left the payroll lead off the list. A workstation log at 16:12 showed a failed open of the payroll share because the host was down. The person who approves the risk, and the person who carries the change, are narrower than this audience. Notice that begins after the outage has already started comes too late. A business calendar belongs in the check, because a close or a clinic day changes who must hear. Record the clock time each person was told, and add new names if the scope grows. Silence after a late note is not a yes unless the written process says that silence counts.',
        'r1': 'People who feel a change are stakeholders. The person who asked for the work is a stakeholder. Operators who run the system are stakeholders. Anyone whose work will stop is a stakeholder. Tell stakeholders before the window opens. A Friday payroll close shows a missing name. The person who approves risk is not the whole list. The person who does the work is not the whole list. Silence after a note is not agreement. Write down when each person was told. Add names if the change grows. Customers count when their work will stop.',
        'r2': 'A building manager does not shut the water off until the tenants on every floor have been told which taps will go dry. The people who feel the stop are those tenants and the staff whose work needs the water. The manager who signed the repair permit is one person, and the signature does not replace the warning. If the pipes go quiet and people learn by a dry tap, the warning failed. One engineer who turns the valve is a different role from the whole list of people who lose water.\n\nThose tenants and that staff are the stakeholders, because their work stops when the water stops. The signature on the permit is approval, a narrower act than telling everyone who will feel the loss of water.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9004 and read the names told about the Friday file server reboot. Write down the payroll lead if that name is missing. Write the clock time each person was notified. Check the business calendar for the payroll close. Separate the approval signature from this list of names. Separate the person who will run the reboot from this list of names. Do not start the reboot yet. Do not treat a silent inbox as agreement. Stop when the affected people and the proof of notice are written on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'People whose work stops are the audience that must be told before the window. The person who approves the risk is not that whole audience. The single owner who carries the change is not that whole audience either. A missed Friday payroll close is what a gap in the audience looks like. Notice that starts after the share is already down comes too late. Silence is not agreement. Customers and on-site contractors count when their work will stop. Proof of notice belongs on the ticket with the names.',
    },
    'Impact analysis': {
        'body': 'Impact analysis is the account, written before the first command, of what can break, who is affected, and how bad that harm is. The account still has to exist while the change can be refused or shrunk. A clinic lead should be able to read the account without help. A line on a template that says low is not an account if nobody traced the links. A note typed after the outage, to explain a surprise, is also not this forecast.\n\nThe work walks both directions of dependence. Systems this change needs belong on the list, and systems that need this change belong on the list too. The same note names how long the harm lasts, who cannot work, and any security effect, such as a temporary rule that lets every source reach a server the old rule had kept narrow. The worst plausible miss belongs in that note beside the likely miss. If the scope changes, the forecast is written again. People who approve the change can read the forecast, and the approval does not replace the forecast.\n\nTicket 9115 planned a reboot of the identity server and called the impact low. The ticket never said that clinic wireless sign-in asks that same server for every user. When the server went down, the clinic could not sign in. The wireless controllers logged authentication failures against that identity host for the whole forty minutes of the reboot. The reboot was on the calendar. The reach of the outage was not.\n\nA defender writes this forecast before the window, in words a business reader can follow, and attaches the systems and the expected minutes. Hidden jobs and upstream identity services are the usual misses, so the questions continue until the answers stop naming new victims. A security side effect belongs in the same writing. The defender does not edit production while the rating is still a blank low with no dependency check behind it.\n\nStudents confuse this forecast with evidence from a lab, and with the steps that return a system to the last known good state. Lab evidence shows whether a trial passed or failed. The return steps say how to get back after a bad result. The separating fact is time and purpose. Impact analysis is the map of harm written before the start. A hidden dependency, such as wireless sign-in that dies when the identity server dies, is the classic hole in that map.',
        'r0': 'Before any command runs, list the systems that can break, the people who are affected, and the severity. Ticket 9115 called an identity server reboot low and never mentioned clinic wireless sign-in. The wireless controllers then logged authentication failures for forty minutes against that identity host. Dependencies in both directions, the duration, and security side effects such as a wider temporary rule belong in the same forecast. A pilot log is test evidence, and restore steps are a return path, so neither one is this forecast. A template rating of low, with no check of what else stops, is an empty forecast. If the scope grows, write the forecast again before the window. The person who approves the change reads this forecast and does not replace it.',
        'r1': 'Write what can break before you touch the system. Name the people who are hurt. Say how bad that harm is. Look for systems that depend on this one. Look for systems this one needs. A lab pass is not the forecast of harm. Undo steps are not the forecast of harm. A low rating with no look behind it is empty. Hidden sign-in links are a common miss. Security side effects belong in the same note. Write the note again if the scope grows. Ask what else stops, then ask once more.',
        'r2': 'Before a warehouse crew shuts the main freezer, the lead walks every aisle and writes which rooms will thaw, whose stock will spoil, and how long the thaw will last. The walk happens before the switch is thrown, while there is still time to leave the freezer on. A taste of one sample box is a different job from that list. A note about how to turn the freezer back on is a different job as well. If the lead copies the word low from an old form and never opens the door, the list is fake.\n\nThe list of rooms, stock, people, and minutes is the impact analysis in this picture. The sample taste and the turn-back note are not that forecast of harm.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9115 and list every system that stops if the identity server has a reboot. Write down clinic wireless sign-in if those controllers ask that server for users. Write the expected minutes and who cannot work during them. Add any temporary rule that is wider than the rule it replaces. Separate this forecast from pilot logs. Separate this forecast from the steps that put an old copy back. Do not reboot the server yet. Stop when the breakage is written before the change.',
        'exam': 'A forecast of breakage, written before the start, is this account. Lab evidence is a trial record and is not this account. Steps that return a system to the last known good state are not this account. A hidden dependency is the usual miss, such as wireless sign-in that dies with the identity server. A template line that says low, with no look at links, is not a real forecast. Security side effects belong in the forecast. Approval can read the forecast and does not replace it.',
    },
    'Test results': {
        'body': 'Test results are the evidence that a change worked in a safe place, or that it failed and must not go forward. The safe place may be a lab, a sandbox, or a small pilot. A feeling is not evidence. A ticket note that only says the change looks fine is not evidence either. The record has to show what was tried, what passed, what failed, who ran the trial, and the raw output.\n\nA useful record also names the build that was tried. After a fix, the old attachment describes the old build, so the trial has to be run again. A pilot that looks nothing like production predicts little, even when every pilot machine stays up. The safe place has to resemble the systems that will receive the change, including the same sign-in path and a similar build. A pass on three lab machines that lack the clinic agent does not speak for the clinic. An honest failure is a valid result because that failure is what stops a bad push. Editing the crash out of the attachment leaves the readers with a lie.\n\nTicket 9226 carried a note that the new agent looked fine. The pilot logs said otherwise. Three pilot laptops crashed on build 4.2.9. Each log showed an agent fault and a process exit, and the service desk kept the three host names beside the note. The cheerful sentence and the logs could not both be the result. The logs were the result. The change had to stop.\n\nA defender reads the raw output before anyone promotes the change, and blocks promotion when the pilot failed. The tester name stays with the output. A second test of the fixed build is required before a second look at production. The defender does not rewrite the note to hide a crash, and does not treat a live demo on a production desk as a substitute for the earlier trial.\n\nStudents treat a forecast of harm as if it were this evidence, and they treat return steps the same way. The forecast is written before a trial and says what might break. The return steps say how to get back to the last known good state. The separating fact is the attached record from a safe place. A crash on three pilot laptops is a result that should stop the change. A smile in the note is not.',
        'r0': 'A lab, a sandbox, or a pilot has to produce a pass or a fail with raw output attached. Ticket 9226 said the new agent looked fine while three pilot laptops crashed on build 4.2.9. Each crash log showed an agent fault and a process exit, and the tester name was kept with those logs. A forecast of harm is written before any trial, so that forecast is not this evidence. Steps that restore the last known good state are not this evidence either. A pilot set that does not resemble production can pass and still predict little. An honest failure is valid evidence because it stops a bad promotion. After a fix, run the trial again, because the old attachment describes the old build.',
        'r1': 'Test results are evidence from a safe place. Show a pass or a fail, not a feeling. Raw logs belong with the result. The tester name belongs with the result. Three crashed pilot machines are a fail. A note that says looks fine can hide a crash. A forecast of harm is not a test result. Undo steps are not a test result. A pilot unlike the real systems proves little. Keep the failure if the failure is true. Test again after a fix, on the new build. Do not hide a crash to push the change.',
        'r2': 'A baker bakes one loaf on a side tray and writes down whether it rose, before promising the whole order for a wedding. The written note is the result, and a smile in the kitchen is not the note. If the loaf falls, the order does not go out to the hall. A guess about which guests might complain is a different page. A plan to throw the loaf away and use yesterday dough is a different page. Copying the word fine over a fallen loaf leaves the room with a lie.\n\nThe written trial of that one loaf is what test results are. The guess about guests and the throw-away plan are not the trial record.',
        'r4': 'Open the pilot logs on ticket 9226 and read the host names, not only the summary note. Write down build 4.2.9 and the tester name beside each fault. Write whether each laptop passed or crashed. Compare the pilot machines with the production image before you trust a pass. Separate these logs from the forecast of harm. Separate these logs from the return steps. Do not promote the agent yet. Do not edit a crash out of the attachment. Stop when the raw output is the record that decides.',
        'exam': 'Evidence from a lab, a sandbox, or a pilot is this record, and a failure is a real record. A forecast written before any trial is not this record. Steps that return to the last known good state are not this record. A note that says looks fine can hide crashed pilot laptops, and the logs win. A pilot that does not resemble production predicts little. Hiding a crash is not a pass. After a fix, the old attachment is the old build and has to be run again.',
    },
    'Backout plan': {
        'body': 'Backout plan means the prepared way to return to the last known good state, written before anyone needs it. The plan names the saved prior copy, the actions that put the copy back, the person who may decide to invoke it, and the deadline inside the window. A hope that someone will think of something is not a plan. A general habit of taking backups is not the plan for this change either.\n\nThe prior copy has to be the real last known good state, not an older file that missed an emergency edit last week. The window has to be long enough to perform the return, not only long enough to make the forward change. Where only part of the change can be restored, the plan says what comes back and what is left. A rehearsal belongs in the work when the system allows a rehearsal, because an untested return is still a story. Version history can hold the old copy. The history is not the decision, the actions, or the deadline.\n\nTicket 9337 added a firewall rule that blocked mail. Nobody had saved the previous rule set. The mail log showed denies on port 25 while the new rule was in place, and the only idea on the call was to keep editing the live rules until mail returned. There was no prior file to put back, no named decider, and no minute in the window set aside for a return. The change had a forward path and no way back.\n\nA defender saves the previous rule set before the first new rule is added, and writes the restore actions on the ticket in the same sitting. The decider is a named person. The minutes required for the return are counted inside the maintenance window, not borrowed from the next morning. The defender does not start the edit when the prior copy is missing. If the return would be partial, that limit is written in plain words before the window opens.\n\nStudents call a pile of old files a return path, and they call the harm forecast a return path as well. Version control keeps the history of a change, including a way to open an older revision. The separating fact is that a backout plan is how you return to the last known good state during this window: who decides, which copy, which actions, and by what time. A folder with no actions is only storage. A forecast of who might suffer is a different page, not the return.',
        'r0': 'Write the return to the last known good state before the edit, including the saved copy, the restore actions, the decider, and a deadline. Ticket 9337 put a new firewall rule in place, mail failed, and the previous rule set had not been saved. The mail log showed denies on port 25, and the call kept editing live rules with no file to restore. The window has to contain the minutes the return will take, not only the minutes the forward edit will take. Name the revision or the file that is the last known good state, and rehearse the return when the system allows it. A vague line that the team will think of something is not a plan. If only part of the change can be restored, write that limit before the window opens.',
        'r1': 'Write how to get back before you change anything. The goal is the last known good state. Save the previous copy before the edit. Name the person who may call the return. Leave enough time to actually return. A missing previous rule set means you cannot return. A forecast of harm is not the return path. A lab pass is not the return path. A promise to figure it out later is not a plan. Name the version you can restore. Count the return time inside the window.',
        'r2': 'A shipping clerk keeps yesterday labels in a box on the bench while trying a new label design. The box, and the choice to put the old labels back, are decided before the first new label is stuck on a crate. If the box was thrown away, there is no way back to the labels that scanned. A list of customers who might complain is a different paper. A trial sticker on one crate is a different paper. A promise to invent a new label if these fail is not the box.\n\nThe box and the choice to reopen it are the backout plan in this picture. They are how the clerk returns to the last known good labels.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9337 and look for the previous firewall rule set. Write down the file name or revision that is the last known good state. Write the actions that put that copy back, and the person who may decide. Write how many minutes the return needs, and check that those minutes fit the window. Do not add the new rule yet if the prior copy is missing. Do not treat a general backup habit as this plan. Stop when the return path is written before the edit.',
        'exam': 'The prepared return to the last known good state is this plan, and it is written before it is needed. A pile of old files is history, not the decision, the actions, or the deadline. A forecast of who might suffer is not this plan. A lab pass is not this plan. A promise to think of something during the outage is not this plan. The window has to be long enough to perform the return. A backup habit in general is not the plan for this change.',
    },
    'Maintenance window': {
        'body': 'Maintenance window is the agreed time when interruption is allowed. The agreement has a start and an end, chosen when the business can bear the stop, and written on the ticket before the work is queued. The word whenever is not a window. The range is a promise to the people who will feel the stop, not a private note for the engineer. A span that begins when an engineer happens to be free is not a window either.\n\nThe length of the window has to cover the risky work and the return to the last known good state. Doing the forward edit with no minutes left for a return means the agreement was too short. Jobs the window forbids stay forbidden for the whole span. The open clock is not permission to try every idea that fits in the hour. If the end time arrives and the work is not stable, the return path is used rather than sliding the outage into the next busy period.\n\nTicket 9402 shows what a missing agreement does. A reboot of a clinic server started at 10:00 on a weekday morning because the ticket said the window was whenever. Visiting hours had already begun at 08:00, and the front desk was full. The reboot belonged in a quiet range such as 01:00 to 03:00, which the clinic had used for earlier host work. Nothing in the ticket named that range. The interruption landed on patients because the time was never agreed.\n\nA defender writes the clock range on the ticket, confirms it with the people whose work will stop, and refuses a start outside that range. The defender checks that the range still fits the work plus the return after the plan is final. Forbidden jobs are read again before the first command, so a spare half hour is not treated as room for a new project. When the end of the range is close and the change is not healthy, the defender stops and returns to the last known good state instead of stretching the work into the morning.\n\nStudents swap this agreed time for the period users cannot do the work. A two-hour window can hold twenty minutes of that period, and that period can also run past the window. The separating fact is what the number measures. The window measures when interruption is allowed. The other number measures how long users cannot work. Writing whenever measures neither one.',
        'r0': 'Agree a start and an end for the time when interruption is allowed, and put that range on the ticket. Ticket 9402 started a clinic reboot at 10:00 on a weekday morning because the window text said whenever. Visiting hours had begun at 08:00, so the interruption hit a full front desk. A quiet range such as 01:00 to 03:00 is a real window, and whenever is not. The range has to fit the work and the return to the last known good state. The period users cannot work is a different number from this range. Jobs the window forbids stay forbidden even when the clock is open. If the end time hits and the change is not stable, return rather than extending into the busy morning.',
        'r1': 'A maintenance window is the agreed time for interruption. Do the risky work only in that time. Put a start and an end on the ticket. Whenever is not a real window. A clinic morning is a bad time for a surprise reboot. The window must fit the work and the return. Downtime is how long users cannot work. The window is when you may interrupt. Forbidden jobs stay forbidden during the window. Do not slide the work into the busy day. Stop if the end time arrives unfinished. Tell the affected people the clock range first.',
        'r2': 'A school repairs the bell only in the hour the principal set, after the last class, not in the middle of the morning because someone was free. The hour has a start and an end, and the repair stops when the hour ends. The length of time the halls are noisy is a different fact from the hour itself. Jobs the principal has forbidden, such as painting the gym, stay forbidden even while the hour is open. A note that says whenever would let the repair land on a class.\n\nThe agreed hour is the maintenance window in this picture. The noisy stretch is how long people cannot use the quiet hall, which is a different fact from the hour.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9402 and read the start time and the end time. Write the range down if the text only says whenever. Check that 10:00 on a clinic morning is outside visiting hours before you accept it. Write a quiet range, such as 01:00 to 03:00, if the business can bear that hour. Confirm the range with the people who will feel the stop. Do not start the reboot outside the range. Do not treat spare minutes as room for a new project. Stop when the agreed clock range is on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'The agreed start and end, the time when interruption is allowed, is this window. The period users cannot do the work is a different number and can be shorter or longer than the window. Whenever is not an agreed time. A two-hour window does not by itself state a two-hour stop for users. The range has to fit the work and the return to the last known good state. Forbidden jobs stay forbidden while the clock is open. A start at 10:00 during clinic visiting hours is a missed agreement.',
    },
    'Standard operating procedure': {
        'body': "Standard operating procedure is the step-by-step way the organization already agreed to do a repeated job. The steps are written, kept where the worker can read them at the moment of the work, and taught to new people before those people work alone. A personal habit is not a procedure, even when the habit has worked for one careful person. A policy is not a procedure either. The policy says what must be true. The procedure says the order of actions that make the repeated job come out the same way each time.\n\nDuring the job, the worker follows the current page. If the page is wrong, the page is corrected through a real change, because people will follow the old page until the writing changes. A quiet private edit, saved only on one laptop, leaves everyone else on the old steps. One approved change ticket is not the standing method. The ticket allows a single edit. The procedure is how that kind of edit is done every time the job comes around.\n\nProcedure 14 is the agreed way to add a firewall rule. The steps say to save the current rule set, add the one rule named in the ticket, and record the ticket number in the change note. A new hire on ticket 9418 ignored that page and invented a personal order, adding three rules from memory and saving nothing. Mail stopped. The live rule list showed the three extra rules, and the change note was empty. The hire was not short of access. The hire was short of the agreed steps.\n\nA defender opens the procedure beside the live work and compares each action to the written order. A skipped save, or an extra rule the page does not name, is a reason to stop. The defender does not invent a fourth private step in order to finish faster. If the page itself is stale, the defender sends the page to be updated and follows the current page until that update is approved. The updated page then becomes the method people are told to follow, so the written rules match the new practice.\n\nStudents call any ticket this kind of procedure, and they call a personal shortcut the same thing. The separating fact is repetition plus agreement. The organization already agreed to these steps for a job that will happen again. One change is a single approved edit. A habit that lives in one person's memory is neither the ticket nor the procedure. Updating the page is a later act. Following today's page is the duty while the job is in hand.",
        'r0': 'A repeated job follows the steps the organization has already agreed, in the written order, every time. Procedure 14 says to save the current firewall rule set, add the one approved rule, and record the ticket number. On ticket 9418 a new hire added three rules from memory and saved nothing, and mail stopped. The live rule list showed those three extra rules, and the change note was empty. A policy that says changes must be traced is not these steps. One change ticket approves a single edit and is not the standing method for every future edit. If the page is wrong, update the page through a change, because people follow the old page until it is replaced. During the job, compare each live action with the current page and stop when a step is skipped.',
        'r1': 'A repeated job needs one agreed set of steps. The organization already accepted those steps. Follow the written order, not a private habit. A new hire does not invent a new method. A policy says what must be true. The procedure says how the job is done. One change ticket is not the standing method. Save the current rules before you add a rule. Record the ticket number in the note. Stop if the live work skips a written step. Send a wrong page back to be updated. Do not quietly diverge from the page.',
        'r2': 'Every picker in a warehouse follows the same posted card when a bin must be recounted at night. The card is the method the warehouse already agreed to, and a private shortcut is not a second card. One special order with its own paper is a different sheet from the card used every night. A rule that says counts must be honest is a different sheet from the steps on the card. If the card is wrong, the warehouse replaces the card for everyone, and until that day the pickers still follow the card on the wall.\n\nThe posted card is the standard operating procedure in this picture. The special order is one change, and the honesty rule is a policy, not the steps.',
        'r4': 'Open procedure 14 beside the live firewall rules on ticket 9418. Write down whether the current rule set was saved before the new rules. Write down how many rules were added, and whether the change note has the ticket number. Compare each live action with the written order. Do not add another private step to finish faster. Do not replace the page on one laptop only. If the page is wrong, send it to be updated and follow the current page until then. Stop when the live work matches the agreed steps or has stopped.',
        'exam': "Agreed steps for a repeated job are this procedure. One change ticket is a single approved edit and is not the standing method. A personal habit in memory is not the procedure. A policy says what must be true, and the procedure says the order of actions. People follow the old page until the writing changes, so a private edit on one laptop is not an update. Skipping a written save, or adding rules the page does not name, breaks the procedure. Following today's page is the duty during the job.",
    },
    'Allow lists and deny lists': {
        'body': "An allow list states what may run or connect, and it refuses everything it does not name. A deny list states what is blocked, and it misses everything it does not name. The default is the whole difference. Unknown software dies on an allow list. Unknown software runs on a deny list. Teams that hear deny and think it sounds stricter have the comparison backward.\n\nOn a laptop, application control checks a publisher, a path, or a hash before a program starts. If the clinic allow list contains only approved publishers, a new installer from a mail attachment does not start, even though nobody has seen that file before. A deny list of known-bad hashes stops those hashes and lets the new file through. Mail gateways still use deny lists of known-bad senders because the universe of legitimate mail is too wide to name. That can be a reasonable choice for mail and a weak choice for programs. Say which list you mean. A block event can be reviewed later, but the block itself is the preventive act. The review is a second control.\n\nA staff member downloaded setup.exe from a vendor message onto a clinic laptop. AppLocker wrote event 8004, a block, because the publisher was not on the allow list. Ticket END-90 holds the event, the file name, and the user. Last year's malware hash list would not have contained setup.exe. The file was new. The allow list did not need a prior sighting. Turning the control off so the installer could run would have restored the weaker default. The right follow-up is a change that adds the publisher if the software is truly needed, or a refusal if it is not.\n\nDefenders keep the allow list complete enough for clinical work and short enough to review. Add software through a change with an owner, not through a hallway exception that never expires. Read the block log so a broken bedside application is fixed by an approved update rather than by a local disable. Where a deny list is the honest design, such as known-bad mail domains, record the feed you trust and how fresh it is. A stale deny list is a list of yesterday's problems. Name the default in the standard: unnamed programs must not run.\n\nStudents call every block an allow list. Blocking one named bad domain, while every other domain still resolves, is deny-list behavior. Students also describe a deny list as the tighter control because the word sounds negative. Ask what happens to a program the list has never named. If it is stopped, you are looking at an allow list. If it runs, you are looking at a deny list. That question is the separating fact, and event 8004 on an unseen installer is the allow-list answer.\n",
        'r0': 'An allow list permits named software or connections and refuses the unnamed rest. A deny list blocks named items and allows everything else. AppLocker event 8004 on END-90 blocked setup.exe because its publisher was absent from the clinic allow list. A hash deny list from last year would have missed that new file. Mail often uses a deny list of known-bad senders because legitimate mail cannot all be named. Application control should default the other way. Add a publisher through a change, not by disabling the control. A block is preventive, and reading the block log is a later review. The test is simple: an item the list has never seen should fail on an allow list and succeed on a deny list.\n',
        'r1': 'An allow list names what may run, and it stops the rest. A deny list names what is banned, and it misses the rest. Unknown software dies on an allow list. Unknown software runs on a deny list. The allow list is the tighter default. A block of one bad name is not automatically an allow list. Mail can reasonably ban known-bad senders. Laptops should not use that same loose default for programs. Add new software through a change. Do not turn the control off to make an installer work. Ask what happens to a file nobody has seen before.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen that accepts only the ingredients on this week's delivery sheet. A jar that is not on the sheet goes back, even if nobody has declared that brand poisonous. A forbidden-jar list works the other way: named bad jars are turned away, and a brand-new jar walks in. The delivery sheet is stricter because silence means no. The forbidden list is looser because silence means yes. A tasting note written after a bad meal is not the same as the sheet at the door. On the laptop, the publisher list is the delivery sheet. Event 8004 is the jar of setup.exe sent back because it was not on that sheet.\n",
        'r4': 'Open event 8004 on END-90 and record the file name, user, and publisher. Confirm setup.exe was blocked because it was unnamed, not because its hash was famous. Do not disable application control to let the installer run. If the software is required, open a change to add the publisher. Keep a mail deny list in a separate note from the laptop allow list. State the default in the ticket in one sentence. Stop when the note says what would happen to a file nobody has listed.\n',
        'exam': 'An allow list stops what it has never named. A deny list allows what it has never named. That default is the separating fact. One blocked bad domain is deny-list behavior if everything else still works. Disabling the control is not an update to the list. A block log review does not change which list you built. The unseen installer that cannot start is the allow-list stem.\n',
    },
    'Restricted activities': {
        'body': 'Restricted activities are jobs a change window forbids even though the clock is open. Schema edits are a typical example. Copies of production data are another. The list is written on the ticket next to the work that is actually allowed, and it is read again at the start of the window. Spare time does not add permission.\n\nAn allow list names what is permitted and everything else is refused. The work named in the ticket behaves that way for this window: those jobs may proceed, and a job that was never named is refused. A deny list names what is blocked and everything else is allowed. That second pattern is easy to confuse with the forbidden-job list, and the confusion is dangerous. Forbidding a schema edit does not mean every other unnamed idea is suddenly allowed. The open window is the time interruption is allowed. It is not a free pass for a new project that happens to fit in the hour.\n\nTicket 9440 was a patch window from 01:00 to 04:00. At 02:17 the database log recorded a schema change that the ticket did not list. The person on the change had time left and started a rewrite of production tables, then began a copy of production data while the users were away. The schema change and the data copy were both forbidden. The patch itself was the permitted work. The extra acts were not a clever use of spare minutes. They were jobs the window forbade.\n\nA defender stops the extra work as soon as it appears, records who started it, and leaves the schema and the production data as they were. The defender does not help the rewrite along because the window is still open. If the forbidden act already changed data, the return to the last known good state is the path back from that mistake, and the original patch does not continue on top of a schema nobody planned. The note on the ticket says what was forbidden and what was touched.\n\nStudents think any job that fits inside the maintenance window is authorized. The separating fact is that these forbidden jobs stay forbidden for the whole agreed time. A deny list blocks the named items and allows everything else, and a change window does not work that way. The permitted set is what the ticket names, which follows the allow-list pattern of naming what may proceed and refusing the rest. A network allow list or deny list is a standing control on programs or connections. These restricted jobs are what people must not do during this one change.',
        'r0': 'List the jobs the window forbids, such as schema edits and copies of production data, beside the jobs the ticket permits. Ticket 9440 was a patch from 01:00 to 04:00, and at 02:17 the database log showed a schema change the ticket did not name. A rewrite of production tables and a copy of production data were started because time remained. Those two acts stayed forbidden for the whole window. An allow list names what is permitted and everything else is refused. A deny list names what is blocked and everything else is allowed. Forbidding a schema edit does not allow every other unnamed job, so the deny-list pattern does not describe this window. Stop the extra work, and do not keep going only because the clock is still open.',
        'r1': 'Some jobs stay forbidden during the change window. Schema edits are a common forbidden job. Copies of production data are a common forbidden job. An open clock is not permission for a new idea. A database rewrite must be in the ticket. Stop work that the ticket does not name. An allow list names what is permitted. Everything else on an allow list is refused. A deny list names what is blocked. Everything else on a deny list is allowed. Forbidden jobs do not permit every other idea. Write the forbidden jobs on the ticket.',
        'r2': 'The night crew may repair the fridge during the closed hours, and those same hours do not let them repaint the dining room or copy the recipe book onto a home drive. Closed hours are when interruption is allowed. The paint job and the copy were never the repair. A list of banned spices, where every other spice is still fine, is a different kind of list. A guest list, where only named guests may enter and everyone else is turned away, is also different. Spare time after the fridge is cold again is not a reason to start painting.\n\nThe repaint and the home copy are the restricted activities, jobs the closed hours still forbid. A list that blocks only named items and allows every other item does not describe those closed hours.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9440 and read the jobs the window allows. Write down schema edits and production data copies if those jobs are forbidden. Read the database log around 02:17 and write down any schema change the ticket does not name. Do not continue a rewrite that was never in the ticket. Do not copy production data because the users are away. Do not treat minutes left in the window as new permission. If data already changed, follow the path back to the last known good state. Stop when only the permitted patch remains in scope.',
        'exam': 'An open maintenance window does not authorize every job that fits the clock. Schema edits and copies of production data stay forbidden when the window forbids them. A deny list names what is blocked and everything else is allowed, and that pattern is not this rule. Forbidding one job does not permit every unnamed idea. An allow list names what is permitted and everything else is refused. The ticket names the permitted work for this window. A standing network list of programs or connections is a different control from these forbidden jobs.',
    },
    'Downtime': {
        'body': 'Downtime is the period users cannot do the work, and that period belongs in the ticket before it happens. The number is about users, not about how long an engineer sits at a keyboard. A hopeful five minutes, written to make a ticket look easy, is still a false figure when the real stop is forty. The people who will feel the stop need the honest number in time to plan their own work.\n\nThe estimate includes the restart itself and the checks after it. A service that returns in two minutes can still leave users blocked while sign-in, queues, or a dependent system catch up. The maintenance window is a different measurement. The window is the agreed time when interruption is allowed. The user stop can sit inside that window and be shorter than the window. The user stop can also run past the window, which means the agreement was broken as well as the estimate. After the work, the actual span is compared with the figure on the ticket so the next estimate is less of a wish.\n\nTicket 9551 ran a reboot of an identity server. The desk planned for five minutes of lost user work. The reboot took forty minutes, and users could not sign in from minute 2 through minute 41. The service desk queue shows calls starting in that second minute, with the same complaint that sign-in failed. Nothing in the early ticket text warned the clinic of a forty-minute stop. The engineer was busy for those same forty minutes, but the figure that mattered is the lost time of the users, which should have been written before the reboot was approved.\n\nA defender writes the estimate before approval, warns the affected people, and refuses to shrink the number to protect the look of the ticket. The warning states a count of minutes rather than a vague promise of a short pause. If a service restart will drop one path while the host stays up, that shorter stop is still a period of lost user work and still goes on the ticket. Afterward the defender records the measured span next to the estimate. A large gap is a reason to revisit the forecast, not a reason to edit the past.\n\nStudents read a maintenance window and think the lost-work figure has already been stated. The separating fact is the question each number answers. The window answers when interruption is allowed. Downtime answers how long users cannot do the work. A line that says 02:00 to 04:00 does not say the lost time is two hours, and a line that says five minutes is wrong when the identity path takes forty. Both numbers belong on the ticket, and they are not substitutes.',
        'r0': 'Write, before the work starts, how long users will be unable to do their work. Ticket 9551 planned five minutes for an identity reboot that actually took forty minutes. Users could not sign in from minute 2 through minute 41, and the service desk queue filled with that complaint. The estimate has to include the restart and the checks that follow it, not only the first command. The maintenance window is when interruption is allowed, which is not the same number as this period. A two-hour window can contain twenty minutes of downtime, and downtime can also run past the window. A service restart can create a short stop on one path while the host stays up, and that short stop still belongs on the ticket. After the work, record the measured minutes next to the estimate.',
        'r1': 'Downtime is the time users cannot do the work. Put that time on the ticket before it happens. Say the number of minutes, not a hope. Forty minutes is not five minutes. Include the restart and the checks after it. Warn the people who will feel the stop. The agreed window is a different fact. A window can be longer than the downtime. Downtime can also run past the window. Measure the real span after the work. A short service stop is still downtime. Do not shrink the number to make the ticket easy.',
        'r2': 'A shop writes on the door, before the shutter comes down, how long customers will be unable to buy. The sign states the real length, not a hopeful minute, when the repair will take half an hour. The evening slot the owner booked for the repair is the time interruption is allowed. That slot can be longer than the time the counter is dark, and the dark time can also run past the slot. Staff who only tell customers it will be a moment have not written the length.\n\nThe dark time, the period customers cannot buy, is downtime. The booked slot is the agreed time for the repair, which is a different fact from that period.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9551 and read the minutes written before the reboot. Write down five as the plan and forty as the identity path. Check the desk queue for calls from minute 2 to minute 41. Include restart time and check time in the estimate. Warn the clinic with a count of minutes. Do not shrink the number to make the ticket look easy. Do not treat the window range as this same number. Stop when planned minutes and measured minutes are both on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'The period users cannot do the work is this figure, and it belongs on the ticket before it happens. The agreed time when interruption is allowed is a different figure. A window from 02:00 to 04:00 does not state the length of the user stop. Five planned minutes are wrong when the identity reboot takes forty. A short stop of one service, with the host still up, is still this figure. Shrinking the number to make the ticket look easy does not change the lost time. The measured span is compared with the estimate afterward.',
    },
    'Service restart': {
        'body': 'Service restart means one background service is stopped and then started, which can drop active sessions for that service only. The server itself stays up, and the other services on that server stay up with it. The act is smaller than a reboot of the whole machine, and it is still an interruption for anyone using the service that was restarted. Calling the restart harmless, only because the host never went down, misses the sessions that were cut.\n\nThe mechanism is narrow on purpose. The operator stops the one service, starts it again, and checks that the process is running and that the service answers. Sessions that were open on that service do not survive the stop. Sessions on other services are left alone. A reboot would take down every service and the host with them. An application restart would bounce one user program and throw away work that program had not saved. Those neighboring acts have a different reach, and the ticket should name which one is intended.\n\nTicket 9662 restarted the mail transfer service just after 01:10. The host stayed up. Port 22 still accepted logons. The mail log showed connection reset on port 25, and the queue paused until the service finished coming back. Open mail connections were dropped even though nobody ran a reboot of the machine. The operators who still had a sign-in to the host were fine. The mail already being sent was not.\n\nA defender checks who is using the service, warns those people, and restarts only the service named in the ticket. The defender reads the log for the reset and for a clean start, and confirms the service answers before calling the work done. The defender does not reboot the host in order to be sure when the plan was a single service. The defender also does not close a user program in the same motion unless the ticket says that program is part of the work. A dropped session is expected for this service, and it is still written down as lost time on that path.\n\nStudents mix this restart with an application restart and with a full reboot. The separating fact is what stays up and what is lost. Here the host stays up, other services stay up, and the loss is the active sessions of the one background service. An application restart bounces one program, and users of that program lose work they had not saved, while other programs stay up. A reboot takes the machine down. Seeing port 22 still answer while port 25 resets is the sign of a mail service restart, not of a host reboot.',
        'r0': 'Stopping and starting one background service can drop active sessions for that service while the host and the other services stay up. Ticket 9662 restarted the mail transfer service at 01:10. Port 22 still accepted logons, and the mail log showed connection reset on port 25. The queue paused until the service came back, so open mail sessions were lost even though the machine never had a reboot. The restart is smaller than a reboot and is still an interruption of that service. An application restart is different: one user program bounces and work that was not saved is lost. Warn the people using the service, restart only that service, and confirm the service answers before you leave. Write the dropped sessions down as lost time for that path.',
        'r1': 'A service restart stops and starts one background service. Active sessions for that service can drop. The server itself can stay up. Other services can stay up. The restarted service is still interrupted. A service restart is smaller than a full reboot. Mail connections can drop while the host stays up. Warn people who are using that service. Check that the service came back. Do not reboot the whole machine for one service. An application restart is a different act. Users can lose work they did not save.',
        'r2': "A building takes one elevator out of service and then brings that same car back. People in that car have to step out, while the other cars keep running and the building stays open. The stop is real for anyone who needed that car. Closing the whole building would be a larger act. Restarting one program on a desk is also a different act, because that is one person's work on a screen, not a shared car for the whole floor. Calling the stop nothing, only because the building never closed, ignores the people who had to step out.\n\nTaking that one car out and bringing it back is a service restart in this picture. The other cars are the other services, and they stay available while that one car drops its riders.",
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9662 and confirm the plan names the mail transfer service, not the whole host. Look at who has open sessions on that service and write those names down. Warn those people before the stop. After the start, read the mail log for a connection reset and for a clean return. Check that the host stayed up and that other services still answer. Do not reboot the machine to be sure. Do not close unrelated user programs in the same step. Stop when the one service is back and the dropped sessions are noted.',
        'exam': 'One background service stops and starts, active sessions of that service drop, and the host stays up. Other services stay up as well. That pattern is not an application restart, which bounces one program and loses work that was not saved. It is not a reboot, which takes the machine down. A mail path can reset while a host sign-in still answers, and that split is the sign of this restart. The restart is smaller than a reboot and still interrupts the one service. Calling it harmless because the host stayed up misses dropped sessions.',
    },
    'Application restart': {
        'body': "Application restart means one program is bounced, and users of that program lose whatever they had not saved. Other programs stay up, and the machine stays up with them. The act is easy to treat as tiny because the host never has a reboot and the other windows on the screen never close. The loss is limited to that program, and it is still real for the person who had a form open.\n\nThe operator warns people to save, picks a quiet moment, stops the one program, and starts it again. A check that the program is back and usable belongs in the same piece of work. Programs that were not named keep running. Background services keep running unless the ticket actually names one of them, which is a different line of work. A reboot is the wrong tool when only one application is at fault. A service restart is the wrong tool when the fault is the user program and the background service is healthy.\n\nTicket 9773 restarted the scheduling application at lunch. Three clerks lost the form they had not saved. Each screen returned to an empty form, and the entries that had been typed were gone. The mail program on the same machines stayed open, and the mail transfer service on the server kept running. The computers themselves did not restart. The harm was the three forms that had never been saved. Nobody had been told to save before the bounce.\n\nA defender tells the users to save, waits for that save, and only then bounces the one program. The defender confirms the program is usable before leaving the users, and writes down who lost work if the warning was missed. The defender does not reboot the computer to restart one application, and does not restart unrelated programs without a reason in the ticket. If the problem is a background service rather than this program, the defender uses the service plan instead of closing the forms on the clerks' desks.\n\nStudents hear the word restart and treat every restart as the same outage. The separating fact is which thing stops and what the user loses. An application restart stops one program and drops work that was not saved, while other programs and the host stay up. A service restart stops one background service and drops active sessions for that service only, while the host stays up. A reboot stops the machine. The empty scheduling form on three desks, with mail still open beside it, is this case.",
        'r0': 'Bouncing one program drops work users of that program had not saved, while other programs and the host stay up. Ticket 9773 restarted the scheduling application at lunch, and three clerks lost the form on the screen. Each screen came back empty, the mail program on those computers stayed open, and the mail service on the server kept running. The warning to save has to happen before the bounce, not after the forms are gone. A background service restart drops sessions of that service and does not, by itself, close a clerk form. A reboot of the computer is a larger act than this bounce. Confirm the program is usable again, and record who lost work if the save warning was missed. Do not close unrelated programs in the same motion.',
        'r1': 'An application restart bounces one program. Users of that program can lose open work. Work that was not saved is gone. Other programs on the machine stay up. The machine itself stays up. Warn people to save before the bounce. Three clerks can lose a form at lunch. A background service restart is a different act. Mail can keep running while one program restarts. Pick a quiet moment for the bounce. Confirm the program is back before you leave. Do not reboot the machine for one program.',
        'r2': "A clerk closes one scheduling book at the desk and opens it again. Pages that were not saved are gone, while the other books on the desk stay open and the room stays open. The clerk needed a warning to save before the book was closed. Taking a shared hallway bell out of service is a different act, because that bell serves the whole floor rather than one person's book. Closing the building would be larger still. A smile and a promise that the page is probably fine do not bring the lost lines back.\n\nClosing and opening that one book is an application restart in this picture. The lost pages are the work that was not saved, and the other books are the other programs that stay up.",
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9773 and confirm the plan names the scheduling program only. Tell the three clerks to save, and write down that the warning was given before lunch. Do not bounce the program until those forms are saved. After the start, look at the screens and write down any form that came back empty. Check that mail and other programs stayed open. Do not reboot the computers. Do not restart a background service unless the ticket names that service. Stop when the one program is usable and any lost forms are noted.',
        'exam': 'One program bounces, users of that program lose work they had not saved, and other programs stay up. The host stays up too. That pattern is not a service restart, which drops active sessions of one background service while the host stays up. It is not a reboot of the machine. A warning to save has to come before the bounce. Three empty scheduling forms at lunch, with mail still open, are this case. Treating every restart as the same outage mixes these acts.',
    },
    'Legacy applications': {
        'body': 'Legacy applications are old programs the business still needs, and they are often unable to meet a new control without a workaround. People skip the fact that the business still needs the old program. The program is old, and billing or another core job still runs through it, so deleting it this week does not protect the work. The gap is that a new requirement, such as a multifactor sign-in, does not fit the program. Pretending the new control is already in place leaves the gap open on the network.\n\nThe workable response is a fence, not a story. A jump host, a tight network segment, or a similar boundary keeps the old program off the open network. The exception is documented with the reason, the boundary, and a review date. Replacement can be a later plan with its own window. Until that plan lands, the fence is what makes the leftover risk visible and smaller. A dependency link, such as the database the program calls, is a separate fact. The program can have dependencies and still be legacy. The legacy fact is the control it cannot meet.\n\nTicket 9884 covers the billing application. The application speaks only an old sign-in method and cannot perform the multifactor check the rest of the clinic now requires. The application is reached through a jump host, and the firewall allows that billing path only from the jump host address. It is not exposed on the guest network. A reviewer who only reads the new sign-in policy would think billing had been made modern. The policy is true for new applications. The billing path still shows the old method, with the jump host as the workaround.\n\nA defender confirms the fence is still the only path, documents the exception in the ticket, and refuses a request to publish the application so the workaround can be dropped early. The defender does not mark the new control as met for this program. The review date is a real date on a calendar, not a wish. If a change would weaken the fence, that weakening is part of the harm forecast for the change, not a quiet extra step. The business keeps the program because billing still depends on it, and the fence stays until a replacement can take the control.\n\nStudents use the word legacy as a synonym for any old dependency, or as a polite word for deleting the program now. The separating fact is that the business still needs the program and the program cannot meet the new control. The honest move is a workaround that fences it, not a claim that the sign-in is now modern, and not a removal in the same week that stops billing. A dependency is another system this change relies on, or that relies on it. The billing application may depend on a database and still be legacy for a different reason, the control it cannot take.',
        'r0': 'An old program the business still needs, and that cannot meet a new control, stays in place behind a workaround rather than on the open network. Ticket 9884 is the billing application, which still uses an old sign-in method and cannot do the multifactor check. The path is allowed only from the jump host, and the guest network cannot reach the application. Deleting the application this week would stop billing, and claiming the new sign-in is present would be false. A dependency, such as the database the application calls, is a separate fact from the control the application cannot meet. Document the fence, the reason, and a review date. Do not publish the application in order to drop the workaround before a replacement exists. Read the firewall path, not only the new sign-in policy, when you judge the control.',
        'r1': 'A legacy application is an old program still needed. The business cannot drop it this week. The old program often cannot meet a new control. A workaround fences the program instead. A jump host can keep the program off the open network. Do not pretend the new control is in place. An old sign-in method is a common gap. Billing still has to run behind that fence. A dependency is a link to another system. Legacy means the new control does not fit. Document the exception and the reason. Do not expose the old program to everyone.',
        'r2': 'An old side door is still the way the deliveries come in, and the new lock does not fit that door. A guard stands at the door instead of a false claim that the new lock is on it. Taking the door away this week would stop the deliveries. The fact that the door also needs the alley light is a different fact from the lock that does not fit. Leaving the door open to the street, with a sign that says the new lock is installed, pretends the gap is closed.\n\nThe old door is the legacy application in this picture, still needed and unable to take the new control. The guard is the workaround that fences it, and the alley light is only something the door depends on.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9884 and write down the sign-in method the billing application still uses. Confirm the path is only from the jump host, and write the address that is allowed. Check that the guest network cannot reach the application. Write the review date for the exception. Do not publish the application to the open network. Do not mark the multifactor check as met for this program. Do not plan to delete the application this week while billing still depends on it. Stop when the fence, the reason, and the review date are on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'An old program the business still needs, and that cannot meet a new control, is this case. A dependency on another system is a different fact, even when the same program has both. Deleting the program this week stops the business and is not the workaround. Pretending the new sign-in is present, while the path still shows the old method, is false. The fence, such as a jump host, is the workaround, and it is documented with a reason and a review date. A new sign-in policy for other applications does not modernize this one.',
    },
    'Dependencies': {
        'body': 'Dependencies are the other systems a change relies on, or that rely on it. Both directions belong in the list before the change starts. Upstream means what this system needs in order to work. Downstream means what will break if this system stops. A drawing that shows only the box being changed, with no lines leaving it, is not a dependency list. The lines are the subject.\n\nEach link names the other system, the owner of that system, what fails, and how the failure shows up in a log. The harm forecast uses the links to say how bad the harm is, who feels it, and how long it lasts. The links themselves are not that forecast. A test in a lab may show one path working and still miss a downstream system that was never attached to the lab. The production tie is the dependency, whether or not the pilot included it.\n\nTicket 9901 treated the badge system as independent. The badge software was not in the change at all. The change was a reboot of the directory server. Every badge lookup asks that directory for a name, so the doors stopped when the directory stopped. The badge server log showed directory bind failed, then four hundred lookup failures in ten minutes. Nobody had changed a badge setting. The link ran from the badges to the directory, which means the directory reboot had a downstream victim the ticket never named.\n\nA defender writes both directions before approval, tests the dependent path, and tells the owners of the linked systems. The defender asks what else breaks if this one stops, and asks what this one needs in order to start again. A directory, an identity server, a file share, or a database behind an application that looks separate is the usual surprise. The defender does not call a system independent because the team that runs it is a different team. Different owners make the link more important to write down, not less.\n\nStudents list only the systems their change needs, and they call that list the harm forecast. The separating fact is direction plus purpose. Dependencies include systems that rely on the change as well as systems the change relies on. The harm forecast is the account of who is affected and how bad it is, built from those links. A badge log full of directory bind failures is the sign of a downstream link. The forecast should have named the door failure before the reboot, and the link is what makes that forecast possible.',
        'r0': 'Name the systems a change needs and the systems that need the change, before the window. Ticket 9901 ran a reboot of a directory server and treated the badge system as independent. Every badge lookup asks that directory for a name, so the doors stopped with the directory. The badge log showed directory bind failed and four hundred lookup failures in ten minutes. No badge setting had been edited. The harm forecast uses these links and is a different page from the links themselves. A lab that never included the badge path can pass and still miss this downstream tie. Tell the owners on both sides, and do not call a system independent only because another team runs it.',
        'r1': 'Dependencies are other systems tied to a change. Some of those systems are ones you rely on. Some of those systems rely on you. A badge reader can look independent at first. The badge reader may ask a directory for every name. If the directory stops, the badges stop. Write both directions before the change. The harm forecast is a different document. Ask what else breaks if this system stops. Name the owner of each linked system. Test the path that depends on you. Tell those owners before the window.',
        'r2': 'The dining room lights go out when the panel in the closet is off, and the panel itself goes dark when the feed from the street is off. Each of those links runs in a direction, and both directions matter before anyone opens the panel. A written guess about how bad the dark will be is a later page. The lights looked separate until the closet was named. A trial that never turns the closet panel off can leave the dining room looking fine and still teach nothing about the link.\n\nThose links, in both directions, are the dependencies in this picture. The written guess about the harm is a different page, built from the links and not the same thing as the links.',
        'r4': 'Open ticket 9901 and list what the directory reboot relies on. Write down the badge system if every lookup asks the directory for a name. Read the badge log for directory bind failed and note the count of lookup failures. Name the owner of the badge system and the owner of the directory. Do not call the badge system independent because a different team runs it. Do not reboot yet. Separate this list of links from the written forecast of harm. Stop when both directions are on the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Other systems a change relies on, and other systems that rely on it, are these links. Listing only what the change needs misses the downstream direction. The harm forecast says who is hurt and how bad that is, and it is built from the links rather than being the links. A lab that omits a production tie can pass and still miss the tie. Different teams do not make a system independent. Badge lookups that fail when the directory stops are a downstream link the ticket has to name before the reboot.',
    },
    'Updating diagrams': {
        'body': 'Updating diagrams means the picture of the network or the system matches what was just changed. The picture may be a wall drawing, a published file, or the sheet a new hire is told to trust. History can keep the old picture. The current picture is a different object, and it has to be true on the day of the change. A procedure can be updated in the same window and still leave the picture wrong. The procedure tells people the steps to follow. The diagram shows how the wires and networks actually sit.\n\nThe work is part of closing the change, not a favor saved for a later week. After the wires move, a subnet is added, or a host changes role, the diagram is edited and compared with the live configuration. The comparison looks for networks that exist in only one of the two places. A diagram stored as a new revision is still wrong if the content of that revision is the old drawing. Version control keeps that history. Making the diagram current means the newest picture matches the change. The date of the picture belongs on the ticket with the change number.\n\nTicket 9912 added a guest network, subnet 10.8.20.0/24 on vlan 20. The wall diagram in the clinic closet still showed one flat network, dated the previous year. A technician followed the wall picture, plugged a guest laptop into the clinic switch, and landed that laptop on the clinic network rather than the guest network. The live configuration had the new subnet. The picture people could see did not. The guest laptop was on the wrong network because the diagram was stale, not because the change had failed to create the subnet.\n\nA defender updates the diagram before the ticket is closed, writes the diagram date on the ticket, and checks the picture against the live networks. The wall copy is included, not only the file on a laptop. The defender does not treat a revised procedure as a substitute for the drawing, and does not treat a folder of old drawings as the update. If the picture and the wires disagree, the wires are inspected before the ticket is called done, in case the diagram edit itself is what is wrong. The same day is the rule because every hour of a stale picture is an hour of bad directions.\n\nStudents update a procedure and think the diagrams are therefore done, or they save the old drawing into version history and think that save is the update. The separating fact is what each artifact is for. A diagram shows the network or the system as it is now. A procedure tells the steps people should follow, and those written rules also have to match the new practice or people will follow the old page. Version control keeps each revision so an older picture can be recovered. None of those acts draws the current picture again. The guest network has to appear on the sheet people use today.',
        'r0': 'On the day of the change, make the picture of the network match the wires, including the copy posted on the wall. Ticket 9912 added guest subnet 10.8.20.0/24 on vlan 20, and the wall diagram still showed one flat network from the previous year. A technician followed that picture, plugged a guest laptop into the clinic switch, and placed the laptop on the clinic network. The live configuration already had the new subnet, so the failure was the stale picture. A revised procedure does not draw the picture again, and saving the old drawing as history does not either. Compare the current picture with the live networks before the ticket is closed. If they disagree, inspect the wires before you decide the drawing is the only fault.',
        'r1': 'The picture must match the change you just made. Update the drawing on the same day. A wall picture counts, not only a file. A new guest network must appear on the picture. One flat drawing is wrong after a second network. A stale picture sends people to the wrong wire. A new procedure is not the same as a new picture. Old drawings kept for history are not the update. Compare the picture to the live network. Do this before the ticket is closed. Write the date of the new picture on the ticket. Do not leave the wall for a later week.',
        'r2': 'A new room is built on a floor, and the plan posted by the stairs still shows a closet where the room now is. People following the posted plan walk to the wrong place on the same day as the change. A new instruction card that tells staff how to book the room is a different paper from the plan. Keeping last year plan in a folder is history, and it does not fix the sheet on the wall. Checking the card only, and never looking at the wall, leaves the wrong closet in place.\n\nMaking the posted plan match the new room is updating diagrams in this picture. The instruction card is a procedure, and the folder of old plans is history, not the current picture.',
        'r4': 'Open the wall diagram and the published file for ticket 9912. Write down whether guest subnet 10.8.20.0/24 and vlan 20 appear on both. Compare that picture with the live configuration. Write the date of the picture on the ticket. Do not close the ticket while the wall still shows one flat network. Do not treat a revised procedure as the picture. Do not treat a folder of old drawings as the update. If the picture and the wires disagree, inspect the wires before you blame the change. Stop when the current picture matches the networks.',
        'exam': 'The current picture of the network or the system has to match the change, including the copy people see on the wall. Updating a procedure changes the steps people follow and does not draw the picture. Saving an old drawing into history keeps a past revision and does not make the current sheet true. A stale flat drawing can place a guest laptop on the clinic network after the guest subnet already exists. The same day is when the picture is fixed. If the picture and the wires disagree, inspect both before closing the ticket.',
    },
    'Updating policies and procedures': {
        'body': 'A change is unfinished while the page people are told to follow still describes the old practice. Updating policies and procedures means bringing those written rules into line with what the organization does now. Otherwise the night shift will obey the page, and the new setting will look optional, wrong, or unknown.\n\nThe mechanism sits in the change path, not in a later documentation dream. The template should name every procedure, handbook page, and posted card the change touches. The owner of each page revises it in the same window, with a revision and a date. The old sentence and the new sentence both belong on the ticket so a reviewer can see the delta. Training is the moment you tell the people who actually do the work. Leaving both versions in the drawer only creates confusion, so the stale copy has to be removed or marked so it cannot be mistaken for current. Version history of a firewall file is useful and still is not this step. A diagram of cables is not this step either.\n\nRemote wipe became mandatory in POL-MDM-03 revision 6. Handbook page 14 still said the wipe was optional. Change CHG-2402 was marked complete after the console setting changed. Ticket DOC-19 reopened it. A night supervisor, reading page 14, told a colleague that wipe was optional and delayed the report of a lost phone by hours. The console and the handbook disagreed. People followed the handbook. The fix was a revised page 14, a note in the shift log, and removal of the loose photocopy that still said optional. The console setting had been correct the whole time. The control people could see was not.\n\nDefenders add the documentation task before the change window, not after someone files an incident. Quote both sentences. Name the page owner. Check the places staff actually look, including shared drives and laminated cards, not only the policy library. Do not close CHG-2402 while page 14 contradicts the setting. When the practice changes again, the revision number moves again. An undated edit on a wiki is hard to defend because nobody can show which sentence was current on the night of the loss.\n\nStudents update the network drawing and think the procedures are done. The drawing shows where cables go. It does not tell a supervisor whether wipe is mandatory. Students also point at version control of a script and stop. History of a config file does not replace the handbook. A golden laptop image is a build, not the instruction a person reads. The separating fact is the page the worker is told to obey. If that page still describes the old practice, as page 14 did, this update is not finished.\n',
        'r0': 'Updating policies and procedures makes the written rule match the new practice in the same change window. POL-MDM-03 revision 6 made remote wipe mandatory, while handbook page 14 still said optional. CHG-2402 was closed on the console change alone. DOC-19 reopened it after a supervisor delayed a lost-phone report by following page 14. Quote the old sentence and the new sentence. Remove stale photocopies. A network diagram update is a different task. Config version history is a different task. A golden image is a build, not the handbook. Evidence is the revised page the night shift can open, with a revision and a date.\n',
        'r1': 'Change the page people are told to follow. If the page is old, people will do the old thing. A new setting in a console is not the page. Write the new rule in the same window as the change. Quote the old line and the new line. Take the stale copy out of the drawer. A picture of the cables is not this page. A history of a config file is not this page. A standard laptop build is not this page. Do not close the change while the handbook disagrees.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse closing checklist clipped to the cage. Last month the door code changed, and the checklist still prints the old code, so the closer sets the wrong alarm and leaves proud of a finished list. The alarm panel itself has the new code. The failure is the paper the closer trusts. A fresh floor plan on the office wall does not fix the checklist. A saved copy of yesterday's panel config does not fix it either. The clinic handbook is that checklist. Page 14 still saying wipe is optional is the old door code, and DOC-19 is the morning you discover the closer followed it.\n",
        'r4': 'Compare POL-MDM-03 revision 6 with handbook page 14. Quote the mandatory sentence and the optional sentence on DOC-19. Revise page 14 before you close CHG-2402 again. Remove loose copies that still say optional. Tell the night supervisors in the shift log. Do not treat a network drawing update as this task. Stop when the page a worker would obey matches the console setting.\n',
        'exam': 'The page people follow has to match the new practice. A finished console change is not that page. A network diagram is not that page. Version history of a config is not that page. A standard image is not that page. The stem to recognize is a handbook that still says optional after the rule became mandatory. Close the change only after the stale sentence is gone.\n',
    },
    'Version control': {
        'body': 'Version control keeps the history of a change. Each revision of a config or a script is kept, along with who changed it and a way to return to an older revision. The newest text does not erase the text it replaced. A reader can open the previous revision, see the name of the author, and put that revision back. A file that is edited in place, with only the newest text left on disk, has no history, even if the person at the keyboard remembers the old lines.\n\nThe record for each revision needs an identifier, a time, and the person who made it. The return plan may name one of those identifiers as the last known good state. The plan is still a different document. The plan says how you return: who may decide, what actions to follow, and the time limit inside the window. What the older states were, and the fact that they remain available, is the history. A disaster backup can also hold a copy, and that copy is not the same as a sequence of revisions. A backup is for a disaster. The history is the ordered set of edits to this config or this script.\n\nTicket 9920 asked for a small edit to an infrastructure file. The file lived on a laptop. The person saved new text on top of the only copy. After the save, the text from yesterday was gone, the author field was blank, and there was no revision identifier to hand to the person who might need to return. The ticket mentioned a return in one vague line and did not point at any retained revision. When the new text broke a script the host runs at startup, nobody could show the last known good file. The history had never been kept.\n\nA defender requires the change to land as a new revision, with the name of the author and the time, and writes the prior revision identifier into the return steps. The defender does not accept an edit of the only copy as the same thing. Before the window, the defender opens the prior revision and checks that it is really the last known good state, not a draft that was not finished. After the change, the new revision identifier is what the ticket records. If the return is invoked, the restore uses that retained revision rather than the memory of one person.\n\nStudents call the history a return plan, and they call a single backup the same thing as this history. The separating fact is what is kept and what is decided. The history keeps each revision, the person who made it, and a way to return to an older one. A backout plan is how you return to the last known good state, written before you need it, with a decider and actions. A backup taken for a disaster is a safety copy, not the sequence of revisions. Without the history, the plan has nothing real to restore.',
        'r0': 'Keep each revision of a config or a script, with the author and a way to restore an older revision. That record is the history of the change. Ticket 9920 edited an infrastructure file on a laptop and wrote on top of the only copy. The text from yesterday was gone, the author field was blank, and no revision identifier remained. A script the host runs at startup then failed, and nobody could produce the last known good file. The return plan is how you get back to that state, with a decider and actions, and it is not the same thing as the history. A disaster backup is a safety copy, not the ordered set of revisions. Write the prior revision identifier into the return steps before the window, and refuse an edit of the only copy.',
        'r1': 'Version control keeps the history of a change. Each revision of a config or a script is kept. The name of the person who changed it is kept. An older revision can be brought back. A file edited with no history has no return. The copy from yesterday must still be readable. The return plan is a different document. The plan says how to get to the last good state. History makes that older state available. A single overwritten copy destroys the history. Write the revision identifier in the ticket. Do not edit the only copy in place.',
        'r2': 'Each time a notice on a wall is rewritten, the old sheet is kept in a folder with the author name and the date, and the old sheet can be hung again. The folder holds every sheet, not only the one nailed up today. Throwing the old sheet away and keeping only the new sheet leaves no way back. A separate note that says when to hang the old sheet again, and who may decide, is a different paper from the folder. A single copy locked in a drawer is different, because it is not the stack of dated sheets.\n\nThe folder of dated sheets is version control in this picture. The note that says when to hang the old sheet is how you return to the last known good notice, which is a different paper from the folder.',
        'r4': 'Open the infrastructure file and look for a previous revision. Write down the revision identifier, the author, and the time for the last known good copy. Write that identifier into the return steps. Open the prior revision and confirm it is the last known good state. Do not save new text on top of the only copy. Do not treat a disaster backup as the sequence of revisions. Do not treat the return steps as a substitute for keeping the old text. Stop when yesterday can still be opened and the author is named.',
        'exam': 'Each revision of a config or a script, with who changed it and a way back to an older one, is this history. The history is not the plan for how you return to the last known good state. That plan names the decider, the actions, and the time. A single backup for a disaster is a safety copy, not the ordered revisions. An edit that leaves only the newest text has no history to restore. The prior revision identifier belongs in the return steps. Memory of the old lines is not a retained revision.',
    },
}
